window.__cindyI18n = {"Bias Variance Tradeoff":"偏差–方差权衡","MLU-expl":"Cindy","Variance":"方差","Tradeoff":"权衡","Jared Wilber":"","& Brent Werness, January 2021":"","Prediction errors can be decomposed into two main subcomponents of interest: error from":"预测误差可以拆成两个我最关心的部分：来自",", and error from":"的误差，以及来自","variance":"方差",". The tradeoff between a model's ability to minimize bias and variance is foundational to training machine learning models, so it's worth taking the time to understand the concept.":"的误差。模型既要压低偏差、又要压低方差，这个权衡是训练时几乎躲不开的事，值得慢慢看清楚。","Using our wildest imagination, we can picture a dataset consisting of features X and labels Y, as on the left. Also imagine that we’d like to generalize this relationship to additional values of X - that we’d like to predict future values based on what we’ve already seen before.":"大胆想象一下：左边是一份数据，特征是 X，标签是 Y。我还想把这种关系推到还没见过的 X 上——也就是凭已经看到的，去猜以后的值。","With our imagination now undoubtedly spent, we can take a very simple approach to modeling the relationship between X and Y by just drawing a line to the general trend of the data.":"想象力大概也用完了。最简单的做法，就是顺着数据的大趋势画一条线，当作 X 和 Y 的关系。","A Simple Model":"一个简单模型","Our simple model isn’t the best at modeling the relationship - clearly there's information in the data that it's failing to capture.":"这条线并不太会讲这个关系——数据里明明还有信息，它没抓住。","We'll measure the performance of our model by looking at the":"我用","mean-squared error":"均方误差","of its output and the true values (displayed in the bottom barchart). Our model is close to some of the training points, but overall there's definitely room for improvement.":"来看它好不好：拿输出跟真实值比（见下面的柱状图）。有些训练点离得近，整体还是有得改。","The error on the training data is important for model tuning, but what we really care about is how it performs on data we haven't seen before, called test data. So let's check that out as well.":"训练误差对调模型很重要，但我真正在意的，是它在没见过的数据上怎样——也就是测试数据。一起看一眼。","Low Complexity & Underfitting":"低复杂度与欠拟合","Uh-oh, it looks like our earlier suspicions were correct - our model is garbage. The test error is even higher than the train error!":"糟了，刚才的担心没错——这个模型挺烂的。测试误差比训练误差还高！","In this case, we say that our model is":"这种时候，我会说模型在","underfitting":"欠拟合","the data: our model is so simple that it fails to adequately capture the relationships in the data. The high test error is a direct result of the lack of complexity of our model.":"：它太简单，抓不住数据里的关系。测试误差高，就是因为复杂度不够。","An underfit model is one that is too simple to accurately capture the relationships between its features X and label Y.":"欠拟合的模型太简单，没法准确抓住特征 X 和标签 Y 之间的关系。","A Complex Model":"一个复杂模型","Our previous model performed poorly because it was too simple. Let's try our luck with something more complex. In fact, let's get as complex as we can - let's train a model that predicts every point in our training data perfectly.":"上一个模型差，是因为太简单。我换个更复杂的试试。干脆复杂到头：训练一个能完美打中每一个训练点的模型。","Great! Now our training error is zero. As the old saying goes in Tennessee: Fool me once - shame on you. Fool me twice - er... you can't get fooled again ;).":"好，训练误差现在是零。套句老话：被骗一次怪别人，被骗两次……呃，不会再上当了 ;)。","High Complexity & Overfitting":"高复杂度与过拟合","Wait a second... Even though our training error from our model was effectively zero, the error on our test data is high. What gives?":"等一下……训练误差几乎是零，测试误差却很高。怎么回事？","Unsurprisingly, our model is too complicated. We say that it":"不出所料，模型太复杂了。我会说它","overfits":"过拟合","the data. Instead of learning the true trends underlying our dataset, it memorized noise and, as a result, the model is not generalizable to datasets beyond its training data.":"了数据。它没有学到底下真正的趋势，而是把噪声背下来了，所以换一批数据就不灵。","Overfitting refers to the case when a model is so specific to the data on which it was trained that it is no longer applicable to different datasets.":"过拟合指的是：模型对训练那份数据太专一，换一份就不适用了。","In situations where your training error is low but your test error is high, you've likely overfit your model.":"训练误差很低、测试误差却很高，多半就是过拟合了。","Test Error Decomposition":"测试误差怎么拆","Our test error can come as a result of both under- and over- fitting our data, but how do the two relate to each other?":"测试误差既可能来自欠拟合，也可能来自过拟合。这两者又怎么缠在一起？","In the general case,":"一般说来，","mean-squared error can be decomposed into three components: error due to bias, error to to variance, and error due to noise.":"均方误差可以拆成三项：偏差带来的误差、方差带来的误差，以及噪声带来的误差。","Or, mathematically:":"写成式子就是：","We can’t do much about the irreducible":"不可约的","term, but we can make use of the relationship between both bias and variance to obtain better predictions.":"这一项我拿它没办法，但偏差和方差之间的关系，还可以拿来把预测做得更好。","Bias represents the difference between the average prediction and the true value":"偏差衡量的是平均预测和真实值之间的差距","The term":"这个","is a tricky one. It refers to the average prediction after the model has been trained over several independent datasets. We can think of the bias as measuring a":"有点绕。它指的是：同一个模型在好几份独立数据上训练之后，预测的平均值。我会把偏差理解成一种","systematic":"系统性","error in prediction.":"的预测误差。","These different model realizations are shown in the top chart, while the error decomposition (for each point of data) is shown in the bottom chart.":"上面那张图是不同的模型实现，下面那张是每个数据点上的误差分解。","For underfit (low-complexity) models, the majority of our error comes from bias.":"欠拟合（低复杂度）的模型，误差大部分来自偏差。","As with bias, the notion of variance also relates to different realizations of our model. Specifically,":"和偏差一样，方差也跟模型的不同实现有关。具体来说，","variance measures how much, on average, predictions vary for a given data point":"方差衡量的是：对同一个数据点，预测平均会跳多少","As you can see in the bottom plot, predictions from overfit (high-complexity) models show a lot more error from variance than from bias. It’s easy to imagine that any unseen data points will be predicted with high error.":"看下面这张图：过拟合（高复杂度）模型的误差，更多来自方差而不是偏差。不难想象，没见过的点也会被猜得很偏。","Finding A Balance":"找一个平衡","To obtain our best results, we should work to find a happy medium between a model that is so basic it fails to learn meaningful patterns in our data, and one that is so complex it fails to generalize to unseen data .":"想要最好的结果，得找一个中间地带：别简单到学不到有用的模式，也别复杂到遇见新数据就不会了。","In other words, we don’t want an underfit model, but we don’t want an overfit model either. We want something in between - something with enough complexity to learn learn the generalizable patterns in our data.":"换句话说，我不要欠拟合，也不要过拟合。我要中间那个——复杂度刚好够学会那些能泛化的模式。","By trading some bias for variance (i.e. increasing the complexity of our model), and without going overboard, we can find a balanced model for our dataset":"用一点偏差去换方差（也就是把模型变复杂一点），只要别做过了，就能给这份数据找到一个更平衡的模型","Across Complexities":"换着复杂度看","We just showed, at different levels of complexity, a sample of model realizations alongside their corresponding prediction error decompositions.":"刚才在不同复杂度下，我各画了一些模型实现，以及对应的预测误差分解。","Let’s direct our focus to the error decompositions across model complexities.":"接下来把目光放到：误差分解怎样随复杂度变化。","For each level of complexity, we’ll aggregate the error decomposition across all data-points, and plot the aggregate errors at their level of complexity.":"每一个复杂度，我把所有数据点上的误差分解加总，再画到对应的复杂度上。","This aggregation applied to our balanced model (i.e. the middle level of complexity) is shown to the left.":"左边就是把这件事做在平衡模型（中间那档复杂度）上的结果。","The Bias Variance Trade-off":"偏差–方差权衡","Repeating this aggregation across our range of model complexities, we can see the relationship between bias and variance in prediction errors manifests itself as a U-shaped curve detailing the trade off between bias and variance.":"把这种加总沿着复杂度一路做下去，偏差和方差在预测误差里的关系，就会画出一条 U 形曲线，把两者的权衡摊开。","When a model is too simple (i.e. small values along the x-axis), it ignores useful information, and the error is composed mostly of that from bias.":"模型太简单（横轴靠左）时，它会丢掉有用信息，误差大多来自偏差。","When a model is too complex (i.e. large values along the x-axis), it memorizes non-general patterns, and the error is composed mostly of that from variance.":"模型太复杂（横轴靠右）时，它会把不能泛化的模式背下来，误差大多来自方差。","The ideal model aims to minimize both bias and variance. It lays in the sweet spot - not too simple, nor too complex. Achieving such a balance will yield the minimum error.":"理想的模型想把偏差和方差都压低。它落在那个甜区——不太简单，也不太复杂。踩住这个平衡，误差才会最低。","Many models have parameters that change the final learned models, called hyperparameters Let's look at how these hyperparameters may be used to control the the bias-variance tradeoff with two examples: LOESS Regression and K-Nearest Neighbors.":"很多模型有一些会改变最终学到什么的参数，叫做超参数。我用两个例子看看，怎样用它们拧偏差–方差权衡：LOESS 回归和 K 近邻。","LOESS Regression":"LOESS 回归","LOESS (LOcally Estimated Scatterplot Smoothing) regression is a nonparametric technique for fitting a smooth surface between an outcome and some predictor variables. The curve fitting at a given point is weighted by nearby data. This weighting is governed by a smoothing hyperparameter, which represents the proportion of neighboring data used to calculate each local fit.":"LOESS（局部估计散点平滑）是一种非参数方法，在结果变量和一些预测变量之间拟合光滑曲面。某一点上的拟合，会按附近数据加权。这个权重由平滑超参数管着，它表示每次局部拟合会用到多大比例的邻居。","Thus, the bias variance tradeoff for LOESS may be controlled for via the smoothness parameter. When the smoothness is small, the amount of data we consider is insufficient for an accurate fit, resulting in large variance. However, if we make the smoothness too high (i.e. over-smoothed), we trade local information for global, resulting in large bias.":"所以 LOESS 的偏差–方差权衡，可以用平滑参数来拧。平滑太小，用来拟合的数据不够，方差会很大。平滑太大（过度平滑），又会拿局部信息去换全局，偏差就会很大。","Below a LOESS curve is fit to two variables. Randomize the training data to observe the effect different model realizations have on variance, and control the smoothness to observe the tradeoff between under- and over-fitting (and thus, bias and variance).":"下面用两个变量拟合一条 LOESS 曲线。打乱训练数据，看看不同实现会怎样拉动方差；拧平滑参数，看看欠拟合和过拟合（也就是偏差和方差）怎么换。","Randomize Train Data":"打乱训练数据","K-Nearest Neighbors":"K 近邻","K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) classification is a simple technique for assigning class membership to a data point with some majority vote of its K-nearest neighbors. For example, when K = 1, the data point is simply assigned to the class of that single nearest neighbor. If K = 69, the data point is assigned to the majority class of its 69-nearest neighbors.":"K 近邻（KNN）分类很简单：看一个点的 K 个最近邻居，用多数表决给它贴类别。比如 K = 1，就直接跟那个最近邻居同一类。K = 69，就看 69 个邻居里哪一类占多数。","We can observe the bias variance tradeoff in KNN directly by playing with the hyperparameter K. When K is small, only a small number of neighbors are considered during the classification vote. The resulting islands and jagged boundaries are a result of high variance, as classifications are determined by very localized neighborhoods. For large values of K, we see very smoothed regions that deviate sharply from the true decision boundary - go too high and you’ll just obtain a majority vote. This is high bias. On the other hand, for medium values of K, we see smooth the regions that follow along the true decision boundary.":"拧超参数 K，就能直接看见 KNN 里的偏差–方差权衡。K 很小时，表决只看很少几个邻居，图上会出现小岛和锯齿边界——那是高方差，因为分类被非常局部的邻域决定。K 很大时，区域被抹得很平，会明显偏离真正的决策边界；再大下去就只剩全局多数票，这是高偏差。K 取中等时，区域会比较平滑，也更贴着真正的决策边界走。","Explore the trade-off for yourself below! The plot on the left shows the training data. The plot on the right shows decision regions based on the current value of K. Deeper colors reflect more confidence in the classification. Hover over a point to see its classification to the right, and the K-nearest neighbors used for consideration to the left.":"下面可以自己拧着玩。左边是训练数据，右边是当前 K 对应的决策区域。颜色越深，分类越有把握。把鼠标停在一个点上：右边会显示它的分类，左边会标出参与表决的 K 个邻居。","Randomize Data":"打乱数据","What About Double Descent?":"那双重下降呢？","You may have heard about the phenomena, known as Double Descent, wherein which the classic U-shaped bias variance curve (seen above and in textbooks everywhere) is followed by a second dip (shown below). Such a phenomena must nullify the bias variance tradeoff we just spent so long explaining, right?":"你也许听过双重下降：经典的 U 形偏差–方差曲线（上面有，课本里也到处是）后面，还会再凹下去一次（见下图）。那岂不是把我刚花这么久讲的权衡给推翻了？","Fret not, dear reader! As we will detail in our next series of articles, Double Descent actually supports the classical notion of the bias variance trade off. Stay tuned to learn more!":"先别慌。下一篇我会细写：双重下降其实是在给经典的偏差–方差权衡撑腰。接着看。","It's Finally Over":"终于写完了","Exhales deeply.":"长出一口气。","It's finally over. Thanks for reading! We hope that the article is insightful no matter where you are along your machine learning journey, and that you came away with a better undersatnding of the bias variance tradeoff.":"终于写完了。谢谢你看到这里。不管你学到哪一步，希望这篇能让偏差–方差权衡更清楚一点。","To make things compact, we skipped over some relevant topics (such as regularization), but stay-tuned for more MLU-Explain articles, where we plan to explain those, and other, topics related to machine learning.":"为了写得短一点，有些相关话题（比如正则化）我先略过。这些笔记后面还会写那些，以及其他机器学习相关的题目。","To learn more about machine learning, check out our":"今天先写到这里。想接着看可以回","self-paced courses":"画廊","youtube videos":"视频",", and the":"，也可以翻翻","Dive into Deep Learning":"《动手学深度学习》","textbook. If you have any comments/ideas/etc. related to MLU-Explain articles, feel free to":"。如果你对这些笔记有想法，欢迎","reach out directly":"直接跟我说",". The code is available":"。代码在","References + Open Source":"参考与开源","This article is a product of the following resources + the awesome people who made (& contributed to) them:":"下面这些材料和开源项目帮过我：","Reconciling modern machine learning practice and the bias-variance trade-off":"Reconciling modern machine learning practice and the bias-variance trade-off","(Mikhail Belkin; Daniel Hsu; Siyuan Ma; Soumik Mandal, 2019)":"","The Elements of Statistical Learning":"The Elements of Statistical Learning","(Trevor Hastie; Robert Tibshirani; Jerome Friedman, 2009).":"","(Aston Zhang and Zachary C. Lipton and Mu Li and Alexander J. Smola, 2020).":"","Understanding The Bias-Variance Tradeoff":"Understanding The Bias-Variance Tradeoff","(Scott Fortmann-Roe, 2012).":"","LOESS Curve Fitting":"LOESS 曲线拟合","(statsdirect.com).":"(statsdirect.com).","(Mike Bostock, Philippe Rivière)":"","Rough Notation":"Rough Notation","(Preet Shihn)":"","(Emily Eisenberg, Sophie Alpert)":"","Scrollama":"Scrollama","(Russel Goldenberg)":"","d3-regression":"d3-regression","(Harry Stevens)":"","This article is a product of the following resources + the awesome people who made (and contributed to) them:":"下面这些材料和开源工具，撑起了这篇笔记：","[1] A study of cross-validation and bootstrap for accuracy estimation and model selection":"[1] 交叉验证与自助法用于精度估计和模型选择的一项研究","(Ron Kohavi, 1995).":"","[2] Elements of Statistical Learning":"[2] 《统计学习要素》","(Friedman, Tibshirani, and Hastie, 2001).":"","[3] No Unbiased Estimator of the Variance of K-Fold Cross-Validation":"[3] K 折交叉验证的方差没有无偏估计","(Yves Grandvalet & Yoshua Bengio, 2004)":"","[4] Cross-Validation for Selecting a Model Selection Procedure":"[4] 用交叉验证来选「模型选择」这件事本身","(Yongli Zhang & Yuhong Yang, 2015).":"","[5] A Comparative Study of Ordinary Cross-Validation, v-Fold Cross-Validation and the Repeated Learning-Testing Methods":"[5] 普通交叉验证、v 折交叉验证与重复学习–测试法的比较","(Prabir Burman, 1989).":"","[6] Regression And Other Stories":"[6] 《回归与其他故事》","(Gelman, Hill and Vehtari 2020).":"","(Mike Bostock & Philippe Rivière)":"","(Emily Eisenberg & Sophie Alpert)":"","(Rich Harris)":"","Cross Validation":"交叉验证","Reduce, Reuse, Resample":"少浪费，多抽几次","Jasper Croome":"",", May 2022":"，2022 年 5 月","K-Fold Cross-Validation":"K 折交叉验证","Rather than worrying about which split of data to use for training versus validation, we'll use them all in turn. Our strategy will be to iteratively use different portions of our data to train and validate our model. The exact process is actually quite simple: We'll randomly split our dataset into":"与其纠结哪一段拿去训练、哪一段拿去验证，我干脆轮流用。办法是：轮着用不同的数据块来训练和验证。其实很简单：我会把数据随机切成","sets, or folds, of equal size. One fold will be reserved as the validation set (or \"hold-out set\") and the remaining":"份，每份一样大，叫一折。留一折当验证集（也叫「留出集」），剩下","folds will be used as the training set. The training set will fit our model's parameters, and the validation set will be used for evaluation. This process will be repeated on our data":"折拿去训练。训练集用来拟合参数，验证集用来打分。这件事会对数据重复","times, using a different fold for the validation set at each iteration. At the end of the procedure, we'll take the average of the validation sets' scores and use it as our model's estimated performance. This process is known as":"次，每次换一折当验证集。最后把各次验证分数取平均，当作对模型表现的估计。这个过程叫",", and requires re-fitting our model":"，还得把模型重训","times (once for each fold).":"次（每折一次）。","Below we show the process for":"下面是","folds of our data. Note that the test data always remains untouched (after all, it's the final hold-out set), but the distribution of training and validation sets differs at every fold:":"折时的过程。注意测试数据始终没动（它是最后才拆的信封），但每一折的训练 / 验证划分都不一样：","If you think this looks familiar, you're on the right track! It's just the validation set applied":"看着眼熟就对了！这不过是验证集办法重复了","times - only using different splits of training/validation data each time. But this simple extension to the validation approach is very effective at overcoming its shortcomings. The main benefit is that, because we train our model on multiple subsets of our data and take the average of the evaluation scores on those subsets, our evaluation estimates from K-Fold Cross-Validation will have lower variance than will the evaluation estimates from the validation set approach. Additionally, K-Fold Cross-Validation looks at more data during training. In the validation set approach, only one sample of the data is used for the training set, and it's possible that some information just wasn't included in that sample. With K-Fold Cross-Validation, the whole ensemble uses all of the data, so every data point will get included in the training of a model, and the evaluation of that model will then be factored into the final evaluation estimate. Finally, it's worth stating the obvious fact that test error estimates are more accurate when more data is used in the training set. Even for modest values of":"次——每次训练 / 验证的切法不同。可这么往验证集办法上推一小步，短板就被补上很多。最大的好处是：我在多份数据子集上训练，再把那些子集上的分数取平均，K 折交叉验证给出的评估，方差会比一次性验证集办法更小。另外，K 折交叉验证训练时看见的数据也更多。验证集办法里，训练集只是样本的一次抽取，有些信息可能刚好没被抽到。K 折交叉验证这边，整组模型用上了全部数据，每个点都会进入某个模型的训练，而那个模型的评估也会进到最后的总估计里。还有一件很浅显的事：训练集越大，测试误差的估计通常越准。即便 K 折交叉验证里的","in K-Fold Cross-Validation (e.g.":"不大（比如","), the training set comprises 80 percent of our data, so the approach typically doesn't overestimate the test error as much as the validation set approach could for small training set sizes.":"），训练集也能占到八成，所以通常不会像验证集办法在训练数据很少时那样，把测试误差估得偏高。","While K-Fold Cross-Validation has a lot of obvious benefits, it does come with a cost: the need to train and evaluate a model multiple times (once for each fold).":"K 折交叉验证好处很明显，可也有代价：模型要训练、评估很多次（每折一次）。","In machine learning we need to estimate the performance of a model before we put it into production. While we could just evaluate our model's performance on the same data that we used to fit its parameters, doing so will give us unreliable assessments of our model's ability to generalize to unseen data. Because obtaining new data may be difficult, we'd like to find a way to assess the generalization capabilities of a model without having to wait for new data. This article discusses one of the most common approaches for this task:":"机器学习里，模型还没上线，我就得先估它的表现。要是用拟合参数的同一份数据来打分，对「没见过的数据」会有多准，这个评估不可信。新数据不一定好拿，所以我想在不等新数据的情况下，先看看模型的泛化能力。这篇要写的，就是这件事最常见的办法之一：",". We'll first discuss the Validation Set approach we learned in the":"。我先从","Train, Test, and Validation Sets article":"三份数据，三种用途那篇",". Then we will describe how K-Fold Cross-Validation extends that approach, and discuss some concerns around selecting the values of":"里学过的验证集办法讲起。接着看 K 折交叉验证怎么把它推进一步，以及选","Concerns With Selecting K":"选 K 时的别扭","Up to this point, we've talked about K-Fold Cross-Validation in the general sense, and described the two most-extreme cases: LOOCV (":"到这里，我都在一般意义上讲 K 折交叉验证，也说了两个极端：留一交叉验证（",") and the Validation Set Approach (":"）和验证集办法（","). Given the different options for selecting":"）。面对这么多",", how do we select the best value? This question is actually more complex than it may seem! In answering it, let's revisit our old friend, the":"可以选，怎样才算最好？比听上去麻烦。回答之前，我先把老朋友请回来：","Bias-Variance Tradeoff":"偏差–方差权衡",", to understand how different values of k affect the quality of our evaluation estimates.":"，看不同的 k 会怎样影响评估质量。","Let's start first with how":"先看","affects bias. To qualify our discussion of bias, we'll look at the true test error of a model as a function of the size of the dataset it's trained on. That is, we refer to bias here as the shift in accuracy that a model gets when trained on less data,":"怎么影响偏差。为了把偏差说清楚，我把模型的真实测试误差，看成训练数据量的函数。这里的偏差，指的是少给数据训练时，准确率会发生的那截偏移，",", we use only a handful of the available data for model training, usually around 60 percent of the data. On the other hand,the training set for LOOCV uses almost the entire dataset, all":"时，训练只用到手头一部分，大概六成。另一头，留一法几乎用完全部数据，一共","observations, to train our model. As such, LOOCV gives us approximately unbiased estimates of our test error. In general, K-Fold Cross-Validation will use a training set with":"个点来训练。所以留一法估测试误差，偏差几乎没有。一般的 K 折交叉验证，训练集有","observations. Then, as our value of":"个点。于是","increases, the bias of our estimates should theoretically decrease, as larger training datasets should better approximate the test error. Under this framework, common choices of":"变大，偏差按理说会变小：训练数据越多，越接近真实测试误差。按这个说法，常见的",", will yield intermediate levels of bias, and low values should yield high. Therefore, if we want to reduce the contribution of bias to our evaluation estimates, we can increase":"会落在中间的偏差，很小的值则偏差偏高。所以如果想减少评估里偏差的贡献，可以把",", enlarging the data each model sees during training. It's important to not get confused about what we're discussing here: the bias here refers to our estimate of the test error from what we see during training/validation (not the bias of the test error itself, which is not affected by the size of our training data).":"加大，让每个模型训练时看见更多数据。这里别把概念搅在一起：我说的偏差，是用训练 / 验证时看到的东西去估测试误差时的偏差（不是测试误差本身的偏差，那个并不被训练数据量推动）。","The effect the value of":"再说","has on the variance of our estimates is where things get more complex. It's often argued that the larger our value of":"对估计方差的影响，才更绕。常有人说，越大的",", the higher the variance of our estimates, with LOOCV having very high variance.":"，估计的方差越高，留一法方差特别大",". The oft-cited intuition here is that the variance should be higher for larger":"。常见的直觉是：更大的",", since each model is trained on nearly identical data, meaning that every model should be essentially the same. However, this intuition isn't always correct and can depend heavily on the type of model and dataset used! For instance, [1] shows that for linear regression LOOCV actually has the smallest asymptotic bias and variance amongst all choices of":"会让方差更高，因为每个模型几乎在同一份数据上训练，模型彼此差不多。可这套直觉并不总对，还跟模型和数据长什么样很有关系！比如 [1] 指出，线性回归里，留一法在所有","in K-Fold Cross-Validation. One thing is certain: higher values of":"的选择里，其实渐近偏差和方差都最小。有一件事是确定的：更高的","require training more models, so often":"意味着要训更多模型，所以实践里","is the largest you might be able to do in practice.":"往往已经是我能做的上限。","K-Fold Cross-Validation can be an excellent tool to help improve estimates of test error rates compared to a simple validation set. It is widely used and an important technique to have in your machine learning toolkit.":"跟一次性验证集比，K 折交叉验证常常能把测试误差率估得更稳。用的人很多，机器学习工具箱里值得有这一件。","Thanks for reading! We hope that the article is insightful no matter where you are along your machine learning journey, and that you came away with a better understanding of K-Fold Cross-Validation in the context of machine learning.":"今天先写到这里。不管你学到哪一步，希望这篇对你有点用，也把机器学习里的 K 折交叉验证看明白了一点。","YouTube videos":"","textbook. If you have any comments or ideas related to":"。对","MLU-Explain articles":"这些笔记",", feel free to reach out":"，有想法，随时跟我说",". The code for this article is available":"。这篇的代码在这里","A special thanks goes out to Brent Werness and":"写的时候还麻烦人帮我看过，","Lucía Santamaría":"","for helping review the content of this article.":"","See For Yourself":"自己拖着看","To make K-Fold Cross-Validation more clear, let's see how it works directly. We'll assume that we’d like to use a simple linear regression model to predict some values of":"为了把 K 折交叉验证看清楚，我直接跑给你看。假设我想用一个简单的线性回归去预测","from some data":"，数据来自",". Drag the value of":"。拖下面的","below to set the number of folds used in K-Fold Cross-Validation. Observe that each value of":"来设定 K 折交叉验证用几折。每个","yields a new model trained and evaluated on different splits of the original dataset. (Note that the test data remains unchanged, as we use it only once, at the very end, to estimate our test Mean Square Error, or MSE):":"都会在不同的数据划分上训练、评估出一个新模型。（测试数据始终不变，我只在最后用一次，用来估测试均方误差，也就是 MSE）：","Estimated Test MSE:":"估计的测试 MSE：","Because each fold uses different data points for training and evaluating each model, each fold's model will be slightly different from the other. The final test MSE is evaluated by average performance on the test set across all of the folds in aggregate.":"每一折用来训练和评估的点都不一样，所以每一折的模型也会稍有不同。最后的测试 MSE，是所有折在测试集上的平均表现。","In exploring the fit in the models above, you may have observed something interesting! The lines of best fit (and estimated test MSE) vary more for lower values of":"看上面这些拟合，你也许会发现一件有意思的事：最佳拟合直线（还有估出来的测试 MSE）在较小的","than for higher values of":"上晃得更厉害，较大的",". This is a result of our old friend, the":"则稳一些。又是老朋友：","Bias-Variance tradeoff":"偏差–方差权衡",". Read on to learn how this tradeoff manifests in the context of K-Fold Cross-Validation.":"。接着看，这个权衡在 K 折交叉验证里是怎么冒出来的。","Leave-One-Out Cross Validation (LOOCV)":"留一交叉验证（LOOCV）","A special case of K-Fold Cross-Validation,":"K 折交叉验证有个特例，","Leave-One-Out Cross-Validation (LOOCV)":"留一交叉验证（LOOCV）",", occurs when we set":"：当我把","equal to":"设成等于",", the number of observations in our dataset. In Leave-One-Out Cross-Validation, our data is split into a training set containing all but one observations, and a validation set containing the remaining left-out observation. That is, the training set consists of":"，也就是数据点的个数。留一交叉验证里，训练集装下除一个以外的全部点，验证集只留被拿出去的那一个。也就是训练集有","data points, and the validation set consists of just one individual data point:":"个点，验证集只有单独一个点：","LOOCV carries all the same benefits mentioned previously, as well as some more that we'll discuss in the Bias-Variance tradeoff section below. While the large value of":"留一法带着前面说的那些好处，后面讲偏差–方差权衡时还有更多。虽然很大的","in LOOCV should minimize the variance in our estimate, it comes with a cost: the need to train a model":"按理说能把估计的方差压低，可代价是：模型要训","times! This is expensive both in the amount of time it takes and the compute resources it requires. For this reason, it's rare to see LOOCV employed in the wild unless there is a specific structure of the model that makes this computation efficient (like ridge regression). A more conservative value of":"次！时间也贵，算力也贵。所以除非模型结构让这个计算特别便宜（比如岭回归），现实里很少真用留一法。更常见的是保守一点的","is more commonly used instead (e.g.":"（比如","Our Previous Approach":"我上次用的办法","Train, Test, and Validation Splits article":"三份数据，三种用途那篇",", we described a standard technique for solving this problem:":"里，我写过一个常用解法：","The Validation Set Approach":"验证集办法",". Recall this involved randomly splitting our data into three mutually exclusive sets:":"。还记得吗：把数据随机切成互不重叠的三份：","The Training Set":"训练集","is used to learn the model parameters.":"用来学模型参数。","The Validation Set":"验证集","is used to select which model or set of hyperparameters you'd like to use.":"用来挑模型，或挑一组超参数。","The Test Set":"测试集","is used to evaluate how your model will perform on unseen data.":"用来看模型遇见没见过的数据会怎样。","} labels=":"} labels=","Validation Set Approach":"验证集办法","The Validation Set Approach is still widely used, especially when resource constraints prohibit alternatives that require resampling (like cross validation). But it's not perfect! The obvious issue is that our estimate of the test error can be highly variable depending on which particular observations are included in the training set and which are included in the validation set. That is, how do we know that the 30% we selected is the best way to split the data? What if we'd used a different split instead? Another issue is that this approach tends to overestimate the test error for models fit on our entire dataset. This is because more training data usually means better accuracy, but the validation set approach reserves a decent-sized chunk of data for validation and testing (and not training). If only we could come up with a way to use more of our data for training while also simultaneously evaluating the performance across all the variance in our dataset...":"验证集办法现在仍常用，尤其是算力不够、没法做需要反复抽样的替代方案（比如交叉验证）的时候。可它并不完美！最明显的问题是：测试误差的估计会很看运气——哪些点进了训练集、哪些进了验证集，数字就能差一截。也就是说，我怎么知道选中的那 30% 就是最好的切法？换一种切法呢？另一个问题是：它往往会把「用全部数据拟合」时的测试误差估高。训练数据越多通常越准，可验证集办法要把一大块留给验证和测试（而不是训练）。要是能把更多数据拿去训练，同时又在数据的各种波动里评估表现，就好了……","Decision Trees":"决策树","The unreasonable power of nested decision rules.":"一层套一层的判断规则，力量大得有点不合理。","Let's Build a Decision Tree":"先长一棵决策树","Let's pretend we're farmers with a new plot of land. Given only the Diameter and Height of a tree trunk, we must determine if it's an Apple, Cherry, or Oak tree. To do this, we'll use a Decision Tree.":"假装我是个刚拿到一块地的农夫。只知道树干的直径和高度，得判断它是苹果、樱桃还是橡树。这件事，我用决策树来做。","Start Splitting":"开始切","Almost every tree with a":"几乎所有满足","Diameter ≥ 0.45":"直径 ≥ 0.45","is an Oak tree! Thus, we can probably assume that any other trees we find in that region will also be one.":"的都是橡树！这块区域里再碰到的，我大概也会当成橡树。","This first":"这第一个","decision node":"决策节点","will act as our":"会当成我的","root node":"根节点",". We'll draw a vertical line at this Diameter and classify everything above it as Oak (our first":"。我会在这个直径处画一条竖线，线以上都标成橡树（第一片","leaf node":"叶节点","), and continue to partition our remaining data on the left.":"），左边剩下的继续切。","Split Some More":"再切一刀","We continue along, hoping to split our plot of land in the most favorable manner. We see that creating a new":"接着往下切，想把这块地分得更顺手。我发现再长出一个","Height ≤ 4.88":"高度 ≤ 4.88","leads to a nice section of Cherry trees, so we partition our data there.":"就会圈出一片不错的樱桃树，于是我在那儿切开。","Our Decision Tree updates accordingly, adding a new":"决策树也跟着改，新添一片","for Cherry.":"给樱桃。","And Some More":"还要切","After this second split we're left with an area containing many Apple and some Cherry trees. No problem: a vertical division can be drawn to separate the Apple trees a bit better.":"第二刀之后，剩下的区域里苹果很多、樱桃还有一些。没关系：再画一条竖线，苹果就能分得更开一点。","Once again, our Decision Tree updates accordingly.":"决策树再次跟着更新。","And Yet Some More":"再来一刀","The remaining region just needs a further horizontal division and boom - our job is done! We've obtained an optimal set of nested decisions.":"剩下那块再横着切一刀，好了——收工！我拿到了一套套得还不错的嵌套判断。","That said, some regions still enclose a few misclassified points. Should we continue splitting, partitioning into smaller sections?":"不过有些区域里仍夹着几个分错的点。要不要继续切，切成更小的格子？","Don't Go Too Deep!":"别切太深！","If we do, the resulting regions would start becoming increasingly complex, and our tree would become unreasonably deep. Such a Decision Tree would learn too much from the noise of the training examples and not enough generalizable rules.":"要是接着切，区域会越来越古怪，树也会深得不像话。这样的决策树会把训练样本里的噪声背得太熟，能带走的通用规则反而变少。","Does this ring familiar? It is the well known tradeoff that we have explored in our explainer on":"听着耳熟吗？就是我在","The Bias Variance Tradeoff":"偏差–方差权衡","! In this case, going too deep results in a tree that":"那篇里写过的拉扯。这里切太深，树就会","our data, so we'll stop here.":"这份数据，所以到此为止。","We're done! We can simply pass any new data point's":"收工！新来的点只要把它的","Diameter":"直径","values through the newly created Decision Tree to classify them as either an Apple, Cherry, or Oak tree!":"送进这棵刚长好的决策树，就能分成苹果、樱桃或橡树！","Decision Trees are":"决策树是","supervised":"监督","machine learning algorithms used for both regression and classification problems. They're popular for their ease of interpretation and large range of applications. Decision Trees consist of a series of":"学习算法，回归和分类都能做。好看懂，用得也宽。决策树是一串","decision nodes":"决策节点","on some dataset's features, and make predictions at":"问的是数据的某些特征，预测则落在","leaf nodes":"叶节点","Scroll on to learn more!":"往下滚，后面还有。","Decision Trees are widely used algorithms for":"决策树是很常用的","machine learning. They're popular for their ease of interpretation and large range of applications. They work for both regression and classification problems.":"学习算法。好看懂，用得也宽。回归和分类都能做。","A Decision Tree consists of a series of sequential decisions, or":"决策树是一串接连的判断，也就是",", on some data set's features. The resulting flow-like structure is navigated via conditional control statements, or":"，问的是数据的某些特征。整棵树像流程图，靠条件语句，也就是","rules, which split each decision node into two or more subnodes.":"规则，把每个决策节点劈成两个或更多子节点。","Leaf nodes":"叶节点",", also known as terminal nodes, represent prediction outputs for the model.":"，也叫终端节点，是模型给出预测的地方。","a Decision Tree from data means to figure out the order in which the decisions should be assembled from the root to the leaves. New data may then be passed from the top down until reaching a leaf node, representing a prediction for that data point.":"一棵决策树，就是搞清楚从根到叶这些判断该按什么顺序排。新数据从顶上往下走，落到叶节点，那就是这个点的预测。","Where To Partition?":"刀落在哪？","We just saw how a Decision Tree operates at a high-level: from the top down, it creates a series of sequential rules that split the data into well-separated regions for classification. But given the large number of potential options, how exactly does the algorithm determine where to partition the data? Before we learn how that works, we need to understand":"刚才从高处看了决策树怎么干活：从上往下，用一串规则把数据切成好分开的区域来分类。可候选那么多，算法到底怎么决定刀落在哪？在那之前，得先认识","Entropy measures the amount of information of some variable or event. We'll make use of it to identify regions consisting of a large number of similar (pure) or dissimilar (impure) elements.":"熵衡量某个变量或事件带了多少信息。我会用它来找出一大片相似（纯）或不相似（不纯）的区域。","Given a certain set of events that occur with probabilities":"给定一组事件，发生的概率是",", the total entropy":"，总熵","can be written as the negative sum of weighted probabilities:":"可以写成加权概率的负和：","The quantity has a number of interesting properties:":"这个量有几条有意思的性质：","Entropy Properties":"熵的几条性质","only if all but one of the":"只有在除了一个以外，所有","are zero, this one having the value of 1. Thus the entropy vanishes only when there is no uncertainty in the outcome, meaning that the sample is completely unsurprising.":"都是 0，剩下那个等于 1 时才成立。所以只有结果完全确定、样本毫不意外的时候，熵才会消失。","is maximum when all the":"在所有","are equal. This is the most uncertain, or 'impure', situation.":"都相等时最大。这是最不确定、最「不纯」的情况。","Any change towards the equalization of the probabilities":"概率只要往均等靠一点，","increases":"就会变大","The entropy can be used to quantify the":"熵可以用来度量一袋带标签的点有多","impurity":"不纯","of a collection of labeled data points: a node containing multiple classes is impure whereas a node including only one class is pure.":"：一个节点里混了好几个类就是不纯，只含一类才是纯。","Above, you can compute the entropy of a collection of labeled data points belonging to two classes, which is typical for":"上面可以算一袋带标签的点的熵，两类，也就是典型的","binary classification":"二分类","problems. Click on the":"问题。点下面的","buttons to modify the composition of the bubble.":"按钮，改一改泡泡里的组成。","Did you notice that pure samples have zero entropy whereas impure ones have larger entropy values? This is what entropy is doing for us: measuring how pure (or impure) a set of samples is. We'll use it in the algorithm to train Decision Trees by defining the Information Gain.":"有没有发现：纯的样本熵是 0，不纯的熵更大？熵在这儿干的就是这件事：量一量一袋样本有多纯（或不纯）。训练决策树时，我会用它来定义信息增益。","Information Gain":"信息增益","With the intuition gained with the above animation, we can now describe the logic to train Decision Trees. As the name implies, information gain measures an amount the information that we gain. It does so using entropy.":"有了上面动画的直觉，可以讲训练决策树的逻辑了。顾名思义，信息增益量的是我多拿到了多少信息。它靠熵来算。","The idea is to subtract from the entropy of our data before the split the entropy of each possible partition thereafter":"做法是：用分裂前数据的熵，减去分裂后每一种可能划分的熵",". We then select the split that yields the largest reduction in entropy, or equivalently, the largest increase in information.":"。然后选让熵降得最多的那一刀，也就是信息涨得最多的那一刀。","The core algorithm to calculate information gain is called":"算信息增益的核心算法叫",". It's a recursive procedure that starts from the root node of the tree and iterates top-down on all non-leaf branches in a greedy manner, calculating at each depth the difference in entropy:":"。它是一个递归过程：从树的根节点出发，对所有还不是叶子的枝贪婪地从上往下走，每一层都算一遍熵的差：","To be specific, the algorithm's steps are as follows:":"具体步骤如下：","ID3 Algorithm Steps":"ID3 算法步骤","Calculate the entropy associated to every feature of the data set.":"算一遍数据集里每个特征对应的熵。","Partition the data set into subsets using different features and cutoff values. For each, compute the information gain":"用不同的特征和切断值，把数据划成子集。对每一种划分，用上面的公式算信息增益","as the difference in entropy before and after the split using the formula above. For the total entropy of all children nodes after the split, use the weighted average taking into account":"，也就是分裂前后熵的差。分裂后所有子节点的总熵，用加权平均，权重看",", i.e. how many of the":"，也就是这","samples end up on each child branch.":"个样本分别落到了哪根子枝上。","Identify the partition that leads to the maximum information gain. Create a decision node on that feature and split value.":"找出信息增益最大的那次划分。在那个特征和分裂值上长出一个决策节点。","When no further splits can be done on a subset, create a leaf node and label it with the most common class of the data points within it if doing classification or with the average value if doing regression.":"某个子集没法再切时，长出一片叶子：分类就标成里面最多的那一类，回归就标成平均值。","Recurse on all subsets. Recursion stops if after a split all elements in a child node are of the same type. Additional stopping conditions may be imposed, such as requiring a minimum number of samples per leaf to continue splitting, or finishing when the trained tree has reached a given maximum depth.":"对所有子集递归。若一次分裂后某个子节点里全是同一类，递归就停。也可以另加停止条件，比如每片叶子最少要有几个样本才继续切，或者树到了规定的最大深度就收工。","Of course, reading the steps of an algorithm isn't always the most intuitive thing. To make things easier to understand, let's revisit how information gain was used to determine the first":"光读步骤往往不太直观。为了好懂一点，我回头看信息增益当初怎么定下第一","in our tree.":"在这棵树上。","Our First Split":"我的第一刀","Recall our first decision node split on":"还记得第一个决策节点切在","Diameter ≤ 0.45":"直径 ≤ 0.45",". How did we choose this condition? It was the result of":"。这个条件怎么来的？是","maximizing information gain":"把信息增益做到最大","Each of the possible splits of the data on its two features (":"数据就两个特征（",") and cutoff values yields a different value of the information gain.":"）和切断值，每一种切法都会得到不同的信息增益。","The line chart displays the different split values for the":"折线图展示的是","feature.":"这个特征上不同的分裂值。","Move the decision boundary yourself":"决策边界可以自己拖","to see how the data points in the top chart are assigned to the left or right children nodes accordingly. On the bottom you can see the corresponding entropy values of both children nodes as well as the total information gain.":"，看上图的数据点怎样被分到左、右子节点。下图能看到两个子节点各自的熵，以及总的信息增益。","The ID3 algorithm will select the split point with the largest information gain, shown as the peak of the black line in the bottom chart of":"ID3 会选信息增益最大的分裂点，也就是下图黑线的峰，对应","Diameter = 0.45":"直径 = 0.45","The visualization on the right allows to try different split values for the":"右边的图可以试","A Note On Information Measures":"关于信息度量的一句","An alternative to the entropy for the construction of Decision Trees is the":"长决策树时，熵之外还有一个选择，是","Gini impurity":"基尼不纯度",". This quantity is also a measure of information and can be seen as a variation of Shannon's entropy. Decision trees trained using entropy or Gini impurity are comparable, and only in a few cases do results differ considerably. In the case of imbalanced data sets, entropy might be more prudent. Yet Gini might train faster as it does not make use of logarithms.":"。它同样在度量信息，可以看成香农熵的一个变体。用熵或基尼不纯度训练出来的决策树大体相当，只有少数情况差得明显。类别不平衡时，熵可能更稳妥。不过基尼不用对数，训练有时更快。","Another Look At Our Decision Tree":"再看一眼这棵决策树","Let's recap what we've learned so far. First, we saw how a Decision Tree classifies data by repeatedly partitioning the feature space into regions according to some conditional series of rules. Second, we learned about entropy, a popular metric used to measure the purity (or lack thereof) of a given sample of data. Third, we learned how Decision Trees use entropy in information gain and the ID3 algorithm to determine the exact conditional series of rules to select. Taken together, the three sections detail the typical Decision Tree algorithm.":"先把到现在为止的记下。第一，决策树靠一串条件规则，反复把特征空间切成一块块区域来分类。第二，熵是常用的纯度（或不纯度）度量。第三，决策树把熵用进信息增益和 ID3，来决定到底选哪一串规则。三节合在一起，就是典型的决策树算法。","To reinforce concepts, let's look at our Decision Tree from a slightly different perspective.":"为了把概念再钉牢一点，我换一个角度看这棵决策树。","The tree below maps exactly to the tree we showed in":"下面这棵树，和上面","How to Build a Decision Tree":"怎样长一棵决策树","section above. However, instead of showing the partitioned feature space alongside our trees structure, let's look at the partitioned data points and their corresponding entropy at each node itself:":"那一节画的是同一棵。不过这次不把切好的特征空间和树结构并排，而是看每个节点上被切出来的点，以及对应的熵：","From the top down, our sample of data points to classify shrinks as it gets partitioned to different":"从上往下，要分类的样本会越切越少，分别落到不同的","decision":"决策","nodes. In this manner, we could trace the full path taken by a training data point if we so desired. Note also that not every leaf node is pure: as discussed previously (and in the next section), we don't want the structure of our Decision Trees to be too deep, as such a model likely won't generalize well to unseen data.":"节点上。愿意的话，我可以顺着一条训练样本把整条路径走完。也注意，不是每片叶子都纯：前面说过（下一节还会说），决策树别长太深，太深的模型遇见没见过的数据多半会慌。","The Problem of Pertubations":"扰动带来的麻烦","Without question, Decision Trees have a lot of things going for them. They're simple models that are easy to interpret. They're fast to train and require minimal data preprocessing. And they hand outliers with ease. Yet they suffer from a major limitation, and that is their instability compared with other predictors. They can be":"决策树的好处确实不少。模型简单，好解释。训练快，数据预处理也少。异常值也难不倒它。可它有一个大毛病：跟别的预测器比，它不够稳。它们会","extremely sensitive to small perturbations in the data":"对数据里一点点扰动极其敏感",": a minor change in the training examples can result in a drastic change in the structure of the Decision Tree.":"：训练样本稍稍改一点，整棵决策树的结构就可能大变。","Check for yourself how small random Gaussian perturbations on just 5% of the training examples create a set of completely different Decision Trees:":"可以自己看：只给 5% 的训练样本加一点点随机高斯扰动，就会长出完全不一样的一堆决策树：","Why Is This A Problem?":"这有什么不好？","In their vanilla form, Decision Trees are unstable.":"原样的决策树，本身就不稳。","If left unchecked, the ID3 algorithm to train Decision Trees will work endlessly to minimize entropy. It will continue splitting the data until all leaf nodes are completely pure - that is, consisting of only one class. Such a process may yield very deep and complex Decision Trees. In addition, we just saw that Decision Trees are subject to high variance when exposed to small perturbations of the training data.":"要是不加限制，训练决策树的 ID3 会没完没了地压熵。它会一直切，直到每片叶子都完全纯——也就是只含一类。这样可能长出又深又复杂的决策树。再加上刚才看到的：训练数据稍稍扰动，决策树就会有很高的方差。","Both issues are undesirable, as they lead to predictors that fail to clearly distinguish between persistent and random patterns in the data, a problem known as":"两件事都不想要，因为预测器会分不清数据里哪些是稳定规律、哪些只是随机噪声，这个问题叫","overfitting":"过拟合",". This is problematic because it means that our model won't perform well when exposed to new data.":"。麻烦在于，模型遇见新数据时就不会好。","There are ways to prevent excessive growth of Decision Trees by pruning them, for instance constraining their maximum depth, limiting the number of leaves that can be created, or setting a minimum size for the amount of items in each leaf and not allowing leaves with too few items in them.":"有办法拦住决策树长太疯，比如剪枝：限制最大深度、限制能长出多少叶子，或规定每片叶子最少要有几个样本，太少就不许再切。","As for the issue of high variance? Well, unfortunately it's an intrinsic characteristic when training a single Decision Tree.":"高方差呢？可惜，训练单棵决策树时，这几乎是它自带的脾气。","The Need to Go Beyond Decision Trees":"单靠一棵决策树不够","Perhaps ironically, one way to alleviate the instability induced by perturbations is to introduce an extra layer of randomness in the training process. In practice this can be achieved by creating":"说起来有点拧：要减轻扰动带来的不稳，训练时反而再加一层随机。实践里可以长出","collections of Decision Trees":"一丛决策树","trained on slightly different versions of the data set, the combined predictions of which do not suffer so heavily from high variance. This approach opens the door to one of the most successful Machine Learning algorithms thus far: random forests.":"，各自在稍有差别的数据版本上训练，合起来的预测就不会被高方差伤得那么重。这条路通向机器学习里最成功的算法之一：随机森林。","Stay tuned for our future article!":"下一篇我再写。","Thanks for reading! We hope that the article is insightful no matter where you are along your Machine Learning journey, and that you came away with a better understanding of the Decision Tree algorithm.":"今天先写到这里。不管你学到哪一步，希望这篇能让你把决策树算法看得更清楚一点。","To make things compact, we skipped over some relevant topics, such as using Decision Trees for regression, end-cut preference in tree models, and other tree-specific hyperparameters. Check out the resources listed below to learn more about those topics.":"为了写得短，回归树上的用法、端切偏好、以及其他树专属的超参数，我都先略过了。想看这些，可以翻下面列出的材料。","To learn more about Machine Learning, check out our":"想接着看可以回",", feel free to reach out directly to":"有想法，随时跟我说","A special thanks goes out to":"谢谢","Brent Werness":"","for valuable contributions to this article.":"给这篇添过很多东西。","A Mathematical Theory Of Communication":"通信的数学理论","(Claude E. Shannon, 1948).":"","Induction of decision trees":"决策树的归纳","(John Ross Quinlan, 1986).":"","A Study on End-Cut Preference in Least Squares Regression Trees":"最小二乘回归树中的端切偏好","(Luis Torgo, 2001).":"","The Origins Of The Gini Index: Extracts From Variabilità e Mutabilità (Corrado Gini, 1912)":"基尼指数的起源：摘自《变异性与可变性》（1912）","(Lidia Ceriani & Paolo Verne, 2012).":"","d3-annotation":"d3-annotation","(Susie Lu)":"","Double Descent":"双重下降","Part 1: A Visual Introduction":"上篇：先看图","& Brent Werness, December 2021":"","Note - this is part 1 of a two article series on":"说明：这是关于",". Part 2 is available":"的两篇里的上篇。下篇在","previous discussion of the bias-variance tradeoff":"前面写偏差–方差权衡的那篇",", we ended with a note about one of modern machine learning’s more surprising phenomena:":"的末尾，我留了一句现代机器学习里更让人意外的现象：","double descent":"双重下降",". Double descent is interesting because it appears to stand counter to our classical understanding of the bias-variance tradeoff. Namely, while we expect the best model performance to be obtained via some balance between bias (underfitting) and variance (overfitting), we instead observe strong test performance from very overfit, complex models. As a result, many practitioners and researchers are left questioning the relevance of the traditional bias-variance tradeoff in modern machine learning.":"。双重下降有意思，是因为它看起来跟经典的偏差–方差权衡对着干。按说最好的表现应该来自偏差（欠拟合）和方差（过拟合）之间的某个平衡，可实际看到的是：非常过拟合、非常复杂的模型，测试表现反而很好。于是很多人开始怀疑，传统权衡在现代机器学习里还算不算数。","Here, we'll first introduce the phenomenon for a general example and then offer a soft explanation for why it's occurring. (In a follow up article, we'll describe the phenomena in more low level, mathematical detail).":"这里我先用一个通用例子把现象摆出来，再给一个比较软的解释。（下一篇会把数学细节写低一层。）","At the end of it all, we conclude that the double descent phenomenon actually reinforces the importance of the bias-variance tradeoff.":"写到最后，我的结论是：双重下降其实是在给偏差–方差权衡加重量，而不是把它掀翻。","Plotting the training and testing error against some measure of model complexity (say, training time) for a typical case may look like in this figure, with both following the same decreasing direction and the test error hovering slightly above the train error.":"把训练误差和测试误差画在某种复杂度（比如训练时间）上，典型情况会像这张图：两条都往下走，测试误差略悬在训练误差上面一点。","Under the classical bias-variance tradeoff, as we move further right along the x axis (i.e. increasing the complexity of our model), we overfit and expect the test error to skyrocket further and further upwards even as the train error continues decreasing.":"按经典的偏差–方差权衡，沿着横轴再往右（模型越来越复杂），就会过拟合：训练误差继续降，测试误差却该一路飙上去。","However, what we observe is quite different. Indeed the error does shoot up, but it does so before descending back down to a new minimum. In other words, even though our model is extremely overfitted, it has achieved its best performance, and it has done so during this second descent (hence the name,":"可实际看到的不太一样。误差确实会冲上去，但冲完还会再降到一个新的最低点。也就是说，模型已经极度过拟合了，却在这第二次下降里拿到了最好的表现（所以才叫","We call the under-parameterized region to the left of the second descent the":"第二次下降左边那块欠参数区，我叫它","classical regime":"经典区",", and the point of peak error the":"，误差最高的那个点叫","interpolation threshold":"插值阈值",". In the classical regime, the bias-variance tradeoff behaves as expected, with the test error drawing out the familiar U-shape.":"。在经典区里，偏差–方差权衡还是老样子，测试误差会画出那条熟悉的 U 形。","To the right of the interpolation threshold, the behavior changes. We call this over-parameterized region the":"过了插值阈值，行为就变了。这块过参数区，我叫它","interpolation regime":"插值区",". In this regime, the model perfectly memorizes, or interpolates, the training data. That is, every model passes exactly through the given training data, thus the only thing that changes is how the model connects the dots between these data points.":"。在这里，模型会完美记住、也就是插值训练数据。每个模型都精确穿过那些训练点，变的只是点与点之间它怎么连。","What's Going On?":"这是怎么回事？","To better understand this phenomenon, let's explore it together! Quick note - in this article, we'll keep things fairly high-level. However, if you're interested in more mathematical, lower-level details,":"想把它看清楚，下面一起拖着玩。先说一声：这篇我会写得比较上层。若你想看更数学、更底层的细节，","check out our sibling article on double descent":"可以看双重下降的下一篇","Let's begin with a simple problem. We will train on data sampled from a cubic curve that has been occasionally corrupted with noise. We will generate a tiny training set and a larger test set so that we can rapidly explore what is going on, while still trusting the values for the test error, which is obtained from predictions on the test set. To maximize the stability of training, we will not employ a full neural network, but rather pick random non-linear features and then train a linear model on top.":"先从一个简单问题开始。训练数据来自一条三次曲线，偶尔被噪声弄脏。我会做一份很小的训练集和一份更大的测试集，这样能很快摸清楚发生了什么，测试误差也仍然可信——它来自测试集上的预测。为了让训练尽量稳，我不用完整的神经网络，而是随机抽一些非线性特征，再在上面训一个线性模型。","Below we show our data, as well as each model's associated mean absolute error (MAE).":"下面是数据，以及每个模型对应的平均绝对误差（MAE）。","To begin, we plot a simple model. Recall from":"先画一个简单模型。回想","our previous discussion on the bias-variance tradeoff":"前面那篇偏差–方差权衡","that basic models cannot capture complex patterns in the data and are thus underfitted, providing poor performance for the task at hand.":"：太朴素的模型抓不住数据里的复杂模式，于是欠拟合，手头这件事就会做得很差。","Next, we plot a model that's not too simple, nor too complex. It is in this complexity region where, traditionally, we expect to find the best performing model. This is reflected in the low error ( ≤ 0.25) in the bottom chart.":"再画一个不太简单也不太复杂的。按传统看法，最好的模型就该落在这档复杂度。底下图里误差很低（≤ 0.25），也符合这个预期。","Now, let's plot a complex model, one where our number of features is equal to the number of dimensions. This situation, in which the model passes through each and every point in the training set, is our interpolation threshold. At this stage we're overfitting, which leads to the high test error shown in the plot. Typically, we would stop increasing the complexity here and revert to a more simple model that achieves a good tradeoff between bias and variance.":"现在画一个复杂模型：特征数刚好等于维数。模型穿过训练集里每一个点，这就是插值阈值。这一步已经过拟合了，所以图上的测试误差很高。按老习惯，这里就该停止加复杂度，退回一个偏差和方差更平衡的简单模型。","The existence of the double descent phenomenon means that this picture is incomplete. We stopped making more and more intricate models when we reached a certain complexity level. But what happens if we go further, beyond the interpolation threshold? Let's look at a single example with 256 random features and what happens with the test error curve as we extend to that situation.":"双重下降的存在，说明这张图还不完整。复杂度到某一档我就停手了。可要是继续走，越过插值阈值呢？看一个 256 个随机特征的例子，以及把测试误差曲线延伸到那里会怎样。","The test MAE is even lower for these large models! The traditional U-shape is sometimes telling only part of the story. Past the traditional U-shaped region is the interpolation regime. The idea is that every model past that spike in error is complex enough to pass through every single training data point, thus all models are interpolating the training set. More complicated models can achieve smoother interpolations. If the conditions are right (as they are in this experiment) these enormous interpolating models can perform far better than traditional well-fit models.":"这些大模型的测试 MAE 甚至更低！传统 U 形有时只讲了故事的前半段。U 形再往右，就是插值区。误差尖峰之后的每一个模型，都复杂到能穿过每一个训练点，所以都在插值训练集。更复杂的模型可以把插值做得更平滑。条件合适时（这个实验就是），这些巨大的插值模型可以比传统「拟合得刚好」的模型好得多。","Try for yourself! Toggle the slider to modify the number of non-linear features used to build the models.":"自己试试：拖滑块，改用来建模的非线性特征个数。","Minding The Gap":"留意缝隙","It's important to pay attention to the gap":"要留意","between the points":"点与点之间的缝隙","once we've entered our interpolation region (K > 36). In the image to the left, we show, for a small portion of the data above, how the interpolation varies across 40 to 500 features.":"——一旦进了插值区（K > 36）。左边这张图，截了上面数据的一小段，看插值怎样从 40 个特征变到 500 个。","Once we're at the interpolation threshold, every model from that complexity level onwards passes through each training data point.":"到了插值阈值，从那一档复杂度往后，每个模型都会穿过每一个训练点。","The only thing that changes is how the model connects the in-between points":"变的只有：点与点之间它怎么连",". As the models become more and more complex, these connections can become smoother, and the resulting prediction may fit your test data better. This is why models in the interpolation region can perform so well.":"。模型越来越复杂，这些连线可以更平滑，对测试数据的预测也可能更好。插值区的模型之所以能表现这么好，原因就在这里。","Final Takeaways":"我记下的几句","The key takeaway here is that double descent is a real phenomenon, although its existence does not nullify the bias-variance tradeoff. It is believed that double descent contributes to explain why deep neural networks perform so well at many tasks. By building models with many more parameters than data points, deep neural networks are often operating in this interpolating regime. Traditional intuition from the bias-variance tradeoff would discourage such approach, indicating that simpler models with fewer parameters should be more performant. However, this has been contradicted with experiments. Double descent provides an indication that even though models that pass through every training data point are indeed overfitted, the structure of the resulting network forces the interpolation to be smooth and results in superior generalization to unseen data.":"最想记下的是：双重下降是真实现象，但它并没有把偏差–方差权衡作废。人们觉得，它有助于解释深度网络为什么在很多任务上这么好。参数远多于数据点时，深度网络常常就工作在这个插值区。按偏差–方差的老直觉，这种做法不该提倡，更简单、参数更少的模型才该更好。可实验并不买账。双重下降给出一个提示：穿过每一个训练点的模型确实过拟合了，但网络自己的结构会把插值逼得比较平滑，于是对没见过的数据反而泛化得更好。","Thanks for reading! If you've made it this far, consider viewing":"能看到这里谢谢你。想把双重下降再讲细一点，可以看","our follow-up article":"下一篇","explaining double descent in more detail. To learn more about machine learning, check out our":"。想接着学，也可以回","textbook. If you have any comments or ideas related to MLU-Explain articles, feel free to":"。如果你对这些笔记有想法，欢迎","References & Open Source":"参考与开源","Deep Double Descent: Where Bigger Models And More Data Hurt":"Deep Double Descent: Where Bigger Models And More Data Hurt","(Preetum et al., 2019).":"","More Data Can Hurt for Linear Regression: Sample-wise Double Descent":"More Data Can Hurt for Linear Regression: Sample-wise Double Descent","(Preetum Nakkiran, 2019).":"","Double Descent 2":"双重下降：数学","Part 2: A Mathematical Explanation":"下篇：把数学写清楚","Brent Werness &":"",", December 2021":"","Note - this is part 2 of a two article series on":"说明：这是关于",". Part 1 is available":"的两篇里的下篇。上篇在","A Sketch of the Mathematics":"数学先画个草图","previous discussion of the double descent phenomenon":"前面写双重下降的那篇",", we have made use of a piecewise linear model that we fit to our data. This may seem a somewhat strange choice, but we made it for a simple reason: you can rigorously prove that it will show double descent for essentially any dataset! This does not seem to be in the literature anywhere, so we've taken it upon ourselves to at least sketch the argument to provide some mathematical justification for why double descent occurs.":"里，我用了一个分段线性模型去拟合数据。这选择看起来有点怪，理由其实很简单：几乎对任何数据集，都可以严格证明它会出现双重下降！文献里好像还没人把这件事写清楚，所以我自己来画个论证草图，给双重下降一点数学上的着落。","This article is made of three parts. First, we provide a very short discussion of the behavior of the model with a small number of segments—the classical regime. Second, we will explain why the model must perform poorly at the interpolation threshold. Third, we will identify what happens in the limit of infinitely many linear pieces.":"这篇分三截。第一，模型只有很少几段时怎么表现——也就是经典区，我只写很短。第二，为什么到了插值阈值它一定差。第三，线性段多到无穷时会发生什么。","Throughout this discussion, we will see that the behavior boils down to":"整篇看下来，行为其实就落在","two core principles:":"两条核心原则上：","At the interpolation threshold, there can often be a":"到了插值阈值，常常只有","choice of model that works, and there is no reason to believe that said model will be good.":"一种能用的模型，而且没理由相信它会好。","In the limit of infinitely large models, there will be a vast number of interpolating models, and we can pick the best amongst them.":"模型大到无穷时，插值模型会多到可以挑，我就能从里面选最好的。","Throughout this discussion, we will use a simple example dataset to demonstrate our points. This is the collection of points:":"下面用一份很简单的例子数据把话说清楚。就是这些点：","This example dataset has no noise, and is simply a quadratic function sampled at 6 equally spaced points. We will get to know this dataset well.":"这份例子没有噪声，只是一个二次函数在 6 个等距点上的采样。后面会跟它混得很熟。","Our Piecewise Linear Model":"我用的分段线性模型","Let's be precise about exactly what model we are working with. We work entirely in one dimension, so our input data is a vector":"我先把模型说清楚。这里只看一维，输入是向量",", and our target is a vector":"，目标是向量",". Our model will attempt to fit a piecewise linear function to this dataset, and the way we'll do that is to pick":"。我要用分段线性函数去拟合，做法是先选一些","knot points":"节点","where our linear function will be allowed to bend. This will be represented by use of":"，折线只允许在这些地方拐弯。表示方法是","radial basis functions":"径向基函数",", in particular we will define:":"，具体我定义成：","and say that our model will be written as":"并规定模型写成","are learnable parameters. It can be readily checked (since the absolute value function is linear aside from a singularity at the origin) that this is a parametrization of piecewise linear functions with breakpoints only allowed at the":"是可学习参数。不难核对（绝对值函数除了原点那个尖点之外都是线性的）：这就是分段线性函数的一种参数化，断点只允许出现在这些","points. We will consider the knot points":"点上。节点","to be fixed, and picked as independent and identically distributed random points from a continuous distribution with density":"我先固定下来，并当成从密度为",". Typically we take":"的连续分布里抽出的独立同分布随机点。通常我会让","to be uniform on the range of":"在",", although we discuss the problem in generality.":"的取值范围上均匀，不过下面按一般情形来讲。","We will fit this model by ordinary least squares regression on our learnable parameters. As is standard, we will seek the minimum norm solution when the system is under-determined (when we have more parameters than data points). To simplify the analysis, we will assume we are only looking to minimize":"拟合用普通最小二乘，对可学习参数来做。欠定（参数比数据点多）时，按惯例取最小范数解。为了分析简单，我只最小化","(excluding":"（不把","from the minimization).":"放进最小化里）。","Below The Interpolation Threshold":"插值阈值以下","While not the focus of the document, let's take a moment to consider what happens when we are well below the interpolation threshold. Without any knot points":"虽然不是这篇的重点，还是先看一眼远远低于插值阈值时会怎样。一个节点都没有时","this is ordinary least squares regression.":"这就是普通最小二乘回归。","As our dataset is not linear, this fit is not particularly great, and we will be able to improve our fit by adding a point.":"数据并不是线性的，所以这条拟合不算好；再加一个点，拟合就能改善。","How much this improves performance depends heavily on the dataset, the ground truth, and choice of random point. Either numerical testing or an exact computation shows that on average the performance is improved versus the linear solution with regards to":"能改善多少，非常依赖数据、真实函数，以及那个随机点落在哪。数值试验或精确计算都表明，平均而言，相对纯线性解，表现会变好——对任意","-th power of any":"次幂、任意","-norm with":"范数，只要",". This includes the MSE and the MAE metrics.":"。均方误差和平均绝对误差都算在内。","So far, this perfectly matches traditional statistical intuition. Our simple linear fit is poor since the ground truth function is non-linear. Expanding our model to allow for non-linearity should improve the fit, as long as there is sufficient data. In this case, out six data points are enough to provide a better model when you allow for a single bend at a random location.":"到这里，完全符合传统统计直觉。真实函数不是线性的，所以简单直线拟合会差。只要数据够，把模型放开一点、允许非线性，拟合就该变好。这里六个点已经够：允许在一个随机位置拐一次，就能得到更好的模型。","At The Interpolation Threshold":"就在插值阈值上","We now turn our attention to the behavior":"接下来看","the interpolation threshold. Our goal here will be to illustrate why this will perform poorly by showing that, for this model, the average error (averaged over our random knot points) is infinite!":"插值阈值上的表现。我要说明它为什么差：对这个模型，平均误差（对随机节点取平均）是无穷大！","Let's visualize an example of a model at the interpolation threshold.":"先看一个插值阈值上的模型例子。","In this case, we see that we have four knot points placed between the five right-most data points, and then no knot point between the two left-most ones. The main takeaway we want you to have is that, for this choice of points, there is no freedom in how the interpolating model is selected once the knot points are fixed. There is a unique line which passes through the left two points, and if we want to exactly interpolate the data we must start with that line.":"这里可以看到：四个节点落在最右边五个数据点之间，最左边那两个点中间一个节点都没有。我想让你带走的是：节点一旦固定，对这种点的摆法，插值模型没有任何自由度。穿过左边两点的直线是唯一的，若要精确插值，就只能从这条线出发。","This line intersects the vertical blue line denoting the knot point at":"这条线会碰到表示节点","at a point which is uniquely determined by the line on the left, and then it must bend to pass through the data point at":"的那条竖直蓝线，交点被左边那条线唯一决定，然后必须拐弯，才能穿过数据点",". From there it hits the knot point at":"。接着碰到节点",", at which point again it must bend to hit the point at":"，到了那里又必须拐弯，才能打中点",", and so on. There is never any choice since it needs to be an interpolating function, so the entire solution is fixed.":"，如此一路下去。因为它必须是插值函数，从来没有选择，整个解都被钉死了。","This is one of the main lessons of what happens at the interpolation threshold—there is":"这是插值阈值上最重要的一课——你拟合的模型带着","rigidity":"刚性","in what model you fit. Indeed, it is not uncommon that there is exactly":"。事实上并不少见：恰好只有","such choice (and a count of variable and constraints tells us that this should frequently happen when we have two fewer knot points than data points, although this is not guaranteed). If there is only one choice, we have no control over whether that model is good or not, and we should expect a bad fit.":"一种选择（数一数变量和约束就知道：节点比数据点少两个时，这种事会经常发生，虽然不是必然）。如果只有一种选择，我没法控制它好不好，平均而言就该预期会差。","To see an example that is a bad fit, let's move the left-most knot point towards the right.":"看一个很差的拟合：把最左边的节点往右挪。","This performance is extremely bad—jumping up to almost a value of":"表现极差——预测值几乎跳到","when predicting a function whose maximum value is":"，而这段区域里真实函数的最大值才","in this region. Indeed, we can shift again and make it even more pronounced, jumping all the way to almost":"。再挪一下会更夸张，几乎跳到","This phenomena can be made so pronounced that our":"这现象可以夸张到：我的","error can be made infinite for all":"误差对所有","First, let's focus on what this picture is showing. We are throwing down four random knot points, three of which are to the right of":"先看这张图在说什么。我扔下四个随机节点，其中三个在","(for concreteness, say the first three), and one (say the last) occurs just to the left of":"的右边（说具体点，就当前三个），还有一个（就算最后一个）刚好落在",". To be explicit, let's make that left-most point fall in the interval of":"的左边一点点。再写清楚：让最左边那个点落在区间",". This whole event occurs with probability":"里。整个事件发生的概率是","We will throw out all constants as they are immaterial for the argument being made, and concentrate only on how this depends on":"常数对论证不重要，我全部丢掉，只看它怎样依赖","When this occurs, the left-most segment will hit the left-most knot point somewhere below the point":"一旦发生，最左边那一段会在点","(since the dataset comes from a quadratic function that is curving up). To be concrete let's say that we ensure this by picking":"的下方某处碰到最左节点（因为数据来自一条往上弯的二次函数）。说具体点，我把","sufficiently small that it is no larger than":"取得足够小，保证它不大于",". Then, at that knot point, the next linear segment will need to go from a point no better than":"。然后在那个节点上，下一段直线必须从一个不优于",", which means that it needs to have a slope of at least":"的点出发，也就是斜率至少要有","to pass through the point":"，才能穿过点","With this enormous slope, we have our key component. It means that our function has started oscillating wildly, and we simply need to show it is wild enough to make the average error infinite under any":"有了这个巨大斜率，关键零件就齐了。函数已经开始疯长乱摆，我只需要说明：它乱得足够让任意","norm. Notice that we have no other knot points in the region from":"范数下的平均误差变成无穷。注意，从",". On that region, our interpolating function takes on a value no smaller than":"这段区间里没有别的节点。在这段上，插值函数取值不会小于","is the slope, and thus the value and the error are both of order":"是斜率，所以函数值和误差都是","on that interval of width":"量级，而这段区间宽度是","Now we finish the argument. By considering the sequence of":"现在收束论证。考虑一串","we can create a sequence of disjoint events which we can use to lower-bound our expected error. In particular, letting":"，就能做出一串互不相交的事件，用来给期望误差下界。特别地，令","be this piecewise linear interpolating function, and":"为这条分段线性插值函数，","be the ground-truth quadratic, we see that we may estimate (discarding constants)":"为真实的二次函数，就可以估计（丢掉常数）","where the norm is understood as the integral over the range":"这里的范数理解成在区间","(in essence the infinite data limit). Thus the average error, as measured by the":"上的积分（本质上是无穷数据极限）。于是平均误差，用","-norm (for":"范数来量（对","), is infinite. Indeed, for many reasonable metrics beyond the":"）是无穷大。事实上，许多比","-norm, this error should be infinite.":"范数更一般、仍然合理的度量下，这个误差也该是无穷。","If you follow through this argument in generality, you get the following fact:":"把这个论证推到一般情形，会得到下面这件事：","Proposition 1.":"命题 1.","For any dataset":"对任意数据集","points, not all co-linear, any bounded ground truth function":"个点、且不全部共线，任意有界的真实函数",", and any":"，以及任意",", then the best fit model of this type,":"，这类模型的最佳拟合，在","knot points has":"个节点时有","The moral of this story is that these models are so rigid, that bad behavior is guaranteed independent of the dataset or ground truth. Simply the fact that you have a unique interpolating function that can only bend at random points forces the fit to be poor on average when you are at the interpolation threshold.":"这件事的教训是：这些模型太刚了，坏行为跟数据和真实函数都无关，是保底会发生的。你手里只有一条唯一的插值函数，还只能在随机点上拐弯——光这一条，就逼着插值阈值上的平均拟合变差。","To Infinity! (But Not Beyond)":"走向无穷！（但别再远了）","We now understand why the model performs poorly at the interpolation threshold. In essence, there is only one model that can be used, and that model has no reason to be a reasonable interpolating function at all! This provides the first hint as to why larger models might be better: they provide many choices about which interpolating model to pick, and perhaps we can structure the model in such a way that the interpolation of the prediction between points is much better behaved.":"现在明白为什么插值阈值上会差了。本质上只有一个能用的模型，而它完全没有理由是一条像样的插值函数！这也给出第一个提示，为什么更大的模型可能更好：选择变多了，也许可以把模型结构得让点与点之间的插值更听话。","To see why this is so, suppose we have a nice function (at least twice continuously differentiable) and we select a large number of random knot points":"要看清为什么，假设有一个很乖的函数（至少二阶连续可微），再抽一大堆随机节点","from our density":"，来自密度",". Recall that the way we are selecting the model is to minimize":"。回想一下，选模型的方式是最小化",", which is the norm of the coefficients we put in front of our basis functions. Our goal is to provide an interpretation of this norm in terms of our smooth function":"，也就是基函数前面那些系数的范数。目标是：用光滑函数","In the above figure we see three consecutive knot points:":"上图里有三个相邻节点：",". We will let":"。令","represent the slopes of the two segments adjacent to":"表示紧挨着","denotes the distance between the midpoints of those two segments. We now collect three facts:":"表示这两段中点之间的距离。下面攒三条事实：","are two consecutive intervals in our piecewise linear function, we can express the difference in the slope in terms of the coefficients of the model. In particular, note that":"是分段线性函数里两段相邻区间，斜率差可以用模型系数写出来。特别地，注意","are all linear across both intervals. This means that they all contribute the same constant expression to the slope on both sides, thus the difference in slopes":"在两段上都是线性的。它们给两边斜率贡献的是同一个常数，所以斜率差","is simply the difference in slopes between the two sides of":"就只是",". This difference is":"两侧的斜率差。这个差是","Since the points on our piecewise linear interpolation are assumed to be close together, and all lie on our smooth function":"分段线性插值上的点假定挨得很近，而且都落在光滑函数",", we can say that both":"上，于是可以说","are approximately equal to the derivative of":"都大约等于","at the midpoints of the intervals. Thus, their difference quotient is approximately the second derivative at":"在各段中点处的导数。于是它们的差商大约就是","Finally, let us collect two approximations for the probability that a randomly sampled point from":"最后再攒两个近似：从","lands in the interval connecting the two midpoints. On the one hand, the definition of density tells us this probability is obtained by integrating":"里随机抽到的点，落进连接两个中点的那一段的概率。一方面，按密度的定义，这个概率是把","over the interval, which (because the interval is small) is approximately equal to the rectangular approximation":"在这段上积分；因为区间很小，大约等于矩形近似",". On the other hand, we sampled":"。另一方面，我从","points from":"抽了",", and one landed in that interval, so we may also approximate it by":"个点，其中有一个落在这段里，所以也可以近似成","Note: this third approximation is too rough to fully rigorously derive what follows. Instead, you need to consider an intermediate scale made by collecting together, for instance,":"注意：这第三条近似太粗，还不够拿来严格推出后面的结论。你需要看一个中间尺度，比如把","many points. Since":"个点收在一起。因为",", this lets us both assume the second derivative is approximately constant in that region and that the length of the interval has random fluctuations much smaller than the length itself. Given this is not a formal paper, we do not follow this any further.":"，就可以同时假定二阶导数在那一带近似为常数，而且区间长度的随机起伏远小于长度本身。这不是一篇正式论文，我就不往下追了。","We can try to understand what our norm minimization means in terms of the curve. By our second bullet point, we may write:":"可以试着从曲线本身去理解这个范数最小化在干什么。按第二条，可以写成：","Applying the third bullet point to one of the":"把第三条用到其中一个","The last equation is a discrete approximation to the integral of":"最后一个式子是",", so we can conclude:":"积分的离散近似，于是可以下结论：","We typically consider the case where":"通常考虑","is constant on an interval, and zero outside it, so if we assume that":"在一个区间上为常数、外面为零的情形；若再假定","outside our interval (it is linear outside the interval), then we may conclude that we are trying to minimize":"在区间外（区间外它是线性的），就可以说我在最小化的是","This is the key observation. By minimizing the":"这是关键观察。最小化参数的","-norm of our parameters, we are actually minimizing the integral of the square of the second derivative of our smooth interpolating function. Since second derivatives tell us the change in derivative, this can be interpreted as trying to find an interpolating function whose derivative changes as little as possible, or equivalently one which is as close to linear as possible.":"范数，其实是在最小化光滑插值函数二阶导数的平方积分。二阶导数说的是导数怎么变，所以可以理解成：找一条导数变得尽量少的插值函数，也就是尽量接近直线的那条。","This idea is so fundamental, it has been studied extensively before and goes by the name of the":"这个想法太基本了，前人已经研究得很深，名字就叫","natural cubic spline":"自然三次样条","(see for example these notes":"（比如可以看这些笔记","or Chapter 11 of A&G":"，或 A&G 第 11 章","). This is a well studied class of interpolating functions.":"）。这是一类被研究得很透的插值函数。","This is only one portion of a full proof. Some additional work, such as showing that any convergent subsequence of discrete minimizers must converge to a function that is twice differentiable, is needed. However, once done, this produces the following theorem.":"这只是完整证明的一块。还需要一些额外工作，比如证明离散最小化子的任何收敛子列，都收敛到一个二阶可微函数。做完之后，就得到下面的定理。","Theorem.":"定理.","Take a sequence of our approximating functions":"取一列逼近函数","associated to the first":"，对应前","from an infinite sequence of independent and identically distributed knot points drawn uniformly from an interval containing your dataset. Then the sequence":"个来自无穷独立同分布节点序列的节点，这些节点在包含数据的一个区间上均匀抽取。那么序列","almost surely converges uniformly on that interval to the natural cubic spline.":"几乎必然在该区间上一致收敛到自然三次样条。","We picture the cubic spline and an interpolating function with 1000 pts below. Notice that, as predicted by the theory above, these pictures are indistinguishable.":"下面画出三次样条，以及用 1000 个点做的插值函数。注意：按上面的理论，这两张图已经分不出来。","Let's recall what we've seen.":"回头看一眼走过的路。","In the first section, we briefly discussed the classical regime to see that indeed adding a single point does better than the simple linear fit. This tells us that, for this problem, there is a benefit in adding non-linearity.":"第一节很快看了经典区：加上一个点，确实比简单直线拟合更好。说明在这个问题里，引入非线性是有好处的。","In the second section, we continue to add points until we reach the exact same number of parameters as we have points. This is the point at which we expect interpolation to hold. In this case, we show that no matter the choice of data or target function, the average error":"第二节继续加点，直到参数个数刚好等于点数。这时该发生插值了。这里我说明：无论数据和目标函数怎么选，平均误差","be infinite (as measured by any":"是无穷大（用任何","-norm) simply owing to the fact that there is often only a single interpolating function, which with reasonably high probability is wildly behaved.":"范数来量）——常常只有一条插值函数，而且很大概率会疯长乱摆。","Finally, in the third section, we took the limit to an infinite number of points and saw that our condition of taking the minimum norm solution corresponds to taking the smoothest interpolating function as measured by the integral of the square of the second derivative.":"第三节把点数推到无穷，会看到：取最小范数解，对应的是按二阶导数平方积分来量、最平滑的那条插值函数。","This is exactly the energy minimized by the":"而这正是","interpolation.":"插值所最小化的能量。","In this case, we've reduced double descent to a completely unremarkable fact: that for a quadratic function, the cubic spline interpolating the points is a better approximation than any piecewise linear function with four or fewer segments! The fact that the model performs poorly at the interpolation threshold of five segments is an unavoidable consequence of the rigidity of the model forcing a single choice of interpolating function over which we have essentially no control.":"这样一来，双重下降被还原成一件毫不稀奇的事：对一个二次函数，穿过这些点的三次样条，比任何不超过四段的分段线性函数都逼近得更好！五个分段刚好落在插值阈值上会差，是模型刚性的必然结果——它逼出唯一一条插值函数，而我对它几乎毫无控制。","This exemplifies one of the reasons why people believe that double descent occurs: that the interpolating functions you find using very large models can be better behaved":"这也是人们相信双重下降会发生的原因之一：用非常大的模型找到的插值函数，在点与点","the points you are interpolating, and thus can match the ground truth better if you structure your large model in a way to build in an inductive bias towards the correct type of solution.":"可以更听话，于是只要把大模型结构得带上朝向正确解的归纳偏置，就更能贴住真实函数。","In many ways, one of the most powerful aspects of modern deep models is that the architecture can be tuned to be specific to the type of problem at hand (convolutions for images, recurrent for text, and now transformers for either). This tuning changes the types of functions that can be found, and thus changes the way that these models interpolate between the data points. It is a reasonable conjecture to believe that this tuning contributes to the observation of double descent commonly seen today.":"在很多方面，现代深度模型最厉害的一点，就是架构可以对着手头问题来拧（图像用卷积，文本用循环，现在两边都能用 Transformer）。这种拧法会改变能找到的函数类型，于是也改变模型在数据点之间怎么插值。一个合理的猜想是：今天常常看到的双重下降，跟这种拧法有关。","Thanks for reading! To learn more about machine learning, check out our":"今天先写到这里。想接着看可以回","textbook. If you have any comments, ideas, etc related to MLU-Explain articles, feel free to":"。如果你对这些笔记有想法，欢迎","Resources + Open Source:":"参考与开源","[1] CPSC 303: Energy in Cubic Splines, Power Series as Algorithms, and the Initial Value Problem(Joel Friedman)":"[1] CPSC 303: Energy in Cubic Splines, Power Series as Algorithms, and the Initial Value Problem","(Joel Friedman, 2019)":"","[2] A First Course in Numerical Methods (Uri M. Asher, Chen Greif)":"[2] A First Course in Numerical Methods","(Uri M. Asher, Chen Greif, 2019).":"","Threshold Optimization during Post-Processing":"后处理时优化阈值","To implement EO on a trained model, we can find probability thresholds for both groups,":"模型训好之后，要落实几率均等，我可以给两个群体各自找一个概率阈值，","Sorry, your browser does not support inline SVG.":"这张图需要内联 SVG，当前浏览器好像不支持。","The probability thresholds ultimately determine which outcome class the model will produce. The parameters,":"概率阈值最终决定模型吐出哪一类。模型的参数",", of the model remain unchanged in this case but the":"，这时并不改，但","outputs will differ depending on which threshold is used":"输出会跟着阈值变","To find the thresholds that meet the EO criterion, we try many different probability threshold values (per group) and keep track of what the given TPR (=1-FNR) and FPR values are.":"要找到满足几率均等的阈值，我会给每组试很多个概率阈值，并记下对应的 TPR（=1-FNR）和 FPR。","Check out the visualization below for additional details about the ROC curve and threshold optimization.":"下面这张图把 ROC 曲线和阈值优化摊得更开一点。","[1] Fairness and Machine Learning":"[1] 公平与机器学习","(Solon Barocas, Moritz Hardt, Arvind Narayanan).":"","[2] Fairness Beyond Disparate Treatment & Disparate Impact: Learning Classification without Disparate Mistreatment":"[2] 超越差别对待与差别影响：学习无差别误判的分类","(Muhammad Bilal Zafar, Isabel Valera, Manuel Gomez Rodriguez, Krishna P. Gummadi, 2016).":"","[3] Equality of Opportunity in Supervised Learning":"[3] 监督学习中的机会均等","(Moritz Hardt, Eric Price, Nathan Srebro, 2016).":"","Equality Of Odds":"几率均等","A Visual Introduction to Measuring and Mitigating Bias in Machine Learning":"用图看清机器学习里的偏见，以及怎么把它压下去","Mia Mayer":"",", April 2023":"，2023 年 4 月","Machine Learning models learn to make predictions by looking at data with the help of algorithms, both of which can potentially be biased against different groups of people. Unwanted bias in machine learning can inadvertently harm, and negatively stereotype against underrepresented or (historically and otherwise) disfavored groups. Therefore, it is crucial to":"机器学习模型靠数据和算法来做预测，而这两者都可能对某些人偏心。不想要的偏见会悄悄伤到人，也会把常被忽视、或一向不被待见的群体刻板化。所以我得","evaluate and control data and model predictions not only for general machine learning performance but also for bias":"在看模型好不好用的同时，也把数据和预测里的偏见盯住、管住","Defining Equalized Odds":"几率均等在说什么","In this article, we will review a well-known fairness criterion, called 'Equalized Odds' (EO). EO aims to equalize the error a model makes when predicting categorical outcomes for different groups, here:":"这篇我想把一个很常见的公平标准摊开看看，叫「几率均等」（EO）。它想让模型在给不同群体做分类时，犯的错差不多，这里是：","EO takes the merit different groups of people have into account by considering the underlying ground truth distribution of the labels. This ensures the errors across outcomes and groups are similar, i.e. fair.":"几率均等会看各组真实标签本来长什么样，相当于承认各组底子不同。这样，不同结果、不同群体上的错误才会被拉到相近，也就是更公平。","For example, if we consider a hiring scenario, the errors EO compares are 'wrong rejection' and 'wrong acceptance'. We could simply count the number of wrong rejections and acceptances but as groups generally differ in size, we should use":"比如招人时，几率均等要比较的错是「不该拒却拒了」和「不该收却收了」。只数个数不行，因为各组人数差很多，所以该看","error rates":"错误率","instead as those are scale invariant. Useful error rates to consider are the False Negative Rate (FNR)":"它们不吃样本量的大小。常用的有假阴率（FNR）","and False Positive Rate (FPR)":"和假阳率（FPR）","of a classifier, or the combination of both those error rates":"，或者把这两种错误率合在一起看","According to EO, a":"按几率均等的说法，一个","model is fair if the predictions it makes have the same TPR":"模型算公平，是指它做的预测有相同的 TPR","and FPR across all groups":"和 FPR，对所有群体都成立","in the dataset. Formally, this can be written as:":"。写成式子就是：","`)} denotes predictions (here: positive),":"`)} 表示预测（这里是正类），","refers to the group membership and":"表示属于哪个群体，","represents the ground truth.":"表示真实标签。","Equalized odds aims to match TPR and FPR for different groups, punishing models that perform well for one group only. Unfortunately this can be very hard to achieve in practice, so it makes sense to relax the EO criterion and consider a modified version of the EO equation with":"几率均等想让不同群体的 TPR 和 FPR 对齐，只对某一组特别准的模型会被罚。可惜现实里很难做到，所以我会放松几率均等，改看带","for equalizing TPR":"用来对齐 TPR","(equal opportunity)":"（机会均等）","for equalizing FPR.":"用来对齐 FPR。","While machine learning algorithms have the potential to revolutionize decision-making, we have to ensure that a fairness criteria is used for measuring any potential bias in addition to general Machine Learning metrics. Depending on the outcome of the bias evaluation we should include bias mitigation. Equality of Odds (EO) offers a promising approach to mitigate bias and is a method that can be used in different ways (and even during post-processing with access only to the predictions). However, before using EO for evaluation or bias mitigation, we should carefully consider the context and potential trade-offs between competing objectives.":"机器学习确实可能把决策翻个天。可是除了常规指标，我还得用公平标准去量有没有偏见。量完如果偏了，再考虑怎么纠。几率均等（EO）是一条还算能用的路，用法也多（甚至模型已经训完、手里只有预测，也能做后处理）。不过在拿它来评估或纠偏之前，我得先看清场景，以及它跟别的目标会不会打架。","Constrained Optimization during Training":"训练时加上约束","To implement EO during model training, we can constrain the possible set of parameters,":"想在训练过程中落实几率均等，我可以限制参数",", that the so-called loss function":"，能取的范围，也就是所谓损失函数",", can assume. The constraint can be written as:":"，允许踩到的值。约束可以写成：","\\\\mathrel":"\\\\mathrel","Y, A= ` )}":"Y, A= ` )}","Y, A= \\\\:` )}":"Y, A= \\\\:` )}","`)}. The smaller":"`)}。越小",", the fairer the decision boundary.":"，决策边界就越公平。","Notice that compared to the EO equation, the constraint is actually 'relaxed' as we only require the parameters to create a solution where the difference between FPR and FNR respectively is smaller than":"注意，比起原来的几率均等等式，这个约束其实是「放松」过的：我只要求参数找到一组解，让 FPR 之差和 FNR 之差分别小于","Equalized Odds to measure fairness":"用几率均等来量公平","Using the EO equation, we can derive different metrics to measure the fairness of a model. For example, we can look at:":"从几率均等的式子出发，我可以写出几种度量模型公不公平的指标。比如说：","The metrics above show how fair/unfair the model is by measuring either FPR or FNR; but according to EO, we need":"上面这些指标用 FPR 或 FNR 来看模型公不公平；可按几率均等，我需要","both values to be the same":"两个值都对齐","(known as":"（也叫","Conditional Procedure Accuracy Equality":"条件程序准确率均等","also achieving a certain predictive performance":"同时还得让模型保持一定的预测水平","with the model.":"。","Have a look at the beeswarm plot below. It shows how the predictions of a model change when the probability threshold (the slider) is moved.":"看看下面这张蜂群图。拖动概率阈值（那条滑块），模型的预测会跟着变。","Try to find a probability threshold that results in 0 FPR and FNR difference at the same time; is it even possible? Also observe what the":"试着找一个阈值，让 FPR 差和 FNR 差同时为 0——做得到吗？也盯着","model performance":"模型表现","(here: group-wise accuracy) is doing as you move the slider.":"（这里是各组准确率）在滑块移动时怎么走。","`, false )}":"`, false )}","false )}":"false )}","Note that as you drag the slider, you might find some so-called":"拖滑块的时候，你可能会撞上所谓","lazy solutions where everyone gets rejected or accepted":"偷懒解：所有人都被拒，或所有人都被收",". These are solutions where the FPR or FNR difference is indeed 0. However, while these solutions technically meet the relaxed version of EO, they make little sense from a general ML performance perspective (check out the accuracy values of the model).":"。这些解的 FPR 差或 FNR 差确实是 0。放松版的几率均等技术上算满足了，可从机器学习好不好用的角度看，几乎没意义（去看一眼准确率就知道）。","You can also verify that there is no probability threshold where FPR and FPR are the same for both groups by comparing the values in the chart below:":"对照下面这张图也能看出来：没有哪个概率阈值能让两组的 FPR 对齐：","False Negative Error Rate (FNR) Balance":"假阴率（FNR）平衡","To calculate FNR balance, we work out FNR":"算 FNR 平衡时，我先算出 FNR","per group and take the difference. It is also possible to rewrite this using TPR":"，按组再相减。也可以改写成 TPR","The resulting value will be in the range [-1, 1], the closer to 0 the closer to equal opportunity the groups for EO equation where":"得到的值落在 [-1, 1]，越靠近 0，两组就越接近机会均等，对应几率均等式子里","False Positive Error Rate (FPR) Balance":"假阳率（FPR）平衡","To calculate FPR balance, we work out FPR":"算 FPR 平衡时，我先算出 FPR","per group and take the difference:":"，按组再相减：","The resulting value will be in the range [-1, 1], the closer to 0, the more predictive equality the model achieves and we satisfy the EO equation where":"得到的值落在 [-1, 1]，越靠近 0，预测均等做得越好，也越满足几率均等式子里","Equalized Odds to achieve fairness":"用几率均等来促成公平","Using EO, we can also influence the predictions a model makes to achieve a more fair outcome. We are going to look at two different ways of performing this: by constraining the model during training and by introducing group-wise probability thresholds for a trained model.":"几率均等也能用来改模型的预测，让结果更公平一点。我准备看两条路：训练时给模型加约束，以及模型训完后给不同群体设不同的概率阈值。","To visualize the search for the probability thresholds that meets EO, we can look at the so-called":"想看清满足几率均等的阈值是怎么找出来的，可以看所谓","ROC curves":"ROC 曲线","for both groups,":"，两组各自一条",". We can see that for most probability thresholds the TPR and FPR values are different per group. For the dataset shown below, there is only one point where TPR and FPR are equal for both groups (and not lazy solutions); this is where the EO criterion is satisfied.":"。大多数阈值下，两组的 TPR 和 FPR 都不一样。下面这份数据里，只有一个点能让两组的 TPR、FPR 同时对齐（而且还不是偷懒解）；那就是几率均等被满足的地方。","Probability Threshold":"概率阈值","Count of Outcomes":"结果计数","by Probability Threshold":"按概率阈值","d.group == \"triangle\"))} />":"d.group == \"triangle\"))} />","d.group == \"circle\"))} />":"d.group == \"circle\"))} />","per group":"按组","False Positive Rate":"假阳率","True Positive Rate":"真正例率","match for both groups (neither are 0) and EO is satisfied.":"对上了（两边都不是 0），几率均等就满足了。","In this region,":"在这片区域，","= 1 for both groups (lazy solution).":"= 1，两组都是（偷懒解）。","= 0 for both groups (lazy solution).":"= 0，两组都是（偷懒解）。","How It Works, Briefly":"先快速看一遍","To make linear regression easier to digest, let's go through a quick, high-level introduction of how it works. We'll scroll through the core concepts of the algorithm at a high-level, and then delve into the details thereafter:":"线性回归要好入口一点，我先用很高的视角过一遍它怎么走。先滚过算法的几个核心想法，细节放到后面：","Let's fit a model to predict housing price ($) in San Diego, USA using the size of the house (in square-footage):":"我想用房屋面积（平方英尺）去猜美国圣迭戈的房价（$）：","We'll start with a very simple model, predicting the price of each house to be just the average house price in our dataset, ~$290,000, ignoring the different sizes of each house:":"先从一个特别笨的模型开始：不管房子多大，房价一律猜成数据里的平均价，大约 29 万美元：","Of course we know this model is bad - the model doesn't fit the data well at all. But how can do quantify exactly":"当然我知道这模型很差——完全没贴上数据。可我要怎么把「差」说清楚","To evaluate our model's performance quantitatively, we plot the error of each observation directly. These errors, or":"想把表现说成数字，我直接把每个点的误差画出来。这些误差，也就是",", measure the distance between each observation and the predicted value for that observation. We'll make use of these residuals later when we talk about evaluating regression models, but we can clearly see that our model has a lot of error.":"，量的是每个点和预测值之间的距离。后面评价回归时还会用到残差，不过现在已经能看出：这个模型错得不少。","The goal of linear regression is reducing this error such that we find a line/surface that 'best' fits our data. For our simple regression problem, that involves estimating the y-intercept and slope of our model,":"线性回归要做的，就是把这类误差压下去，找到一条「最贴」数据的线或面。这个小问题里，我要估的是截距和斜率，","`, false )}.":"`, false )}。","For our specific problem, the best fit line is shown. There's still error, sure, but the general pattern is captured well. As a result, we can be reasonably confident that if we plug in new values of square-footage, our predicted values of price would be reasonably accurate.":"对眼前这份数据，最佳拟合线就是这样。误差还在，可大趋势抓住了。所以我大致敢说：再塞进新的面积，猜出的房价也不会太离谱。","Once we've fit our model, predicting future values is super easy! We just plug in any":"模型拟合好了，预测新值就很轻松！任意","values into our equation!":"代入式子就行！","For our simple model, that means plugging in a value for":"这个小模型里，就是把","into our model:":"代进去：","Thus, our model predicts a house that is":"于是模型会猜，这栋","square-feet will cost":"平方英尺的房子要花","Now that we have a high-level idea of how linear regression works, let's dive a bit deeper. The remainder of this article will cover how to evaluate regression models, how to find the \"best\" model, how to interpret different forms of regression models, and the assumptions underpinning correct usage of regression models in statistical settings.":"高层面已经有感觉了，接着再往下挖一点。后面我会写：怎么评价回归、怎么找到「最好」的模型、不同形态该怎么读，以及统计场景里那些假设。","Let's dive in!":"开始往下看！","Model Evaluation":"怎么评价模型","To train an accurate linear regression model, we need a way to quantify how good (or bad) our model performs. In machine learning, we call such performance-measuring functions":"想训练出一个靠谱的线性回归，我得先能量出它好还是差。机器学习里，这种打分函数叫","loss functions":"损失函数",". Several popular loss functions exist for regression problems.":"。回归这边，常用的损失有好几种。","To measure our model's performance, we'll use one of the most popular: mean-squared error (MSE).":"衡量表现时，我先用最常见的一种：均方误差（MSE）。","Mean-Squared Error (MSE)":"均方误差（MSE）","MSE quantifies how close a predicted value is to the true value, so we'll use it to quantify how close a regression line is to a set of points. MSE works by squaring the distance between each data point and the regression line (the red residuals in the graphs above), summing the squared values, and then dividing by the number of data points:":"MSE 量的是预测离真实有多近，所以也可以拿它看回归线离这堆点有多近。做法是：把每个点到回归线的距离平方（就是上面图里那些红色残差），加起来，再除以点数：","\\\\Sigma^":"\\\\Sigma^","`, true )} The name is quite literal: take the mean of the squared errors. The squaring of errors prevents negative and positive terms from canceling out in the sum,":"`, true )} 名字很直白：把平方误差再取平均。一平方，正负就不会在求和时对消，","and gives more weight to points further from the regression line, punishing outliers. In practice, we'll fit our regression model to a set training data, and evaluate it's performance using MSE on the test dataset.":"离线更远的点也会被加重惩罚。实际里，我在训练集上拟合，再用测试集上的 MSE 看它好不好。","Regression models may also be evaluated with the so-called":"回归还可以用所谓的","goodness of fit":"拟合优度","measures, which summarize how well a model fits a set of data. The most popular goodness of fit measure for linear regression is r-squared, a metric that represents the percentage of the variance in":"来概括「贴得怎么样」。线性回归里最常见的是 r-squared，它表示","explained by our features":"的方差里，有多少被特征解释","More specifically, r-squared measures the percentage of variance explained normalized against the baseline variance of our model (which is just the variance of the mean):":"更具体一点，r-squared 是「被解释的方差」再拿模型的基线方差（其实就是均值的方差）去除一下：","(y_i - \\\\bar":"(y_i - \\\\bar","`, true )} The highest possible value for r-squared is 1, representing a model that captures 100% of the variance. A negative r-squared means that our model is doing worse (capturing less variance) than a flat line through mean of our data would.":"`, true )} r-squared 最高是 1，表示抓住了 100% 的方差。要是它是负数，说明我的模型还不如一条穿过均值的平线。","To build intuition for yourself, try changing the weight and bias terms below to see how the MSE and r-squared change across different model fits:":"自己拖一下权重和偏置，看看不同拟合下 MSE 和 r-squared 会怎么跳：","mseScatterClass.shuffleData()} >Shuffle Data":"mseScatterClass.shuffleData()} >打乱","Weight (":"权重（","For our model:":"我的模型：","You will often see R-Squared referenced in statistical contexts as a way to assess model fit.":"统计语境里，常会看到用 R-Squared 来判断模型贴不贴。","Selecting An Evaluation Metric":"选哪个评估指标","Many methods exist for evaluating regression models, each with different concerns around interpretability, theory, and usability. The evaluation metric should reflect whatever it is you actually care about when making predictions. For example, when we use MSE, we are implicitly saying that we think the cost of our prediction error should reflect the quadratic (squared) distance between what we predicted and what is correct. This may work well if we want to punish outliers or if our data is minimized by the mean, but this comes at the cost of interpretability: we output our error in squared units (though this may be fixed with":"评价回归的办法很多，可读性、理论和好不好用，各有各的心事。指标应该对应我预测时真正在意的东西。比如用 MSE，其实是在说：预测错了，代价该按平方距离来算。想惩罚离群点，或数据更该被均值压住时，这很合适，可读性会差一点：误差的单位变成了平方（也可以改用","). If instead we wanted our error to reflect the linear distance between what we predicted and what is correct, or we wanted our data minimized by the median, we could try something like Mean Abosulte Error (":"）。如果我想让误差跟直线距离有关，或者按中位数来压，可以试试平均绝对误差（","). Whatever the case, you should be thinking of your evaluation metric as part of your modeling process, and select the best metric based on the specific concerns of your use-case.":"）。不管哪种，评估指标也是建模的一部分，按眼前这份任务真正在意的来选。","Interpreting Regression Models":"怎么读回归模型","One of the most powerful aspects of regression models is their interpretability. However, different forms of regression models require different interpretations. To make this clear, we'll walk through several typical constructs of a regression model, and describe how to interpret each in turn. For all aforementioned models, we interpret the error term as irreducible noise not captured by our model.":"回归最厉害的地方之一，就是还能读懂。不过形态不同，读法也不同。我挨个走几种常见写法，再分别说怎么读。上面这些模型里，误差项我都当成模型没抓住的、减不掉的噪声。","Select a tab to learn how to interpret the given form of regression model:":"点一个标签，看这种回归该怎么读：","Of course, this is not an exhaustive list of regression models, many other forms exist!":"当然这不是全部，回归还有很多别的样子！","[1] Regression And Other Stories":"[1] 《回归与其他故事》","[3] Mathematical Statistics and Data Analysis":"[3] 《数理统计与数据分析》","(John A. Rice, 2010).":"","Linear Regression":"线性回归","A Visual Introduction To (Almost) Everything You Should Know":"该知道的，我尽量画一遍",", September 2022":"","Regression Model Assumptions":"回归的那些假设","When teaching regression models, it's common to mention the various assumptions underpinning linear regression. For completion, we'll list some of those assumptions here. However, in the context of machine learning we care most about if the predictions made from our model generalize well to unseen data. We'll use our model if it generalizes well even if it violates statistical assumptions. Still, no treatment of regression is complete without mentioning the assumptions.":"讲回归时，常会提到线性回归底下那些假设。为了写全，我也列在这儿。不过在机器学习里，我更在意预测能不能泛化到没见过的数据。只要泛化还行，就算踩了统计假设，我也会用。可回归这篇要是完全不提假设，也不完整。","Validity:":"有效性：","Does the data we're modeling matches to the problem we're actually trying to solve?":"我正在建模的数据，真的对应我想解决的问题吗？","Representativeness:":"代表性：","Is the sample data used to train the regression model representative of the population to which it will be applied?":"拿来训练的样本，能代表以后要套用的那群人吗？","Additivity and Linearity:":"可加性与线性：","The deterministic component of a regression model is a linear function of the separate predictors:":"回归里那个确定的部分，是各个预测变量的线性函数：","Independence of Errors:":"误差独立：","The errors from our model are independent.":"模型的误差彼此独立。","Homoscedasticity:":"同方差：","The errors from our model have equal variance.":"模型的误差方差相同。","Normality of Errors:":"误差正态：","The errors from our model are normally distributed.":"模型的误差服从正态分布。","When Assumptions Fail?":"假设没站住呢？","What should we do if the assumptions for our regression model aren't met? Don't fret, it's not the end of the world! First, double-check that the assumptions even matter in the first place: if the predictions made from our model generalize well to unseen data, and our task is to create a model that generalizes well, then we're probably fine. If not, figure out which assumption is being violated, and how to address it! This will change depending on the assumption being violated, but in general, one can attempt to extend the model, accompany new data, transform the existing data, or some combination thereof. If a model transformation is unfit, perhaps the application (or research question) can be changed or restricted to better align with the data. In practice, some combination of the above will usually suffice.":"要是回归的假设没满足，该怎么办？别慌，世界不会因此结束。先确认这些假设到底重不重要：如果预测已经能泛化到没见过的数据，而我要的就是泛化，那大概没事。不然就找出是哪条假设破了，再对症处理。具体办法会随破掉的那条变，不过大体上可以扩展模型、补新数据、变换现有数据，或几样一起做。要是改模型不合适，也许可以把问题收窄一点，让它更贴这份数据。实践里，上面这些掺一掺，通常就够用。","Linear Regression is a simple and powerful model for predicting a numeric response from a set of one or more independent variables. This article will focus mostly on how the method is used in machine learning, so we won't cover common use cases like causal inference or experimental design. And although it may seem like linear regression is overlooked in modern machine learning's ever-increasing world of complex neural network architectures, the algorithm is still widely used across a large number of domains because it is effective, easy to interpret, and easy to extend. The key ideas in linear regression are recycled everywhere, so understanding the algorithm is a must-have for a strong foundation in machine learning.":"线性回归很简单，也很能打：用一个或多个自变量去猜一个数字。这篇我主要写它在机器学习里怎么用，因果推断、实验设计那些先放下。现在满眼都是复杂神经网络，线性回归好像被冷落了，可它还是到处都在用——有效、好读、也好接着改。它的核心想法会被反复拿出来，想把机器学习的底打牢，这个算法我得先懂。","Let's Be More Specific":"再说具体一点","Linear regression is a supervised algorithm":"线性回归是一种监督学习算法","that learns to model a dependent variable,":"，它学着把因变量",", as a function of some independent variables (aka \"features\"),":"写成自变量（也叫「特征」）的函数",", by finding a line (or surface) that best \"fits\" the data. In general, we assume":"，办法是找一条最贴数据的线（或面）。一般我默认","to be some number and each":"是个数字，而每个","can be basically anything. For example: predicting the price of a house using the number of rooms in that house (":"几乎可以是任何东西。比如用房间数去猜房价（",": price,":"：房价，",": number of rooms) or predicting weight from height and age (":"：房间数），或者用身高和年龄去猜体重（",": weight,":"：体重，",": height,":"：身高，","In general, the equation for linear regression is":"一般来说，线性回归的式子是",": the dependent variable; the thing we are trying to predict.":"：因变量，也就是我想猜的那个。",": the independent variables: the features our model uses to model y.":"：自变量，模型拿来拟合 y 的那些特征。",": the coefficients (aka \"weights\") of our regression model. These are the foundations of our model. They are what our model \"learns\" during optimization.":"：回归的系数（也叫「权重」）。模型的底就在这儿。优化时它真正在学的，就是这些。","represents the intercept of our model, and each other coefficient B":"是截距，其余每个系数 B","(i > 0) is a slope defining how variable x":"(i > 0) 是斜率，用来描述变量 x","contributes to the model. We discuss how to interpret regression coefficients later in the article.\" use:tooltip >[ℹ]":"会怎么进入模型。系数怎么读，我后面再写。\" use:tooltip >[ℹ]",": the irreducible error in our model. A term that collects together all the unmodeled parts of our data.":"：模型里减不掉的误差。数据里没被建模的部分，都堆在这儿。","Fitting a linear regression model is all about finding the set of cofficients that best model":"拟合线性回归，就是找到那组最能描述","as a function of our features. We may never know the true parameters for our model, but we can estimate them (more on this later). Once we've estimated these coefficients,":"作为特征的函数的系数。真实参数我可能永远不知道，但可以估（后面再说）。一旦估出这些系数，","`, false )}, we predict future values,":"`, false )}，我就能预测新的值，","x_p `, true )}":"x_p `, true )}","So predicting future values (often called inference), is as simple as plugging the values of our features":"所以预测新值（也叫推断），其实就是把特征","into our equation!":"代入式子！","A Closed-Form Solution":"解析解","We'd be remiss not to mention the Normal Equation, a widely taught method for obtaining estimates for our linear regression coefficients. The Normal Equation is a closed-form solution that allows us to estimate our coefficients directly by minimizing the":"还有正规方程，课堂上常教，用来直接估线性回归的系数。它是解析解：把数据的","residual sum of squares":"残差平方和","(RSS) of our data:":"（RSS）压到最小，就能直接写出系数：","RSS = \\\\sum^":"RSS = \\\\sum^","`, true )} The RSS should look familiar - it was a key piece in both the MSE and r-squared formulas that represents our model's total squared error:":"`, true )} RSS 应该眼熟——MSE 和 r-squared 里都有它，表示模型的总平方误差：","`, true )} Add circles to the chart below to see how the Normal Equation calculates two featues, the bias and weight, for the corresponding regression model.":"`, true )} 在下面图里加点，看看正规方程怎么算出对应回归的两个量：偏置和权重。","` : newcfCircles?.[3] ? `1 & $":"` : newcfCircles?.[3] ? `1 & $","^T \\\\begin":"^T \\\\begin","` : newcfCircles?.[3] ? `$":"` : newcfCircles?.[3] ? `$","`, true )}":"`, true )}","Despite providing a convenient closed-form solution for finding our optimal coefficients, the Normal Equation estimates are often not used in practice, because of the computational complexity required to invert a matrix with too many features. While our two feature example above runs fast (we can run it in the browser!), most machine learning models are more complicated. For this reason, we often just use gradient descent.":"正规方程写起来是很方便，可实践里常常不用：特征一多，求逆就太贵。上面只有两个特征，浏览器里跑得动；大多数模型更复杂。所以我更常直接用梯度下降。","Are Our Coefficients Valid?":"这些系数靠谱吗？","In research publications and statistical software, coefficients of regression models are often presented with associated p-values. These p-values come from traditional null hypothesis statistical tests: t-tests are used to measure whether a given cofficient is significantly different than zero (the null hypothesis that a particular coefficient":"论文和统计软件里，回归系数旁边常挂着 p 值。它们来自传统的原假设检验：t 检验看某个系数是不是显著不同于 0（原假设是这个系数","equals zero), while F tests are used to measure whether":"等于 0），F 检验则看是不是有","of the terms in a regression model are significantly different from zero. Different opinions exist on the utility of such tests (e.g. chapter 10.7 of":"项显著不同于 0。这种检验到底有没有用，看法不一样（比如","maintains they're not super important). We don't take a strong stance on this issue, but believe practitioners should always assess the standard error around any parameter estimates for themselves and present them in their research.":"第 10.7 章就觉得没那么重要）。我没有很硬的立场，只觉得自己应该先看看参数估计旁边的标准误，写进结果里。","Size of House (sqft)":"房屋面积（平方英尺）","Housing Price ($)":"房价（$）","Error (MSE)":"误差（MSE）","A Regression Model With One Continuous Feature":"一个连续特征的回归","Home Size (sqft)":"房屋面积（平方英尺）","House Price ($)":"房价（$）","Regression Model With One Continuous Feature":"一个连续特征的回归","Example:":"例子：",": This model summarizes the average house prices across differently sized houses (":"：这个模型概括的是，不同大小的房子（",") as measured in square feet.":"）按平方英尺来看，平均房价差多少。","The coefficient,":"系数是",", represents the average difference in housing price for one-unit difference in the square-footage of the house. In other words, we expect each additional square-foot, on average, to raise the price of a house by":"，表示房屋面积每差 1 平方英尺，房价平均差多少。换句话说，每多一平方英尺，房价平均大约涨","The intercept,":"截距",", represents the predicted housing price for houses with":"，表示",", that is, it represents the average price of a zero square-foot house. Because this value doesn't make much intuitive sense, it's common for models to be transformed and standardized before carrying out a regression model.":"时的预测房价，也就是 0 平方英尺的房子的均价。这个数不太好懂，所以做回归前，常会先变换、标准化数据。","A Regression Model With One Binary Feature":"一个二元特征的回归","Has Pool?":"有泳池？","House Price (USD)":"房价（美元）","House Price vs Pool (Binary)":"房价和泳池（二元）",": This model summarizes the difference in average housing prices between houses without swimming pools (":"：这个模型概括的是，没泳池的房子（",") and houses with swimming pools (":"）和有泳池的房子（",", is the average predicted price for houses that do not have swimming pools (to see this, simply set":"，是没泳池的房子的平均预测价（把","to 0 and solve the equation). To find the average price predicted price for houses with pools, we simply plug in":"设成 0，解一下式子就能看见）。想看有泳池的平均预测价，把","to obtain":"代进去，得到","The difference between these two subpopulation means is equal to the coefficient on":"这两群均值的差，正好等于",". It tells us that houses with pools cost":"上的那个系数。它在说：有泳池的房子平均要贵","more on average than houses that do not have pools.":"，比起没有泳池的房子。","Learning The Coefficients":"怎么学这些系数","Let's recap what we've learned so far: Linear regression is all about finding a line (or surface) that fits our data well. And as we just saw, this involves selecting the coefficients for our model that minimize our evaluation metric. But how can we best estimate these coefficients? In practice, they're unknown, and selecting them by hand quickly becomes infeasible for regression models with many features. There must be a better way!":"先收一收：线性回归就是找一条（或一面）贴着数据的线。而刚才也看到了，这等于挑一组让评估指标尽量小的系数。可这些系数到底怎么估？实践里它们是未知的，特征一多，手调很快就不现实。总得有更好的办法！","Luckily for us, several algorithms exist to do just this. We'll discuss two: an iterative solution and a closed-form solution.":"幸好有现成的算法。我写两种：一种迭代，一种解析。","An Iterative Solution":"迭代解","Gradient descent is an iterative optimization algorithm that estimates some set of coefficients to yield the minimum of a convex function. Put simply: it will find suitable coefficients for our regression model that minimize prediction error (remember, lower MSE equals better model).":"梯度下降是一种迭代优化：估一组系数，把凸函数压到最低。说白了：它会给回归找到让预测误差尽量小的系数（记住，MSE 越低，模型越好）。","A full conversation on gradient descent is outside the course of this article (stay-tuned for our future article on the subject), but if you'd like to learn more, click the \"Show Math\" button below. Otherwise, read on!":"梯度下降没法在这篇里讲完（以后有机会再写），想看更多可以点下面的 \"Show Math\"。不看也行，接着读就好！","Gradient descent works as follows. We assume that we have some convex function representing the error of our machine learning algorithm (in our case, MSE). Gradient descent will iteratively update our model's coefficients in the direction of our error functions minimum":"梯度下降是这样走的。我假定有一个凸函数，代表算法的误差（这里就是 MSE）。它会反复把系数往误差函数下降的方向推","In our case, our model takes the form:":"这个例子里，模型长这样：","`, true )} and our error function takes the form:":"`, true )} 误差函数长这样：","`, true )} Our goal is to find the coefficients,":"`, true )} 我要找的系数是",", to minimize the error function. To do this, we'll use the gradient, which represents the direction that the function is increasing, and the rate at which it is increasing. Since we want to find the minimum of this function, we can go in the opposite direction of where it's increasing. This is exactly what Gradient Descent does, it works by taking steps in the direction opposite of where our error function is increasing, proportional to the rate of change. To find the coefficients that minimize the function, we first calculate the derivatives of our error function is increasing. To find the coefficients that minimize first, calculate the derivatives of our loss function, MSE:":"，好让误差最小。这里会用到梯度：它告诉我函数在往哪升、升得多快。我想找最低点，就朝升高的反方向走。梯度下降干的就是这件事：按变化的快慢，朝误差升高的反方向迈步。要找到让函数最小的系数，先对损失函数 MSE 求导：","MSE = \\\\begin":"MSE = \\\\begin",") \\\\text":") \\\\text","`, true )} Now that we have the gradients for our error function (with respect to each coefficient to be updated), we perform iterative updates:":"`, true )} 有了对每个待更新系数的梯度，就可以反复更新：","= \\\\begin":"= \\\\begin","x_i\\\\sum^":"x_i\\\\sum^","Updating these values iteratively will yield coefficients of our model that minimize error.":"这样迭代下去，就能得到让误差尽量小的系数。","Gradient descent will iteratively identify the coefficients our model needs to fit the data. Let's see an example directly. We'll fit data to our equation":"梯度下降会一步步找出拟合数据需要的系数。直接看个例子。我把数据拟合到","`, false )}, so gradient descent will learn two coefficients,":"`, false )}，所以梯度下降要学两个系数，","(the intercept) and":"（截距）和","(the weight). To do so, interact with the plot below. Try dragging the weights and values to create a 'poorly' fit (large error) solution and run gradient descent to see the error iteratively improve.":"（权重）。拖一下下面的图试试：先把权重拉成一个很差（误差很大）的拟合，再跑梯度下降，看误差怎么一步步变好。","Click the buttons to run 1, 10, or 100 steps of gradient descent, and see the linear regression model update live. The error at each iteration of gradient descent (or manual coefficient update) is shown in the bottom chart. With each weight update, we recalculate the error, so you can see how gradient descent improves our model iteratively.":"点按钮，跑 1、10 或 100 步梯度下降，线性回归会当场更新。每一步的误差（或我手改系数时的误差）画在下面那张图。每次改权重，误差都会重算，能看见梯度下降是怎么一点点把模型推好的。","Our model:":"我的模型：","Although gradient descent is the most popular optimization algorithm in machine learning, it's not perfect! It doesn't work for every loss function, and it may not always find the most optimal set of coefficients for your model. Still, it has many extensions to help solve these issues, and is widely used across machine learning.":"梯度下降是机器学习里最常见的优化，可它不是万能的！不是每种损失都能用，也不保证总能找到最理想的那组系数。好在它有很多变体来补这些坑，所以还是到处都在用。","A Regression Model With Interaction Terms":"带交互项的回归","Regression Model With Interaction":"带交互的回归","*sqft + $":"*sqft + $",": If we believe that the slope for":"：要是我觉得","should differ between houses that do have pools and houses that do not, we can add an interaction term to our model,":"的斜率，在有泳池和没泳池的房子上不该一样，就可以给模型加一个交互项，","The coefficient of the interaction term":"交互项的系数",", represents the difference in the slope for":"，表示",", comparing houses that do and do not have pools. Visually, this represents the difference between the slopes of the two lines,":"的斜率，在有泳池和没泳池的房子之间差多少。从图上看，就是上面两条线的斜率差，",", above.":"。",", represents the predicted housing price for houses with no pools and a square-footage of zero.":"，表示没泳池、面积又是 0 时的预测房价。","The coefficient of":"系数，对应",", represents the average expected difference in houses of the same size (0":"，表示同样大小（0",") that differed in whether or not they had a pool. (It's not super useful since we don't have houses with 0 square-feet).":"）但一个有泳池、一个没有时，房价平均差多少。（房子没有 0 平方英尺的，所以这个数不太有用。）",", represents the average expected difference in housing price for houses that do not have a pool (":"，表示没泳池的房子（",") but differ in size by one square-foot.":"）面积每差 1 平方英尺，房价平均差多少。","Live Least-Squares Estimates":"最小二乘，现场估","Click To Add Circles And Update The Estimates":"点一下加点，估计会更新","Dive Deep":"还能挖得更深","The study of linear regression is a very deep topic: there's a ton of different things to talk about and we'd be foolish to try to cover them all in one single article. Some of those topics left unmentioned are: regularization methods, selection techniques, common regression transformations, bayesian formulations of regression, and additional evaluation techniques. For those interested in learning more, we recommend diving deep into the aforementioned topics, or reading the resources below. Hopefully this article serves as a nice starting point for learning about linear regression.":"线性回归能挖的东西很多，一篇里全塞进去不太明智。没写到的还有：正则、变量选择、常见变换、贝叶斯写法，以及更多评价办法。想接着看，可以往这些方向钻，或翻翻下面的资料。这篇就当一个起点。","Thanks for reading. We hope that the article is insightful no matter where you are along your Machine Learning journey, and that you came away with a better understanding of linear regression.":"今天先写到这里。想接着看可以回画廊。","A Multivariate Regression Model":"多元回归","Regression Model With Two Features":"两个特征的回归",": Typically, a regression model will contain more than one feature. We call this a":"：回归通常不止一个特征。我把它叫做","multivariate regression model":"多元回归",". In our example, we model home prices as a function of both the size of the house (":"。这个例子里，房价同时看房子有多大（",") and whether or not it has a pool (":"）以及有没有泳池（","= no pool and":"＝没泳池，","= has a pool).":"＝有泳池）。",", represents the predicted average housing price for houses with all":"，表示所有",". For our model, it represents the cost of houses with no pools and a square-footage of zero.":"时的平均预测房价。在我的模型里，就是没泳池、面积又是 0 的房子的价格。",", represents the average expected price difference in houses of the same size (in":"，表示同样大小（用",") if they do or do not have a pool. In other words, we expect, on average, houses of the same size to cost":"）的房子，有没有泳池时平均差多少。换句话说，同样大的房子，有泳池大约要贵","more if they have a pool than if they do not.":"。",", represents the average expected price difference in housing price for houses that have the same value of":"，表示","but differ in size by one square-foot.":"相同、面积只差 1 平方英尺时，房价平均差多少。","Note that, regardless of whether or not a house has a pool (":"注意，不管房子有泳池（",") or not (":"）还是没有（","), we assume the":"），我默认","slope for":"斜率，对应",". To visualize this, we show two lines above. This isn't always a valid assumption to make, and when we think those subpopulations should have different slopes, we can use interaction terms.":"。图上那两条线就是在画这件事。这个假设并不总成立；要是我觉得两群人该有不同的斜率，就可以上交互项。","Logistic Regression":"逻辑回归","Temperature (Degrees Fahrenheit)":"温度（华氏度）","Regression and Other Stories":"《回归与其他故事》","(Andrew Gelman, Jennifer Hill, and Aki Vehtari, 2020)":"","An Introduction to Statistical Learning":"《统计学习导论》","(Gareth James, Daniela Witten, Trevor Hastie, and Robert Tibshirani, 2021)":"","Regression for Classification":"用来分类的回归","Erin Bugbee":"",", August 2022":"","One major area in machine learning is supervised learning, where the goal is to predict an output given some inputs. The output value may be a numerical or categorical variable. In this article, we will discuss":"监督学习里，我常要拿输入去猜一个输出。输出可以是数字，也可以是类别。这篇我想写","logistic regression":"逻辑回归",": a supervised learning algorithm that can be used to classify data into categories, or classes, by predicting the probability that an observation falls into a particular class based on its features.":"：用特征去估一条样本落进某类的概率，再拿这个概率来分类。","Though it can be extended to more than two categories, logistic regression is often used for binary classification, i.e. determining which of two groups a data point belongs to, or whether an event will occur or not. In this article, we will focus on binary logistic regression.":"它也能处理两类以上，不过我更常拿它做二分类：判断样本属于两堆里的哪一堆，或者一件事会不会发生。这篇只写二元逻辑回归。","The typical setup for logistic regression is as follows: there is an outcome":"逻辑回归通常长这样：有一个结果","that falls into one of two categories (say 0 or 1), and the following equation is used to estimate the probability that":"，只落在两类里（比如 0 或 1）。下面这个式子用来估","belongs to a particular category given inputs":"属于某一类的概率，输入是","If you have ever encountered linear regression, the equation for":"如果你见过线性回归，","likely looks familiar. This is called a linear predictor, and it is transformed by the sigmoid function so that the values fall between 0 and 1, and can therefore be interpreted as probabilities. This resulting probability is then compared to a threshold to predict a class for":" 这个式子大概眼熟。它叫线性预测，再被 sigmoid 压到 0 和 1 之间，才能当概率读。拿这个概率去跟阈值比，就能给","based on":"分类，依据是","Estimating Coefficients":"怎么估系数","How do we find the coefficients (":"怎么找到让损失最小的那些系数（","\" )}) that minimize the loss function? There are two main approaches for logistic regression: gradient descent and maximum likelihood estimation. We’ll briefly discuss both here.":"\" )}) ？逻辑回归常用两种办法：梯度下降，还有最大似然。我先各说一点。","Gradient Descent":"梯度下降","A common way to estimate coefficients is to use gradient descent. In gradient descent, the goal is to minimize the Log-Loss cost function over all samples. This method involves selecting initial parameter values, and then updating them incrementally by moving them in the direction that decreases the loss. At each iteration, the parameter value is updated by the gradient, scaled by the step size (otherwise known as the learning rate). The gradient is the vector encompassing the direction and rate of the fastest increase of a function, which can be calculated using partial derivatives. The parameters are updated in the opposite direction of the gradient by the step size in an attempt to find the parameter values that minimize the Log-Loss.":"估系数，常用梯度下降。目标是把所有样本上的对数损失压下去。先随便给一组参数，再一点点往损失变小的方向挪。每一步都按梯度更新，再乘上步长（也叫学习率）。梯度指向函数升得最快的方向和快慢，可以用偏导数算出来。参数则朝梯度的反方向走，好去找让对数损失最小的那组值。","Maximum Likelihood Estimation":"最大似然","Another approach is finding the model that maximizes the likelihood of observing the data by using Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE). It turns out, minimizing the Log-Loss is equivalent to maximizing the Log-Likelihood. Therefore, the goal is to find the parameter values that maximize the following:":"另一条路是最大似然（MLE）：找让「看见眼前这份数据」最说得通的模型。巧的是，压低对数损失，就等于抬高对数似然。所以我要找的，是让下面这个式子最大的参数：","We can do so by differentiating the Log-Likelihood with respect to the parameters, setting the derivatives equal to 0, and solving the equation to find the estimates of the parameters.":"我对对数似然求导，令导数为 0，再解出参数。","Interpreting A Logistic Regression Model With One Continuous Feature":"一个连续特征时，怎么读模型","Model Form:":"模型：","`, false )} where":"`, false )} 其中",": This model describes the probability of a sunny day across temperatures (measured in degrees Fahrenheit).":"：这个模型说的是，不同气温（华氏度）下，那天是晴天的概率。","The weight":"权重","per unit change in":"每变化一单位",". When exponentiating the weight, this becomes the change in the odds ratio for":"。把权重取指数，就变成优势比的变化，对应",". In other words, the odds are multiplied by":"。换句话说，优势会乘上","}`, false )}. Therefore, for a coefficient of 0.7, the exponentiated value is":"}`, false )}。所以系数是 0.7 时，取指数得到","= 2.01`, false )}, which indicates that on average, for an increase of 1 degree in temperature, the odds that the day will be sunny multiplies by 2.01 (i.e. approximately doubles).":"= 2.01`, false )}，意思是气温每升 1 度，晴天的优势大约乘 2.01（差不多翻倍）。","The intercept":"截距","}}`, false )}.":"}}`, false )}。","If the intercept coefficient is 2, this means that there is a":"如果截距是 2，那就表示有","} = 0.88`, false )} probability that it will be a sunny day at 0 degrees Fahrenheit.":"} = 0.88`, false )} 的概率：华氏 0 度时会是晴天。","Error (Log-Loss)":"误差（Log-Loss）","To Infinity":"无穷","Predicted Probability":"预测概率","Thanks for reading. We hope that the article is insightful no matter where you are along your Machine Learning journey, and that you came away with a better understanding of how logistic regression can be used for classification.":"今天先写到这里。想接着看可以回画廊。","Evaluating Our Model":"怎么评价模型","When fitting our model, the goal is to find the parameters that optimize a function that defines how well the model is performing. Put simply, the goal is to make predictions as close to 1 when the outcome is 1 and as close to 0 when the outcome is 0. In machine learning, the function to be optimized is called the loss function or cost function. We use the loss function to determine how well our model fits the data.":"拟合的时候，我想找到一组参数，让某个「好不好」的函数尽量好看。说白了：结果是 1，预测就尽量靠近 1；结果是 0，就尽量靠近 0。机器学习里，这个函数叫损失函数，也叫代价函数。我靠它判断模型贴不贴数据。","A suitable loss function in logistic regression is called the Log-Loss, or binary cross-entropy. This function is:":"逻辑回归里合适的损失叫对数损失，也就是二元交叉熵。式子是：","is the number of samples, indexed by":"是样本个数，下标是","is the true class for the index":"是这个下标的真实类别","is the model prediction for the index":"是这个下标的模型预测",". Minimizing the Log-Loss is equivalent to maximizing the Log-Likelihood, since the Log-Loss is the negative of the Log-Likelihood.":"。压低对数损失，就等于抬高对数似然，因为对数损失就是对数似然加了个负号。","This graph shows how the Log-Loss depends on the true value for":"这张图看的是对数损失怎么跟着真实值","and the predicted probability. You can see how as the probability gets closer to the true value (":"和预测概率一起变。概率越靠近真实值（","), the Log-Loss decreases to 0. As the probability gets further from the true value, the Log-Loss approaches infinity.":"），对数损失就越掉向 0。离真实值越远，对数损失就越冲向无穷。","Select true value for":"选真实值","Probability:":"概率：","How It Works":"先看它怎么走","Let’s make this a bit more concrete by walking through an example. Suppose that you want to go for a hike in Seattle. You want to predict whether it will be sunny or rainy, so that you can decide whether to hike or drink coffee indoors at a local cafe. You know that it rains often in Seattle, but you’ve heard the summers have nice weather. The question is: can we predict the weather, given factors such as the temperature?":"我想得更具体一点。假设我在西雅图想去爬山。我想先猜那天是晴还是雨，好决定是出门，还是躲进咖啡馆喝一杯。西雅图很爱下雨，不过听说夏天还不错。问题是：看着气温这类因素，我能猜天气吗？","Assume there are two classes: Rainy Days and Sunny Days. We can assign a numeric value of 0 and 1 to each class, say 0 to a Rainy Day and 1 to a Sunny Day. We have one continuous feature: the temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit. For each day, we can plot this value along with the corresponding temperature.":"先假定两类：雨天和晴天。我给它们标成数字，比如雨天是 0，晴天是 1。手头只有一个连续特征：气温，华氏度。每一天，我都可以把天气和气温点在图上。","Clearly, we should not fit a linear regression model to these data. The outcomes of a linear regression model can take any numerical value, but these data can only take on outcomes of 0 or 1, so the predictions of a linear model may not be meaningful.":"这些点显然不该硬套线性回归。线性回归的输出可以是任何数，而这些点只能是 0 或 1，直线猜出来的数字未必有意义。","Instead, we can fit a logistic function to the data. The values of this function can be interpreted as probabilities, as the values range between 0 and 1. We can interpret the line as the probability of a sunny day given a particular temperature.":"所以我改拟合一条逻辑函数。它的值落在 0 和 1 之间，可以当概率读。这条线，就是给定气温时「今天是晴天」的概率。","Now that we have the logistic function to predict the probabilities of each outcome, we can predict the class. We use a decision boundary to decide the predicted class based on the probability of each class given the feature values. A typical threshold for the decision boundary is 0.5, where we predict an outcome will occur if the probability of that outcome is greater than 0.5. This boundary can be adjusted — for example, if you really dislike the rain, you may want to set the boundary higher to be more cautious, so that that you predict a sunny day and go hiking only if the probability of a sunny day exceeds that boundary.":"有了概率，就能猜类别。我用一条决策边界，按特征算出来的概率来拍板。常见阈值是 0.5：概率大于 0.5 就猜「会发生」。边界也能挪——要是我特别讨厌下雨，可以把边界抬高一点，更谨慎：只有晴天概率跨过那条线，我才猜晴天、去爬山。","To see how the model works for yourself, drag the values to make a prediction!":"自己拖一下，看模型怎么猜！","Temperature:":"温度：","Degrees Fahrenheit":"华氏度","Decision Boundary:":"分界：","The prediction is a":"预测是","Because the gradient calculates where the function is increasing, going in the opposite direction leads us to the minimum of our function. In this manner, we can repeatedly update our model's coefficients such that we eventually reach the minimum of our error function and obtain a sigmoid curve that fits our data well.":"梯度指向函数在升高的方向，所以反着走，就会走向最低点。我就这样反复改系数，慢慢摸到误差函数的底，画出一条贴着数据的 sigmoid。","Let's see how gradient descent works for our logistic regression model. We'll use the algorithm to identify which values for bias":"来看看梯度下降在逻辑回归里怎么走。我用它来找偏置",")\", false )} and weight":")\", false )} 和权重","Our Model:":"我的模型：","Interpreting A Multivariate Logistic Regression Model":"多个特征时，怎么读模型",": Typically, a logistic regression model will contain more than one feature. We call this a":"：逻辑回归通常不止一个特征。我把它叫做","multivariate logistic regression model":"多元逻辑回归",". In this example, we model the probability of a sunny day as a function of temperature and whether or not it is foggy.":"。这个例子里，晴天的概率同时看气温，以及那天有没有雾。","The intercept represents the predicted probability of a sunny day for Days with all":"截距表示所有特征都为",": it represents the probability for days without fog and with a temperature of zero degrees Fahrenheit.":"：也就是没雾、气温是华氏 0 度时，晴天的概率。","Interpreting Logistic Regression Models":"怎么读逻辑回归","Interpreting the coefficients of a logistic regression model can be tricky because the coefficients in a logistic regression are on the log-odds scale. This means the interpretations are different than in linear regression.":"逻辑回归的系数不太好读，因为它们停在对数优势（log-odds）这个尺度上。读法和线性回归不一样。","To understand log-odds, we must first understand odds. Odds are calculated as":"想懂对数优势，得先懂优势。优势这样算：","\")}. This gives the ratio of the probability of a sunny day to that of a rainy day. Say that the probability of a sunny day is 0.75. This implies that the probability of a rainy day is 0.25. The odds would then be":"\")}。它是晴天概率比上雨天概率。假如晴天概率是 0.75，雨天就是 0.25。优势就是","= 3\")}, which means that the odds of a sunny day are 3 to 1. If the probability of rain is 0.5, then the odds would be":"= 3\")}，也就是晴天对雨天是 3 比 1。要是下雨的概率是 0.5，优势就是","= 1\" )}, meaning that the odds of a sunny day are 1 to 1, so sun and rain are equally likely. Taking the log of the odds yields the log-odds, and taking ratios of log-odds yields log-odds ratios.":"= 1\" )}，晴雨五五开。对优势取对数，就得到对数优势；再拿对数优势去比，就是对数优势比。","Now that we understand log-odds a bit more, we can discuss how to interpret the models. Since the coefficients are on the log-odds scale, we can transform them to the odds scale by exponentiating so that they are easier to interpret.":"对数优势有点感觉了，再来说怎么读模型。系数在对数优势尺度上，我先取个指数，变回优势尺度，读起来会轻松一点。","Interpreting A Logistic Regression Model With One Binary Feature":"一个二元特征时，怎么读模型",": This model summarizes the difference in the probability of a sunny day for days that are foggy and days that are not.":"：这个模型概括的是，有雾的日子和没雾的日子，晴天概率差多少。","= 0.50`, false )}, which indicates that on average, the odds that the day will be sunny multiplies by 0.50 (i.e. is about half) if it is foggy compared to if it is not.":"= 0.50`, false )}，意思是有雾时，晴天的优势大约乘 0.50（差不多减半）。","Run Model":"跑模型","Epochs: 1,000":"轮数：1,000","# Hidden Layers:":"隐藏层数：","Sigmoid (logistic)":"Sigmoid（logistic）","Squashes input to (0, 1).":"把输入压到 (0, 1)。","Hyperbolic Tangent (tanh)":"双曲正切（tanh）","Squashes input to (-1, 1).":"把输入压到 (-1, 1)。","Rectified Linear Unit (ReLu)":"ReLU（修正线性）","0, \\\\text":"0, \\\\text","Only keep positive values.":"只留下正数。","Step Function (Perceptron)":"阶跃函数（感知机）","+1, \\\\text":"+1, \\\\text","Returns only -1 or 1 (neuron fires or doesn't fire).":"只吐出 -1 或 1（神经元响或不响）。","Thanks For Reading":"写到这里","Thanks for reading! We hope that the article is insightful no matter where you are along your machine learning journey, and that you came away with a better understanding of Neural Networks.":"今天先写到这里。不管你学到哪一步，希望这篇能帮你把神经网络看得更清楚一点。","Go forth and learn!":"接着学就好。","Regression And Other Stories":"《回归与其他故事》","Elements of Statistical Learning":"《统计学习基础》","Mathematical Statistics and Data Analysis":"《数理统计与数据分析》","Neural Networks":"神经网络",", May 2023":"，2023 年 5 月","A Visual Introduction":"图解入门","Neural networks have revolutionized the field of artificial intelligence and are the backbone of popular algorithms today, such as ChatGPT, Stable-Diffusion, and many others. In this visual introduction, we'll journey through the fundamentals of feed-forward neural networks, starting from their essential components, understanding their learning mechanisms, and even getting hands-on experience by interacting with one ourselves.":"神经网络把人工智能掀起来了，ChatGPT、Stable Diffusion 这些热闹东西，背后都靠它。这篇我想从图上走进前馈网络：零件是什么、它怎么学，最后还能亲手拧一拧。","What Is A Network":"什么是网络","Neural networks are networks - that much is clear.":"神经网络是网络——这倒是明摆着。","But what is a \"network\"?":"可「网络」又是什么？","A network is a structure consisting of interconnected computational nodes, or 'neurons', arranged in layers. These nodes perform mathematical operations on input data, learning some underlying patterns in the data, before producing some output based on those patterns. If this sounds confusing, don't worry, we'll make it clear soon enough.":"所谓网络，就是一层层连起来的计算节点，也叫「神经元」。它们对输入做运算，摸出数据里的规律，再据此吐出结果。听着绕的话别急，我马上画给你看。","To build up our intuition, we'll walk through constructing a basic computational graph into a Neural Network:":"为了把感觉建立起来，我先从一张最简单的计算图，一步步搭成神经网络：","add(layer)} class=\"button-plus\" >+":"add(layer)} class=\"button-plus\" >+","subtract(layer)} class=\"button-minus\" >-":"subtract(layer)} class=\"button-minus\" >-","2 ? colorScale(d.y) : colorScale(1)} stroke-width=":"2 ? colorScale(d.y) : colorScale(1)} stroke-width=","in:draw=":"in:draw=","} out:draw=":"} out:draw=","Going Forward: Other Neural Network Architectures":"再往前：别的网络结构","Up to this point, we've described a specific neural network architecture where values flow forward linearly through a network, and gradients flow linearly backwards through a network. These are often referred to as":"写到这里，我讲的是一种特定结构：数值往前直线流，梯度往后直线流。人们常叫它","feed forward neural networks":"前馈神经网络","artificial neural networks":"人工神经网络","). You may even see them referred to as Multilayer Perceptrons, a callback to the early Neural Network compositions of Perceptrons":"）。你可能还会见到多层感知机这个名字，那是早期用感知机一层层叠起来的叫法","However, this is just the tip of the iceberg when it comes to the field of neural networks. While feed forward neural networks have been incredibly successful in a wide range of applications, many other types of neural network architectures exist that can be used to solve different types of problems. To get you started on your journey into the world of neural network's, and to up your buzzword game in today's world of AI obsession, the most popular architectures are listed and briefly explained below.":"不过这只是神经网络这座冰山露出的一小角。前馈网络确实能干很多事，但还有别的结构，专门对付别的问题。想在如今满天飞的 AI 词里不迷路，我把最常见的几种记在下面，各说一句。","Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs):":"循环神经网络（RNN）：","Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) differ from feed-forward neural networks as they have a built-in memory, allowing them to process sequences of data. This makes RNNs well-suited for tasks like natural language processing and time series prediction. They can learn patterns in sequences by connecting the output from one time step to the input of the next, remembering previous information (the":"循环神经网络跟前馈网络不一样，它自带一点记忆，所以能处理序列。自然语言、时间序列这类题，它就对路。做法是把这一拍的输出接到下一拍的输入，把前面的信息记住（名字里的","in the namesake).":"循环，说的就是这件事）。","Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs):":"卷积神经网络（CNN）：","Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are specifically designed for processing spatial data, such as images. Unlike feed-forward networks, CNNs use special convolutional layers to scan and identify local patterns within the input. Imagine a grid sliding across an image, identifying patterns. This makes them more efficient for image recognition, object detection, and other computer vision tasks, where spatial information is crucial. They are also used for sequential tasks, such as time-series applications.":"卷积神经网络是专门对付空间数据的，比如图像。跟前馈网络不同，它用卷积层在输入上扫过去，捉局部的花样。可以想象一块小格子在图上滑，边滑边认。所以做识别、检测这类看图的活，它更省力，空间关系也更重要。序列、时间序列里也会用到它。","Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs):":"生成对抗网络（GAN）：","Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) consist of two distinct neural networks, a generator and a discriminator, that compete against each other. The generator tries to create a data sample, and the discriminator tries to determine if that data sample came from the training data or the generator. By optimizing against each other, GANs learn to generate new data samples by capturing the distribution of the training data. They are widely used for tasks such as image synthesis, style transfer, and data augmentation.":"生成对抗网络里有两个网络在对着干：生成器和判别器。生成器想造出一份样本，判别器则要判断这份样本是训练数据里的，还是生成器编的。两边互相较劲，GAN 就能摸到训练数据的分布，从而造出新样本。图像合成、风格迁移、数据增强，都常请它出场。","Graph Neural Networks:":"图神经网络：","Graph Neural Networks are a type of neural network that operate on graph-structured data, which is not easily handled by feed-forward networks. They are designed to learn and encode the relationships between nodes in a graph, making them useful for tasks such as social network analysis, molecular property prediction, and recommendation systems.":"图神经网络跑在图结构的数据上，这种东西前馈网络不好啃。它要学的是节点之间怎么连、什么关系，所以社交网络分析、分子性质预测、推荐系统这类题会用到它。","Transformer Architectures:":"Transformer 结构：","Transformer architectures differ from feed-forward networks as they rely on a self-attention mechanism to process input data, allowing them to handle long-range dependencies more effectively. These models are massive, and actually incorporate feed forward neural networks in parts of their architecture. They have been especially successful in natural language processing tasks, such as machine translation and text summarization, due to their ability to capture contextual information across large sequences. Most of the currently hyped AI models, such as the GPT family, are variants of transformers.":"Transformer 跟前馈网络不一样，它靠自注意力来看输入，远距离的依赖更能抓住。模型往往很大，结构里其实还嵌着前馈网络。机器翻译、文本摘要这类自然语言任务特别吃香，因为它能在很长的序列里抓住上下文。眼下最热闹的那些模型，比如 GPT 一家，多半都是 Transformer 的变体。","This is not an exhaustive list of network architectures. Rather, these are high-level architectures with many variants - more are developed every day! But it's a great starting point to send you forward on your journey into neural networks and deep learning.":"这当然不是全部。每种下面还有好多变体，几乎每天都有新的。但当起点够用了，够我继续往神经网络和深度学习里走。","0 ? `translate($":"0 ? `translate($",")` : `translate($":")` : `translate($","0 ? nodeHeight * nodeBoost : nodeHeight} />":"0 ? nodeHeight * nodeBoost : nodeHeight} />","0 ? nodeHeight * nodeBoost - 8 : nodeHeight / 2} >":"0 ? nodeHeight * nodeBoost - 8 : nodeHeight / 2} >","Artifical Neuron":"人工神经元","*For simplicity, bias terms are not shown.":"*为了好看，偏置项没画出来。","toggle()}>":"toggle()}>","Select Text Difficulty:":"选一段难度：","handleOptionClick(\"Intro\")} class:selected=":"handleOptionClick(\"Intro\")} class:selected=","handleOptionClick(\"Experienced\")} class:selected=":"handleOptionClick(\"Experienced\")} class:selected=",">Experienced":">进阶","handleOptionClick(\"Expert\")} class:selected=":"handleOptionClick(\"Expert\")} class:selected=","Classification Task":"分类任务","As you can see, neural networks are not so complicated! They are just computational graphs, channeling inputs through successive layers of computation to generate outputs. This process of inference, whereby inputs are fed through the network to produce output predictions, is called the":"看，神经网络没那么玄。它们就是计算图，把输入一层层送过去，算出输出。这种把输入喂进去得到预测的过程，叫","forward pass":"前向传播",". Let's talk more about those layers of computation, the activation functions.":"。接下来我想多写写这些计算层，也就是激活函数。","Activation Functions":"激活函数","Activation functions are at the heart of artificial neurons in a neural network. These crucial components introduce non-linearity into the model, transforming the weighted inputs to generate an output.":"激活函数是人工神经元的心脏。它们给模型注入非线性，把加权后的输入变成输出。","Simply put, an activation function decides how much signal to pass onto the next layer based on the input it receives.":"说白了，它根据收到的输入，决定要把多少信号传给下一层。","This idea of chaining many weighted signals together is what allows neural networks to learn very complex relationships.":"把许多加权信号串起来，神经网络才能学到很弯的关系。","The non-linear nature of these functions is essential for neural networks to learn from complex data. If we only used linear activation functions, no matter how many layers we stacked, the network would behave just like a single-layer perceptron because the composition of linear functions is still a linear function. This limits the complexity of tasks the network can learn. Non-linear activation functions, on the other hand, enable the network to learn complex patterns and solve intricate problems by adding layers of abstraction.":"这些函数必须是非线性的，网络才能从复杂数据里学东西。要是我只用线性激活，叠再多层也只是一层感知机——线性套线性还是线性。任务能有多弯，就被卡住了。非线性激活则能一层层抽象，让网络去捉弯弯曲曲的规律。","There's a diverse range of activation functions utilized in neural networks, each with its unique benefits and applications. Here are four popular activation functions:":"激活函数有一长串，各有各的好处和用法。我先记下四个常见的：","The sigmoid":"Sigmoid","(or logistic) function, which ranges from 0 to 1, is particularly useful in the output layer of binary classification models, representing the probability of a binary event. However, it can suffer from the vanishing gradients problem during backpropagation.":"（也叫 logistic）从 0 到 1，特别适合二分类模型的输出层，当成某件事发生的概率。不过反向传播时它容易梯度消失。","The hyperbolic tangent":"双曲正切","(or 'tanh') function, which ranges from -1 to 1, provides a zero-centered output designed to make learning for the next layer easier. Yet, like sigmoid, it also faces the vanishing gradients issue.":"（也叫 tanh）从 -1 到 1，输出以 0 为中心，下一层会好学一点。可它跟 sigmoid 一样，也会梯度消失。","The Rectified Linear Unit":"修正线性单元","(or 'ReLU') function is a popular choice in hidden layers due to its efficiency. It activates a node if its input is positive, otherwise, it outputs zero. This simplicity reduces computational cost and mitigates the vanishing gradients problem, but it can lead to dead neurons where some neurons never activate.":"（也叫 ReLU）隐藏层里很常见，因为算得快。输入是正数就原样放出，否则吐 0。这么简单，计算量小，也缓解了梯度消失，但可能出现死神经元——有的神经元从此再也不亮。","Despite their simplicity, chaining these functions together in a neural network can have magical results. That said, it's crucial to remember that there's no one-size-fits-all solution when it comes to choosing activation functions. The best choice often depends on the specific characteristics of the problem at hand, the nature of the input and output data, and the architecture of the network. Thus, understanding these functions and their implications is key to building effective and efficient neural networks.":"函数本身很素，串进网络里却常常出奇效。不过选激活没有万能答案。最好的选择往往取决于题目、输入输出长什么样、以及网络结构。把这些函数摸熟，才好搭出又稳又快的网络。","Add Point":"加点","Model Accuracy":"模型准确率","Accuracy (%)":"准确率（%）","setData(data, i)} on:keydown=":"setData(data, i)} on:keydown=","Backpropagation: How Networks Learn":"反向传播：网络怎么学","Now that we've grasped the concept of activation functions and their role in neural networks, it's time to understand how these networks learn. The magic behind this learning process is a technique known as backpropagation.":"激活函数是干什么的，我大概摸清了。接下来想搞懂网络怎么学。这套把戏叫反向传播。","Backpropagation is an algorithm used during the training of neural networks.":"反向传播是训练神经网络时用的算法。","The goal of backpropagation is to update the weights so that the Neural Network makes better predictions.":"它的目标很简单：把权重改好，让神经网络预测得更准。","Specifically, backpropagation will calculate the gradient of the loss function with respect to the weights of the network, updating the weights layer-by-layer to minimize the network's prediction error.":"具体来说，它会算损失函数对每个权重的梯度，再一层层改权重，把预测误差压下去。","To make the process as clear as possible, let's visualize it step-by-step.":"为了看清楚，我按步骤画一遍。","Forward Pass":"前向传播","During the forward pass, input data is fed through a neural network's layers to produce a prediction. This process involves calculating the weighted sums and applying activation functions for each neuron in each layer.":"前向传播时，输入从一层流到另一层，得到预测。每一层的每个神经元都要算加权和，再过激活函数。","We can see that the classification boundary for our current network (the two-colored background region) isn't great.":"看得出来，当前这个网络的分类边界（那片两色背景）并不怎么样。","animation0()} >Replay Animation":"animation0()} >重播动画","The error, also known as loss, measures the difference between the neural network's predicted output and the actual target values. By minimizing this error, the neural network learns to improve its predictions during training.":"误差，也叫损失，衡量网络的预测和真实目标差多远。把这个差压小，网络才能在训练中越猜越准。","Notice those extra big circles in the chart, the ones that are colored differently than their background? Those are the values that were falsely predicted. The goal of backpropagation is to update the model's weights so that it learns to predict with less error.":"看见图里那些特别大的圆吗？颜色还跟背景对不上。那就是猜错的点。反向传播要改的，就是权重，让以后少错一点。","animation1()} >Replay Animation":"animation1()} >重播动画","Backward Pass":"反向过程","In the backward pass, the error is propagated back through the network, starting from the output layer, to adjust the weights and biases of each neuron. This weight adjustment process, guided by the gradients of the error with respect to the weights, aims to minimize the overall error of the network. (This process uses the Chain Rule from calculus to update the weights layer-by-layer, but we won't go into detail here.)":"误差从输出层往回走，去调每个神经元的权重和偏置。怎么调，看的是误差对权重的梯度，为的是把整体误差压下去。（这里会用到微积分里的链式法则，一层层改权重，细节我先不展开。）","In our example, the width of the lines connecting each neuron represent the weights of the network. The weights will be adjusted to reduce the prediction error of our Neural network.":"在这个例子里，连着神经元的线宽就是权重。这些线会被调细或调粗，好把预测误差降下来。","animation2()} >Replay Animation":"animation2()} >重播动画","Predictions flow forward through the network in the forward pass, and errors flow backwards through the network, adjusting weights along the way.":"预测顺着网络往前走，误差顺着网络往回走，一路改权重。","This process is know as Backpropagation.":"这套流程就叫反向传播。","Backpropagation is a supervised learning algorithm used in neural networks that optimizes their weights and biases through iterative forward and backward passes. By computing the gradients of the error with respect to the weights, backpropagation enables the network to learn and improve its predictions.":"反向传播是一种监督学习算法：它靠一轮轮前向、反向，把神经网络的权重和偏置拧到更好的位置。算出误差对权重的梯度，网络才能学，预测才能进步。","restartAnimation3()} >Replay Animation":"restartAnimation3()} >重播动画","More BackPropagation...":"反向传播还会再来……","Backpropagation doesn't occur just once! For a typical neural network, backpropagation is repeated hundreds, if not thousands, of times. To keep things simple, we animate only a couple more runs of backprop here.":"反向传播不是只跑一次！一般的网络会重复成百上千次。为了好看，这里我只再播两三轮。","The key thing to note is that, at each run, the network's weights are updated in a manner that improves our model's performance!":"关键是：每一轮，权重都会被改成更有利于模型表现的样子！",". To see this directly, you can observe how the classification region (the background colors) correspond correctly to the circles.":"。想眼见为实，看背景色有没有把圆圈圈对就行。","restartAnimation4()} >Replay Animation":"restartAnimation4()} >重播动画","Backprop & Gradient Descent":"反向传播和梯度下降","We just went over backpropagation, the process by which Neural Networks try to find the optimal weights for the given prediction task (optimal here meaning the weights that result in the lowest error value).":"刚才走了一遍反向传播：神经网络试着为眼前的预测任务找到一组合适的权重（合适，就是误差最低的那组）。","It's important to note that this process is not perfect! Many issues may occur when training a neural network, even a network as simple as above. To learn more, continue reading!":"但得说清楚：这过程并不完美！就算上面这么简单的网络，训练时也可能出一堆状况。想看下去的话，接着读就好。","See for yourself":"自己上手试试","As they say, talk is cheap! So let's train our own Neural Network.":"都说空谈便宜。来，亲手训一个神经网络。","Below we show the architecture for a fully-interactive feed-forward Neural Network. By selecting the":"下面是一个可以随便拧的前馈网络。点那些","buttons, you can make the network wider, deeper, or both. On the right-hand side, select from three unique classification datasets for the network to classify. The network's accuracy at each epoch is shown below the network's predictions - hopefully the accuracy goes up each time you train!":"按钮，就能让网络更宽、更深，或两者都要。右边有三份分类数据可选。每个轮次的准确率写在预测下面——希望你每训一次，数字都会往上走！","To train the model, we use batch gradient descent: every item in our training data (the blue and pink circles) is run via a forward pass through the data (⬤), the error is calculated, and the weights are then adjusted via backpropagation (":"训练用的是批量梯度下降：训练数据里每一颗蓝点和粉点都会先做一次前向传播（⬤），算出误差，再靠反向传播去调权重（","). This occurs everytime you click":"）。每次你点","Run 1 Epoch":"跑 1 轮","Even for simple classification problems like these, neural networks can be hard to train! If your network accuracy doesn't improve after a few epochs, try reinitializing the weights by clicking":"就算是这么简单的分类题，神经网络也可能不好训！几轮下去准确率还不走，可以点","Reset Weights":"重置权重",", or try changing the network's architecture.":"，或者改改网络结构。","} disabled=":"} disabled=",">Run 1 Epoch":">跑 1 轮","}>Reset Weights":"}>重置权重","Animation Duration:":"动画时长：","How did you do? For these specific problems, you may have found that wider networks perform better than deeper networks. This is because wider networks can create more complex decision boundaries, which can better capture the intricate patterns in the spiral and moon datasets.":"训得怎么样？在这几道题上，你可能会发现：更宽往往比更深管用。因为宽网络能画出更弯的决策边界，螺旋和月牙这类花样更容易被兜住。","You may also have noticed that the neural network doesn't always, well,":"你也许还发现，神经网络并不总是，嗯，",". This may be occuring for a number of reasons, including inadequate weight initialization, a suboptimal learning rate, or even an insufficient number of training epochs. In some cases, the network architecture itself might not be suitable for the complexity of the data at hand.":"。原因可能有一堆：权重初始化不行、学习率不合适、训练轮数不够。有时结构本身也扛不住数据有多弯。","In any case, the key takeaway here is simple:":"不管怎样，我记下的只有一句：","training neural networks isn't easy!":"训练神经网络并不容易！","Even for small, relatively simple datasets such as those shown here, training a neural network can take some care and finesse.":"就算是这里这么小、这么简单的数据，训起来也得小心、得有手感。","Building Blocks: Computational Graphs":"计算图：先搭积木","A computational graph has an input node where data is fed into the graph, a function node where the input data is processed, and an output node where the result of the computation is produced.":"计算图有一个输入节点把数据喂进去，一个函数节点负责加工，一个输出节点吐出计算结果。","As we can see, data flows in one direction, from inputs to output, through the graph.":"看得出来，数据只朝一个方向流：从输入到输出。","We can represent all sorts of algorithms with as computational graphs. For example, we can easily represent":"好多算法都能画成计算图。比如说，我可以很轻松地把","as a computational graph:":"画成计算图：","To do so, we need only change two things:":"只要改两处：","1. Add weights to each edge connecting the input nodes to the function nodes.":"1. 给连着输入和函数的每条边加上权重。","2. Update our function to represent a linear function of a weighted sum:":"2. 把函数改成加权和的线性式：","And just like that, we have a computational graph for linear regression!":"这样就得到线性回归的计算图了！","Model Outputs":"模型输出","To make the process extra clear, let's visualize the outputs of our linear regression model.":"为了再看清楚一点，我把线性回归的输出画出来。","Just as we'd expect, this model is outputting some predictions (the yellow diagonal line) based on the input data.":"正如所料，模型根据输入画出了一些预测（那条黄色斜线）。","As we build up a more complex model for different applications, we will see this output change to reflect the state of the corresponding model.":"后面模型变复杂、用途变了，这张输出图也会跟着变。","What if our problem changes, and want to switch our model from linear regression to":"要是题目变了，我想把模型从线性回归换成","We can just add another input,and update the function in our computational graph, changing the linear function to a sigmoid function:":"只要再加一个输入，并把计算图里的函数从线性换成 sigmoid：","Now, we can see the model output (the decision region background) reflect our new classification problem and dataset.":"现在模型输出（背景上的决策区域）已经换成了新的分类问题和数据。","Our computational graph setup makes it trivial to model different algorithms. For example, if we want to model a Perceptron instead of logistic regression, we need only switch our logistic function to a step function:":"有了这套计算图，换算法变得很轻松。比如我想建一个感知机而不是逻辑回归，只要把 logistic 函数换成阶跃函数：","Note that the classification region in our model output changed as well, to reflect the new model being represented.":"注意，模型输出里的分类区域也变了，跟新模型对上了。","Activation Functions & Artificial Neurons":"激活函数和人工神经元","In a neural network, this function node we're changing is very special - we call it an":"在神经网络里，我一直在换的这个函数节点很特别——它叫","artificial neuron":"人工神经元","An artificial neuron is a fundamental computational element that receives inputs, performs a weighted operation on these inputs, and passes the result through a function.":"人工神经元是最基本的计算零件：收下输入，做一次加权运算，再把结果送进一个函数。","In neural networks, these functions":"在神经网络里，这些函数","be non-linear, and are referred to as":"必须是非线性的，叫作","activation functions":"激活函数","Nothing is stopping us from chaining multiple artificial neurons together, feeding one to another.":"没有谁规定我不能把多个人工神经元串起来，一个喂给下一个。","This is all a neural network is!":"神经网络，其实就是这么回事！","In fact, the original neural networks were called":"其实最早的神经网络就叫","multilayer perceptrons":"多层感知机","because they were composed of layers of perceptrons (artificial neurons with step functions) feeding one into another!":"因为它们就是一层层感知机（带着阶跃函数的人工神经元）往下喂！","In general, a neural network architecture consists of three layer types:":"一般来说，神经网络的结构就三种层：","input layer":"输入层",": A layer with a node for each network input.":"：每个输入对应一个节点。","hidden layer(s)":"隐藏层",": A layer full of artificial neurons.":"：装满人工神经元的一层。","output layer":"输出层",": A layer representing the network's output.":"：代表网络输出的一层。","There should only be one input and one output layer, but there may be an arbitrary number of hidden layers.":"输入层和输出层各只能有一层，隐藏层则想叠多少叠多少。","No Limits":"没有上限","Designing a neural network architecture is more of an art than a science. The input and output layer will be selected for the specific problem, but the hidden layer is fairly arbitrary.":"设计网络结构更像手艺，不像公式。输入层和输出层由题目决定，隐藏层则相当随性。","Neural networks can be":"神经网络可以很",": having many neurons in a given hidden layer, or":"：某一层里堆很多神经元；也可以",": having many hidden layers in the network. Balancing neuron count optimizes performance; more neurons enable complex learning, at the cost of the risk of overfitting and more computational cost.":"：网络里叠很多隐藏层。神经元多了能学更弯的东西，但过拟合和算力也会跟着涨。","Did you enjoy this content? Then check out our other exciting articles.":"要是这篇还挺有意思，旁边还有别的笔记。","Generative Adversarial Networks (GANS's)":"生成对抗网络（GAN）","Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) are a type of neural network architecture that is used for generative modeling. GANs consist of two networks: a generator network and a discriminator network. The generator network learns to generate samples that resemble the training data, while the discriminator network learns to distinguish between real and fake samples. The two networks are trained simultaneously, with the generator network trying to produce samples that fool the discriminator network, and the discriminator network trying to accurately distinguish":"生成对抗网络是一种用来做生成的结构。它有两个网络：生成器和判别器。生成器学着造出像训练数据的样本，判别器学着分辨真的和假的。两个一起训，生成器想骗过判别器，判别器则想准确分清","Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN's)":"卷积神经网络（CNN）","Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are a type of neural network architecture that has been particularly successful in computer vision tasks. The key difference between CNNs and traditional feedforward neural networks is the presence of convolutional layers, which are specialized layers designed to process images and other types of multidimensional data. In a CNN, the convolutional layers learn to detect and extract features from the input data by sliding a set of filters over the input data and performing convolution operations. The":"卷积神经网络在计算机视觉里特别能打。它跟普通前馈网络最大的不同，是多了卷积层——专门用来处理图像和其它多维数据。卷积层会让一组滤波器在输入上滑过去做卷积，从而检出并抽出特征。这","Attention Mechanisms (Transformers)":"注意力机制（Transformer）","Attention Mechanisms, also known as Transformers, are a type of neural network architecture that is particularly well-suited for natural language processing tasks. The key difference between Attention Mechanisms and traditional feedforward neural networks is the use of attention mechanisms, which allow the network to focus on specific parts of the input data. Attention mechanisms work by assigning a weight to each element of the input data, allowing the network to focus more on":"注意力机制，也就是 Transformer，特别适合自然语言这类任务。它跟普通前馈网络最大的不同，是能把注意力放在输入的某些部分上。做法是给输入的每个元素一个权重，让网络更多地盯住","Recurrent Neural Networks (RNN's)":"循环神经网络（RNN）","Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) are a type of neural network architecture that is particularly well-suited for processing sequential data, such as speech or text. The key difference between RNNs and traditional feedforward neural networks is the presence of recurrent connections, which allow information to persist over time within the network. In an RNN, each neuron receives an input as well as a hidden state from the previous time step, which allows the network to use":"循环神经网络特别适合处理语音、文本这类序列。它跟普通前馈网络最大的不同，是有循环连接，好让信息在时间里留下来。每个神经元既收当前输入，也收上一时刻的隐藏状态，于是网络能用","Precision & Recall":"精确率与召回率","Drag The Line!":"拖这条线！","Diagnosis:":"诊断：","Predicted: Positive":"预测：阳性","Predicted: Negative":"预测：阴性","Actual: Positive":"实际：阳性","Actual: Negative":"实际：阴性","Evaluation: From Precision, Recall and F-Factor to ROC, Informedness, Markedness & Correlation":"评估：从精确率、召回率与 F 因子到 ROC、知情度、标记度与相关","(David Martin Ward Powers, 2008).":"","Classification assessment methods":"分类评估方法","(Michael Keller)":"","Accuracy Is Not Enough":"光看准确率不够",", March 2022":"，2022 年 3 月","The Tradeoff... Again":"权衡，再看一遍","To wrap everything up, let's look again at our cancer dataset again, this time from the lens of the tradeoff between the four different evaluation metrics we've discussed:":"收个尾，我再拿这份癌症数据看一遍，这次盯的是刚才那四个指标之间的拉扯：",". To view this tradeoff explicitly, we'll plot each metric's score as a function of our classification threshold:":"。想把这笔账摊开，我把每项分数画成分类阈值的函数：","Decision Boundary Threshold":"决策边界阈值","The tradeoff between Precision and Recall is alive and well. For our data and classifier, lower classification thresholds yield perfect recall at the cost of low precision. However, as we increase the classification threshold, this relationship changes, and precision will eventually dominate recall. As expected, the F1-Score is maximixed when both Precision and Recall perform well relatively close to each other, and is low otherwise. Accuracy is also its highest at the maximum point for the F1-Score, but note that it barely changes thereafter. It's performance is effectively the same as if it just did majority vote, indicating that accuracy is indeed a poor evaluation metric for the problem at hand.":"精确率和召回率还在互相拉扯。这份数据和这个分类器上，阈值压低，召回率能拉满，精确率就瘪下去。把阈值抬高，关系会翻过来，精确率渐渐压过召回。F1 分数果然在两者都不差、又比较靠近的时候最高，其余时候就矮。准确率也在 F1 最高的地方到顶，但再往后几乎不怎么动。它的表现差不多等于永远投多数类——说明在这个问题上，准确率确实不太中用。","Many machine learning tasks involve":"机器学习里很多任务其实是在做",": the act of predicting a discrete category for some given input. Examples of classifiers include determining whether the item in front of your phone's camera is a hot dog or not (two categories, so":"：给输入贴一个离散的类别。比如手机摄像头前面是不是热狗（两类，所以叫","classification), or predicting whether your Amazon package will arrive early, late, or on time (more than two categories, so":"分类），或者猜包裹会早到、迟到还是准时（超过两类，所以叫","classification).":"分类）。","Evaluating classifiers requires careful consideration. In this article, we'll explore why accuracy isn't always a great measure of classification performance, and discuss three other evaluation metrics often used in its place:":"评估分类器得小心。这篇我想讲清楚：为什么准确率常常不够看，以及我更常拿来替它的三个指标：",". To help qualify the importance of these metrics, we'll make use of the":"。为了把这些指标说清楚，我会用到","confusion matrix":"混淆矩阵",", a simple technique for visualizing the performance of a classification model.":"，一张很简单的表，用来把分类模型的表现摊开看。","Predicted: 1":"预测：1","Predicted: 0":"预测：0","Actual: 1":"实际：1","True Positive (":"真正例（","False Negative (":"假负例（","Actual: 0":"实际：0","False Poisitive (":"假正例（","True Negative (":"真负例（","Instead of looking at the model's raw accuracy (the number of correctly assigned categories divided by te total number of predictions) , the confusion matrix decomposes predictions into several categories of interest, making explicit how one class may be":"不看模型的裸准确率（分对的个数除以总预测数），混淆矩阵会把预测拆成几类，让你看清一个类别怎样被","for another:":"成另一个：","True Positives (TP)":"真正例（TP）",": The number of positive instances correctly classified as positive. E.g., predicting an email as spam when it actually is spam.":"：正例被正确标成正例的个数。比如一封信本来就是垃圾邮件，模型也说它是。","False Positives (FP)":"假正例（FP）",": The number of negative instances incorrectly classified as positive. E.g., predicting an email is spam when it actually is not spam.":"：负例被错标成正例的个数。比如一封信其实不是垃圾邮件，模型却说它是。","True Negatives (TN)":"真负例（TN）",": The number of negative instances correctly classified as negative. E.g., predicting an email is not spam when it actually is not spam.":"：负例被正确标成负例的个数。比如一封信本来就不是垃圾邮件，模型也说不是。","False Negatives (FN)":"假负例（FN）",": The number of positive instances incorrectly classified as negative. E.g., predicting an email is not spam when it actually is spam.":"：正例被错标成负例的个数。比如一封信本来是垃圾邮件，模型却说不是。","The distinction between these four different prediction outcomes is often consequential, so it's very important to have a solid understanding of them!":"这四种结果差很远，得先认清楚。","To motivate our problem, we'll pretend that we've trained a binary classification model to diagnose an individual as having cancer or not. Under the hood, our model will output a probability for each individual of having cancer, and we'll compare this probability to the value of our classification threshold to determine whether or not an individual has cancer or not. The classification threshold is just a value we use to translate our probabilities into binary outputs. It is a decision threshold used to bin values into distinct categories. For example, if our classification threshold is 0.5, we'll classify any patient with a probability greater than 0.5 as being cancer-positive, and any patient with a probability less than 0.5 as being cancer-free:":"先拿一个假想的问题热热身：我训练了一个二分类模型，判断一个人有没有癌症。模型会给每个人打一个得癌的概率，再拿这个概率去跟分类阈值比，决定算阳性还是阴性。分类阈值就是把概率收成两类的那条线。比如阈值是 0.5，概率大于 0.5 的病人标成有癌，小于 0.5 的标成没有：","Because of this tradeoff, it's important to understand the problem that you're trying to solve and any inherent consequences of favoring False Positives over False Negatives (or vice versa).":"正因为有这笔权衡，得先想清楚你在解决什么问题，以及偏爱假正例还是假负例会带来什么后果。","Take our cancer example: designing a model with high recall will identify most people that have cancer (":"拿癌症来说：把召回率做高，多数患者会被找出来（","true positives":"真正例","), saving their lives, but at the cost of misdiagnosing healthy individuals as having cancer (":"），能救命，代价是把健康的人误诊成有癌（","false positives":"假正例","), subjecting them to expensive and dangerous treatments like chemotherapy. On the other hand, designing a model for precision yields confident diagnoses (i.e. someone predicted as having cancer very likely does have cancer), but at the cost of failing to identify everyone who has the disease (false negatives), resulting in potentially fatal consequences for those left undiagnosed. (Because False Negatives result in death, our classification threshold would likely be set to optimize recall over precision).":"），让他们去吃化疗这种又贵又伤身的治疗。反过来，冲着精确率做，报出来的诊断更敢信（被判有癌的人多半真有），代价是漏掉一部分患者（假负例），漏诊可能是要命的。（假负例会死人，所以分类阈值多半会偏向召回，而不是精确率）。","Thus, it's important to understand and decide ahead of time what's more consequential,":"所以要提前想清楚，哪边更伤：","False Positives":"假正例","False Negatives":"假负例",", to investigate how the tradeoff manifests for your particular dataset, and to design your model accordingly.":"，再看看这笔权衡在你这份数据上怎么长，然后按这个来设计模型。","That's Not All":"还没讲完","When it comes to evaluating classifiers (or any type of model), the above metrics are by no means exhaustive. Many other techniques exist, including calibration, popular diagnostic tools (specificity, likelihood ratios, etc.), and expectation frameworks. We cover the closely-related concepts of ROC and AUC in an upcoming article, so look out for that!":"评估分类器（或任何模型），上面这些远远不够。还有校准、诊断工具（特异度、似然比之类）、期望框架等等。ROC 和 AUC 我另外写成一篇，到时候再看。","Additionally, while the concepts we introduced in this article were done so in the context of binary classification, they can all be applied to multiclass settings as well. So give them a try!":"另外，这篇虽然拿二分类当例子，这些想法用到多分类也同样成立。不妨试一试。","Thanks for reading. We hope that the article is insightful no matter where you are along your Machine Learning journey, and that you came away with a better understanding of some of the difficulties of evaluating classification models.":"今天先写到这里。不管你学到哪一步，希望这篇能让你对「分类模型到底好不好」这件事更有数一点。","A special thanks to":"谢谢","for feedback and edits.":"帮我改过稿、提过意见。","F1-Score:":"F1 分数：","The F1-Score":"F1 分数","Given this competing tradeoff, it would be very convenient to have a single performance metric that takes into account":"两边互相拉扯，要是有一个指标能同时顾上","precision and recall. And, given that I wrote that sentence in the first place, you can probably guess that there is. And that it's called the":"精确率和召回率就方便了。我都把这句写出来了，你大概也猜到：还真有，它叫","... Nice.":"……挺好。","The F1-score (also sometimes called the":"F1 分数（有时也叫",") is a single performance metric that takes both precision and recall into account. It's calculated by taking the harmonic mean of the two metrics::":"）把精确率和召回率收进同一个数。算法是对这两个指标取调和平均：","which is equivalent to":"也等于","For this reason, it takes values from zero to one, with values closer to one indicating better performance, and values closer to zero indicating poorer performance. In situations where":"所以它从 0 到 1，靠近 1 更好，靠近 0 更差。只要","precision or recall are poor, the F1-score will also be poor. Only when":"精确率或召回率很差，F1 也会差。只有","precision and recall have good performance will the F1-score be high. To see for yourself, try toggling the various values of precision and/or recall below. Or hover over the corresponding region to see the corresponding F1-score, and the precision and recall values used to obtain it:":"精确率和召回率都还行，F1 才会高。下面可以自己拨精确率、召回率看看。也可以把鼠标放在对应的格子上，看那个 F1，以及算它用到的精确率和召回率：","The F1-score is a great way to compare the performance of multiple classifiers. When choosing between multiple models, all with varying values of precision and/or recall, it may be used to determine which one produces the 'best' results for the problem at hand. For this reason, it's often used in practice as a metric by which to rank models by performance.":"比较好几个分类器时，F1 很方便。精确率、召回率各不相同的模型，可以用它来排一排谁更「好用」。所以实践里常拿 F1 给模型打名次。","That said, the F1-score isn't perfect, and isn't suitable for every classification problem. For one, it completely ignores true negatives in its calculations, so it's misleading for unbalanced classes. (Indeed, this is true for precision and recall as well, so if true negatives are important, consider a different metric such as specificity). The F1-score also gives equal importance to precision and recall. This is sometimes problematic because, as previously discussed, different types of mis-classifications incur different costs, and these costs themselves are tied deeply to the performance of the model.":"不过 F1 也不是万能的，不是每个分类问题都适合。它完全不看真负例，类别不平衡时会骗人。（精确率和召回率其实也一样，要是真负例很重要，可以换特异度这类指标。）F1 还默认精确率和召回率一样重要。这有时会别扭：前面说过，不同类型的分错代价不一样，而这些代价又跟模型表现缠在一起。","ROC & AUC":"ROC 与 AUC","This component is a scope example of a D3 chart. It's pretty simple.":"这是一个 D3 图表示例，很简单。","The F1-score combines the precision and recall of a classifier into a single metric by taking their harmonic mean. It is primarily used to compare the performance of two classifiers. Suppose that classifier A has a higher recall, and classifier B has higher precision. In this case, the F1-scores for both the classifiers can be used to determine which one produces better results. The F1-score of a classification model is calculated as follows:":"F1 分数用调和平均把分类器的精确率和召回率收成一个数。主要用来比较两个分类器。假如分类器 A 召回更高，分类器 B 精确率更高，就可以看两边的 F1，谁更合适。计算方法如下：","Random Forest":"随机森林","The Random Forest Algorithm":"随机森林算法","How the majority vote and well-placed randomness can enhance the decision tree model.":"多数表决，再加上恰到好处的随机，能把决策树变得更稳。","Jenny Yeon":"","# of Trees: 1":"树的数量：1","Tree Accuracy: 60%":"单树准确率：60%","But First: A Theorem From 1785":"先看一条 1785 年的定理","All the way back in the year 1785, Marquis de Condorcet expressed a political science theorem about the relative probability of a given group of individuals arriving at a correct":"早在 1785 年，孔多塞侯爵写过一条政治学定理，讲的是一群人做成","majority":"多数","decision.":"决定的相对概率。","Despite its age, the theorem has been applied to a number of different fields, most notably for us the field of machine learning. To see how, let's start with one model, a decision tree with an accuracy of 60 percent.":"虽然老，这条定理后来进了不少领域，机器学习也算其中之一。先看一个模型：一棵准确率 60% 的决策树。","Condorcet's Jury Theorem":"孔多塞陪审团定理","says that if each person is more than 50% correct, then adding more people to vote increases the probability that the majority is correct.":"说：如果每个人正确率都超过 50%，再加人来投票，多数正确的概率会上升。","True to form, if we ask two more decision trees, each also with 60% accuracy, and decide by the majority vote, then the probability of the vote being right goes up to 65%!":"照这个说法，再请两棵同样 60% 的决策树，三棵多数表决，投对的概率就能到 65%！","The theorem suggests that the probability of the majority vote being correct can go up as we add more and more models. With eleven models, the majority can reach 75% accuracy!":"定理还暗示：模型越加越多，多数表决正确的概率还能再往上。十一棵的时候，多数可以到 75%！","There is a caveat. The accuracy may not improve if each model produces the same prediction, for example. The mistake of one model would not be caught by the other models. Therefore, we would want to cast a majority vote using the models that are different.":"有个前提。要是每棵树给出一模一样的预测，准确率就不会涨。一棵犯的错，别的树也抓不住。所以我希望拿来投票的模型彼此不一样。","We can add more and more models.":"模型可以一棵一棵往上加。","Play with the scrollers yourself!":"滑条可以自己拖！","The top controls the number of trees in the ensemble. The bottom controls each tree's accuracy.":"上面那条管集成里有几棵树。下面那条管每棵树自己的准确率。","In machine learning, this concept of multiple models working together to come to an aggregate prediction is called":"机器学习里，好几个模型一起给出一个汇总预测，这叫","ensemble learning.":"集成学习。","It provides the basis for many important machine learning models, including random forests.":"很多重要模型都从这儿长出来，随机森林也是。","Ensemble Learning":"集成学习","Aggregate":"汇总","Prediction":"预测","Sample 1":"样本 1","Sample 2":"样本 2","Sample 3":"样本 3","Sample N":"样本 N","Prediction 1":"预测 1","Prediction 2":"预测 2","Prediction 3":"预测 3","Prediction N":"预测 N","Ensemble learning creates a stronger model by aggregating the predictions of multiple weak models, such as decision trees (check out our":"集成学习把多个弱模型的预测合在一起，拼出一个更强的模型，决策树就是常见的弱模型（可以先看","previous article on Decision Trees":"上一篇《决策树》","to learn more about some of their limitations). Condorcet’s Jury Theorem suggests that the majority vote aggregation can have better accuracy than the individual models. There are other methods to aggregate predictions, such as weighted majority vote.":"，里面写过它们的一些毛病）。孔多塞陪审团定理说，多数表决的准确率可以高于单棵模型。合预测还有别的办法，比如加权多数表决。","Random Forest is an example of ensemble learning where each model is a decision tree. In the next section, we will build a random forest model to classify if a road sign is a pedestrian crossing sign or not. These signs come in many variations, and we will use four simple features: Size, number of sides, number of colors used, and if the sign has text or symbol.":"随机森林就是一种集成学习，里面每一员都是决策树。下一节我会建一个随机森林，判断路牌是不是人行横道标志。这种牌子花样很多，我只用四个简单特征：大小、边数、用了几种颜色、上面是文字还是符号。","We will start with a sampling method called the":"我先用一种抽样办法，叫","Bagging Method":"Bagging 方法","to create multiple samples from the training data to build each tree.":"，从训练数据里抽出多份样本，每份长一棵树。","One way to produce multiple models that are different is to train each model using a different training set. The":"想让模型彼此不一样，一个办法是每棵树用不同的训练集。","Bagging (Bootstrap Aggregating) method":"Bagging（自助聚合）方法","randomly draws a fixed number of samples from the training set with replacement. This means that a data point can be drawn more than once.":"会有放回地从训练集里抽固定数量的样本。同一个点可以被抽到不止一次。","This results in several training sets that are different. Here, we have three samples. Notice that some data points are shared among some samples. Each sample then can be used to build a model. Let’s build some decision trees with these samples!":"于是会得到好几份彼此不同的训练集。这里有三份。你会看到有些点在几份里重复出现。每份都可以拿来长一个模型。那就用这些样本长几棵决策树吧！","Feature Selection":"特征选择","Here are the features: Size, Number of Sides, Number of Colors Used, and Text or Symbol.":"特征是这些：大小、边数、用了几种颜色、文字还是符号。","For each split of the tree building, we compute the best splitting using only a randomly selected subset of the features. This is another way to ensure that the decision trees are as different as possible.":"树在每一次分裂时，我只从随机抽出的一小撮特征里挑最好的切法。这也是为了让这些决策树尽量长得不一样。","Let's build the first tree with the first sample! For the first split, three features are selected to find the best split.":"先用第一份样本长第一棵树！第一次分裂，抽出三个特征来找最好的切。","The best feature to split is \"Number of Colors used.\"":"最好的切是「用了几种颜色」。","Likewise, for the second split, another set of three features are selected to find the best split. The best feature to split this time is \"Text or Symbol.\"":"第二次分裂同样再抽三个特征来找最好的切。这次最好的是「文字还是符号」。","This process continues until the tree is constructed. The other samples will build other trees using the same process.":"一直这么做，直到这棵树长完。别的样本用同一套办法长出别的树。","Here are trees built from each sample.":"每份样本长出来的树在这里。","Notice how these trees are quite different from each other.":"看看，这些树彼此差得还挺多。","Let's bring some test data to see the random forest in action!":"拿点测试数据来，看随机森林怎么干活！","Each tree produces a prediction.":"每棵树都会给出一个预测。","Can a small circular sign be a crossings sign? Let's ask the three trees!":"一块小小的圆形牌子会是人行横道标志吗？问问这三棵树！","Majority vote":"多数表决","The first tree thinks that the sign is not a crossings sign. However, the other trees voted yes. By the majority vote, the prediction is \"Yes\"!":"第一棵树觉得这不是人行横道标志。另外两棵投了是。多数表决，预测就是「是」！","It's your turn":"轮到你了","Click the remaining data points to see how the random forest makes a prediction.":"点剩下的数据点，看随机森林怎么给出预测。","Did you notice that even though the trees produce the same prediction, the decision path - the reason why each thinks the sign is a crossing sign or not - is different from each other? This is good news: The random forest models perform better when the trees in the forest are different. Let's dive deep into this!":"你有没有发现：就算几棵树给出同一个预测，决策路径——各自觉得它是或不是人行横道标志的理由——彼此并不一样？这是好消息：林子里的树越不一样，随机森林往往越好。下面再往深里看。","Variance in Composition":"组成上的方差","We previously discussed":"前面写过","how decision tree model suffers from high variance.":"决策树模型怎么被高方差折磨。","However, this variance among trees is employed in the random forest as a feature, not a bug. The inventor of the random forest model Leo Breiman says in his paper":"可到了随机森林里，树与树之间的这种方差成了特性，不是缺陷。随机森林的提出者布雷曼在论文里说","\"[o]ur results indicate that better (lower generalization error) random forests have lower correlation between classifiers and higher strength.\"":"「结果表明，更好的（泛化误差更低的）随机森林，分类器之间相关更低、强度更高。」","[Random Forest Article]":"[随机森林论文]","The high variance of the decision tree model can help keep the correlation among trees low. The Bagging Method as well as the Feature Selection are the key innovations to keep correlation low.":"决策树模型的高方差，反而有助于把树与树的相关压低。Bagging 方法和特征选择，正是把相关压低的两处关键。","We've just shown how to construct random forests for a given dataset, but how different are our trees from one another in reality? To find out, we've trained a nine-tree random forest on our sign dataset and plotted it below.":"刚才演示了怎么在一份数据上长出随机森林，可这些树实际上能有多不一样？我在这份路牌数据上训练了一个九棵树的随机森林，画在下面。","The outer enclosing circle represents the random forest. Each inner circle represents a unique decision tree in the forest. Hovering over a tree will highlight the tree's classification accuracy, and its feature importances* for four features.":"最外面那一圈是整座随机森林。里面每一圈是林子里一棵独特的决策树。鼠标放到一棵树上，会标出这棵树的分类准确率，以及四个特征各自的重要性*。","Observe that each tree has a fairly unique combination of feature importances and accuracy and there is no obvious pattern. Some trees only use two out of four features, and the tree with similar feature importances as the random forest model still performs much worse than the forest. As discussed in Condorcet's theorem, the key takeaway is the power these models employ when aggregated together in a smart way, as shown by the random forest model having the highest accuracy.":"每棵树的特征重要性和准确率组合都挺独特，看不出明显的套路。有的树四个特征只用了两个；特征重要性跟整座森林很像的那棵，表现仍然比森林差一截。孔多塞定理想说的就是这件事：这些模型聪明地合在一起才有力，所以随机森林的准确率最高。","As expected, the random forest model (the red dots on the right) performs better than any individual tree. Notice the wide range of the feature importance scores across our trees, which can contribute to low correlation. In the very first random forest visualization tool developed by Breiman, he also attempted to show the range in feature importances.":"果然，随机森林（右边那些红点）比任何单棵树都好。各棵树的特征重要性分数跨度很大，这也有助于相关变低。布雷曼做的第一版随机森林可视化工具，也试过把特征重要性的范围画出来。","Variance in Predictions":"预测上的方差","If each tree produces the same prediction, then the accuracy can not improve. Below, each circle represents a prediction from a model. Circles in the same row share the same model, while circles in the same column share the same test data. Blue means \"Yes\" and pink means \"No\". Solid color means that the prediction is correct, while the strip means that the prediction is incorrect. Not all test data points are shown.":"要是每棵树给出同一个预测，准确率就涨不上去。下面每个圆是一个模型的一次预测。同一行是同一个模型，同一列是同一条测试数据。蓝是「是」，粉是「否」。实心表示预测对了，条纹表示预测错了。测试点没有全部画出来。","The irregular pattern in the grid shows how the trees are different in where they make mistakes. As expected, the random forest model performs the best overall even if there are trees with very low accuracy. Note that for the first data point (first column), there are still three trees with the correct prediction while the majority is incorrect. This inspired people to consider other methods, such as Boosting, which is very popular today.":"格子里不规则的花纹，说明这些树在不同的地方犯错。果然，就算有的树准确率很低，随机森林整体仍然最好。注意第一个数据点（第一列）：多数已经错了，仍有三棵树是对的。这也启发了后来很流行的 Boosting 一类方法。","Conclusion":"写在最后","Random Forest models are a popular model for a large number of tasks. In short, it's a method to produce aggregated predictions using the predictions from several decision trees. The old theorem of Condorcet suggests that the majority vote from several weak models with more than 50% accuracy may do the trick. Later, Breiman came up with Bagging and Feature Selection that helps to keep correlation among the trees low which is a key to success.":"随机森林在很多任务上都常用。一句话：它用好几棵决策树的预测，合成一个汇总预测。孔多塞那条老定理说，好几个正确率过 50% 的弱模型来多数表决，也许就够用。后来布雷曼提出 Bagging 和特征选择，把树与树的相关压低，这才是成功的关键。","There has been some theory built around the bias and variance of random forest model in relation to the trees that it has. Please check out this article on the bias-variance tradeoff to learn more about these concepts.":"随机森林的偏差和方差，跟里面那些树的关系，后来也有一些理论。想把这两个概念看清楚，可以去看偏差–方差权衡那一篇。","textbook.":"。","If you have any comments or ideas related to MLU-Explain articles, feel free to reach out directly to":"对这些笔记有想法，随时跟我说","Notes & Resources":"备注与材料","To make things more concise, some details were left out. Additionally, we went with Brieman's implementation using Scikit-Learn's":"为了写得短，有些细节我略过了。另外我按布雷曼的实现走，用的是 Scikit-Learn 的","RandomForestClassifier.":"RandomForestClassifier。","A special thanks goes out to Brent Werness for his valuable input for this article.":"谢谢帮我看过这篇的人。","Additionally, this article is a product of the following resources + the awesome people who made (& contributed to) them:":"另外，这篇能写成这样，靠下面这些材料和把它们做出来的人：","Random Forests":"随机森林","(Leo Breiman, 2001).":"","RAFT (RAndom Forest Tool)":"RAFT（随机森林工具）","(Leo Breiman; Adele Cutler).":"","Essai sur l'application de l'analyse à la probabilité des décisions rendues à la pluralité des voix":"试论以分析计算多数表决所作决策之概率","(Jean-Antoine-Nicolas de Caritat Condorcet, 1785).":"","Reinforcement Learning":"强化学习","Number of Bananas":"香蕉数量","Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction":"《强化学习导论》","(Richard Sutton & Andrew Barto, 2018)":"","A Gentle Introduction To Learning From Decisions":"从做决定里学：一篇好入口","Anand Kamat":"","In many situations, we learn by interacting with our environment. We observe the current situation, take actions, and observe outcomes, and then reflect on these previous occurrences when we take our next action. The field of psychology has historically studied the human ability to learn from the reinforcement of behaviors, and this influenced the development of an area of machine learning:":"很多时候，我是在跟环境来回打交道里学会的：看一眼现在怎样，动手，看结果，下次再动手时就把上次的事拿出来掂量。心理学一直在研究人怎么靠行为被强化而学会，这也影响了机器学习里的一块：","reinforcement learning":"强化学习",". Reinforcement learning (RL) is an area of machine learning that focuses on how learning from interacting with an environment can occur effectively for intelligent agents.":"。强化学习（RL）关心的是：智能体跟环境互动时，怎样才能有效地学。","The goal of RL problems is to learn which actions to take and when to take them to maximize cumulative reward over time. This differs from supervised learning, as the decision maker is not told what is the correct action in the current situation and instead must learn from the consequences of its own actions. It is also different from unsupervised learning, as the goal is not to find structure in unlabeled data but instead to maximize reward.":"强化学习想解决的是：该做什么、什么时候做，好让时间一长拿到的奖励尽量多。它跟监督学习不一样：没人告诉我这一步的标准答案，只能从自己动作的后果里学。也跟无监督不一样：我不是在没标签的数据里找结构，我是在把奖励堆高。","Choosing Between Two Trees":"两棵树里选一棵","In pursuit of finding bananas, the robot can pick either Tree A or Tree B. Tree A produces on average 3 bananas, with a standard deviation of 1 banana, and Tree B produces on average 6 bananas, with a standard deviation of 2 bananas. The goal is to maximize the number of bananas found, however, the robot does not know which tree produces more bananas. Since the robot does not know which action will lead to greater reward, it must make choices and observe the outcomes directly.":"为了找香蕉，机器人可以选树 A 或树 B。树 A 平均产出 3 根香蕉，标准差 1 根；树 B 平均 6 根，标准差 2 根。目标是香蕉越多越好，可它不知道哪棵更慷慨。既然不知道哪个动作奖励更大，就只能自己选、自己看结果。","This type of reinforcement learning problem resembles that of the":"这类强化学习问题，有点像","multi-armed bandit problem":"多臂老虎机问题",". The multi-armed bandit problem is named after the one-armed bandit slot machine, except it has multiple levers or 'arms'. A player must decide, given limited resources, which levers to pull to have the greatest chance of hitting the jackpot. Here, the trees are the 'arms' and the robot must decide how to choose between them.":"。名字来自那种单臂老虎机，只是这里有很多杆，也叫「臂」。筹码有限，该拉哪根，才最可能中奖？这里，树就是「臂」，机器人得决定怎么在它们之间选。","Observe how the robot makes choices between the two trees and receives the reward. This reward information is stored in what is called a Q-value, which reflects the expected reward for each action based on the history of experienced rewards. In this case, the robot maintains two Q-values: one for Tree A and one for Tree B.":"看它怎么在两棵树之间做选择、拿到奖励。这些奖励会记进所谓的 Q 值：根据已经吃到的香蕉，估计每个动作以后大概能拿多少。这里它维护两个 Q 值：树 A 一个，树 B 一个。","Initially, the robot prefers to select Tree A, having known it yields bananas. However, upon occassionally sampling Tree B, it starts reinforcing its decision to select Tree B more, as it is more fruitful. This phenomenon is discussed in the next section.":"一开始它更爱选树 A，因为已经知道那里有香蕉。可偶尔去尝树 B 之后，它越来越强化「选树 B」这个决定——那边更丰盛。下一节会讲这是怎么回事。","Two Options":"两个选择","Tree A, Tree B":"树 A，树 B","runBanditTrials(5)}>Select 5 Action":"选 5 次","runBanditTrials(10)}>Select 10 Actions":"选 10 次","reset()}>Reset":"重置","Let’s see reinforcement learning in action! We’ll walk through a scenario with a robot learning to find bananas.":"来看强化学习怎么跑！我带一个找香蕉的机器人走一遍。","Navigating in a Grid World":"在格子世界里走","Now the robot is in a commonly used environment in reinforcement learning: the gridworld. The robot can now move left, right, up, and down. Again, the robot’s actions affect the environment by changing its state in the grid.":"现在机器人进了强化学习里很常见的环境：格子世界。它可以左、右、上、下移动。动作还是会改环境：它在格子里的位置变了。","There is an obstacle in the gridworld: a":"格子世界里有个障碍：",". If the robot steps onto the cactus, it will receive a":"。踩上去会拿到","negative reward":"负奖励","and will result in the termination of the episode. Therefore, the robot should learn to avoid the cactus. Instead of learning to move left or right as in the line world, now the robot must learn the path to take.":"，这一局也就结束了。所以它得学会躲开仙人掌。不再只是学往左还是往右，现在要学该走哪条路。","See for yourself how increasing epsilon can change the robot's behavior in this 2-dimensional environment.":"自己拖一下 epsilon，看它在这个二维环境里会不会换脾气。","Grid World":"格子世界","X,Y-Position":"X、Y 位置","Move Left, Move Right, Move Up, Move Down":"左移，右移，上移，下移","Number of Bananas, Cactus":"香蕉数量，仙人掌","Epsilon:":"Epsilon：","simulateEpisode()}>Current Route":"当前路线","runAgentTrials(25, episodicValues)} >Run 25 Episodes":"跑 25 局","runAgentTrials(150, episodicValues)} >Run 150 Episodes":"跑 150 局","getOptimalPolicy()}>Optimal Solution":"最优解","In this article, we introduced some of the major concepts in reinforcement learning. We demonstrated how an agent learns from taking actions and observing rewards in a multi-armed bandit problem, as well as when navigating in multi-dimensional environments.":"这篇里我过了强化学习里几个要紧的概念。智能体怎么在多臂老虎机里靠动作和奖励学习，以及在更高维的环境里怎么走，都试过了。",". Banana icons provided by leo-graph.com from":"。香蕉图标来自 leo-graph.com，出自","Noun Project":"Noun Project",". Cactus icon provided by anam from":"。仙人掌图标出自","Navigating in a Line World":"在一条线上走","Let’s progress to a slightly more complex environment. Now, the robot and trees are located on a line. The robot has to decide which action to take: move left or move right. Each action moves the robot one step in that direction. The main difference from the previous scenario is that the robot’s actions affect its state in the environment, reflected by its location. The robot must now learn how valuable each action is depending on its state. This defines a policy that the robot can follow, by learning which action it should take in each state. This is reflected by the arrow in each state, corresponding to the action with the greater Q-value for that particular state.":"环境再复杂一点点。现在机器人和树都在一条线上。它要决定：往左还是往右。每动一次就走一步。跟上一个场景最大的不同是：动作会改它在环境里的状态，也就是位置。它得学会：在每个状态下，哪个动作更值钱。这会形成一份策略——每个状态该怎么动。每个格子上的箭头，指的就是那个状态下 Q 值更大的动作。","The robot currently follows a policy where it moves to the tree on the left, having previously discovered that the tree yields bananas. Though the tree on the left is closer to the robot's initial position, the right tree actually generates much higher rewards, making it more fruitful. If the robot strictly follows its current policy, it will always move to the left tree. This is called exploitation, as the robot exploits its knowledge when deciding how to pursue rewards. The robot needs to explore in order to reduce the uncertainty about the tree on the right, in order to learn that it actually yields greater rewards. This introduces an important concept in reinforcement learning:":"它现在的策略是往左边那棵树走，因为以前发现那里有香蕉。左边离起点更近，可右边其实奖励高得多，更丰盛。要是死守这份策略，它永远只往左。这叫利用：拿已经知道的去追奖励。它还得去探索，降低对右边那棵树的不确定，才能发现那边其实更赚。强化学习里有个要紧的概念：","the explore-exploit dilemma":"探索–利用困境",". Agents must balance exploiting known rewards with exploring the environment, and potentially discovering more rewarding actions.":"。智能体得在「吃已经知道的奖励」和「去环境里转转、说不定发现更赚的动作」之间找平衡。","Constantly performing the action that the robot believes yields the greatest reward is called being":"一直只做它以为奖励最大的动作，叫",". This does not allow the robot to explore at all, and the robot will never discover that the other tree yields greater rewards. To allow for some exploration, the robot can select actions according to":"。这样它完全不探索，也就永远发现不了另一棵树其实更赚。想留一点探索，可以按","epsilon-greedy policy":"epsilon-贪心策略",". This is a simple way to balance exploration and exploitation, by exploring (i.e. choosing action randomly) with probability epsilon and exploiting by greedily following the policy with probability 1-epsilon. Increasing epsilon increases exploration.":"来选动作。这是在探索和利用之间找平衡的简单办法：以概率 epsilon 去探索（随机挑动作），以概率 1-epsilon 按策略贪心。epsilon 越大，探索越多。","See for yourself how increasing epsilon can change the robot's behavior, leading to updating the Q-values and policy. Setting epsilon to zero will result in a greedy polcy.":"自己拖一下 epsilon，看机器人会不会改行为，Q 值和策略跟着更新。把 epsilon 设成 0，就是纯贪心策略。","Let's observe the explore-exploit dilemma in the following simulation. The robot's initial policy suggests perpetually going left towards the tree that has already been discovered.":"下面这个模拟里，看看探索–利用困境。机器人一开始的策略，是永远往左，走向已经发现的那棵树。","The robot has sampled 15 episodes or trials following the epsilon-greedy policy discussed before. You can see the robot still prefers stepping towards the tree on the left for all the states despite the one on the right producing higher rewards. Let's allow the robot to explore the environment further...":"它已经按前面的 epsilon-贪心策略跑了 15 局。你会看见：每个状态下它仍更爱往左，尽管右边奖励更高。让它再多转转……","The robot has now sampled 2500 episodes following the epsilon-greedy policy. You can observe how the robot has updated its policy to prefer stepping towards the tree on the right instead. The plots indicate moving towards the right is more fruitful in each state.":"它现在按 epsilon-贪心策略跑了 2500 局。策略已经改了：更爱往右。图上也看得出，每个状态下往右都更划算。","Line World":"线性世界","Move Left, Move Right":"左移，右移","reset()} />":"reset()} />","simulateEpisode()}>Simulate Episode":"模拟一局","runAgentTrials(15, episodicValues)} >Run 15 Episodes":"跑 15 局","runAgentTrials(50, episodicValues)} >Run 50 Episodes":"跑 50 局","getOptimalPolicy()}>Optimal Policy":"最优策略","Humans often balance short-term gratification and long-term consequences in their decision-making. We are predisposed to value rewards received now more than those received in the future. This makes sense as the distant future is more uncertain than the present. Similarly, the agent needs to find a balance between acquiring immediate rewards and considering long-term gain. The Q-values for the Line World and the upcoming Grid World environments are estimated to be the expected":"人做决定时，常常要在眼前的甜头和以后的后果之间找平衡。我更愿意看重现在拿到的奖励，而不是以后才到手的。说得通：远的未来比当下更不确定。智能体也一样，既要眼前的香蕉，也得惦记长远。线性世界和后面格子世界里的 Q 值，估的是期望","cummulative rewards. This simply means rewards received later are worth less than those recieved immediately. For more information, refer this":"累计回报。就是后拿到的，没有立刻拿到的值钱。想多看可以翻这本","Let's dive in! -->":"开始看！","Problem Structure":"问题长什么样","In reinforcement learning problems, there is an":"强化学习里，有一个","who makes decisions and learns how to achieve a goal. This agent interacts with the":"负责做决定，学着把目标做成。它会去碰","by taking":"采取",". The environment produces":"。环境会吐出","as a result of the actions taken, and these rewards provide insight into the overall":"——动作的结果。这些奖励能让我看出整体的","of taking an action in a particular state. Actions may affect the environment, leading to changes in":"：在某个状态下这么做值不值得。动作还可能改环境，状态跟着变",", which the agent observes. This process continues with the agent taking actions and observing the resulting reward. The agent uses this information to learn what actions it should take depending on the circumstance. This mapping from states to actions governing the agent's behavior is called a":"，智能体会看见。它就这么边做边看奖励。用这些经验，学会在不同情形下该怎么动。从状态到动作的这份说明书，叫",". The agent also learns a":"。它还会学一个","value function":"价值函数","which it uses to estimate how desirable the states are when behaving according to the policy. The learning in reinforcement learning comes from the agent changing its policy and updating its value function as a result of its experiences in the environment.":"，用来估计：按这份策略走，各个状态有多值得待。强化学习里的「学」，就是智能体靠在环境里的经历，改策略、更新价值函数。","Typically, the agent interacts with the environment over time. At each time step, an action is taken based on the agent’s policy, a reward is obtained, and the next state is observed. The sequence of transitions experienced by the agent starting from the intitial state until the final state is called a trial or an episode.":"智能体通常会跟环境耗一段时间。每个时间步，按策略做一个动作，拿到奖励，看见下一个状态。从初始状态走到终态，这一串转移叫一试，或者一局。","Number of Actions":"动作次数","Receiver-Operating Characteristic Analysis for Evaluating Diagnostic Tests and Predictive Models":"用受试者工作特征分析评估诊断检验与预测模型","(Kelly H. Zou, A. James O’Malley and Laura Mauri, 2007).":"","Using the Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve to analyze a classification model: A final note of historical interest":"用受试者工作特征（ROC）曲线分析分类模型：一则历史补记","(University of Utah, 2017).":"（犹他大学，2017）。","Areas beneath the relative operating characteristics (ROC) and relative operating levels (ROL) curves: Statistical significance and interpretation":"相对工作特征（ROC）曲线与相对工作水平（ROL）曲线下的面积：统计显著性与读法","(S. J. Mason, N. E. Graham, 2002).":"","Cloud & Japanese Plane Icons":"云和日本飞机图标","(iejank & Norbert de Graaff, respectively, from NounProject.com)":"（NounProject.com）","A Visual Explanation of Receiver Operating Characteristic Curves and Area Under the Curve":"受试者工作特征曲线与曲线下面积：一篇看图的说明",", June 2022":"，2022 年 6 月","previous article discussing evaluating classification models":"讲评估分类模型的上一篇",", we discussed the importance of decomposing and understanding your model's outputs (e.g. the consequences of favoring False Positives over False Negatives, or vice versa). Left unmentioned were two closely-related concepts, the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (the":"里，我说过要把模型输出拆开看（比如更在乎假阳性还是假阴性，后果很不一样）。当时没提两个紧挨着的概念：受试者工作特征曲线（","ROC Curve":"ROC 曲线",") and the Area Under the ROC Curve (":"）以及 ROC 曲线下的面积（","ROC curves were first employed during World War 2 to analyze radar signals: After missing the Japanese aircraft that carried out the attack on Pearl Harbor, the US wanted their radar receiver operators to better identify aircraft from signal noise (e.g. clouds). The operator's ability to identify as many true positives as possible while minimizing false positives was named the":"ROC 曲线最早用在二战，拿来分析雷达信号：珍珠港那批日本飞机漏掉以后，美国希望雷达操作员更能从噪声（比如云）里认出飞机。把「尽量抓住真阳性、少报假阳性」这种本事，叫作","Receiver Operating Characteristic":"受试者工作特征",", and the curve analyzing their predictive abilities was called the ROC Curve. Today, ROC curves are used in a number of contexts, including clinical settings (to assess the diagnostic accuracy of a test) and machine learning (the focus of this article).":"，分析这种判断力的曲线就叫 ROC 曲线。今天它出现在很多地方，包括临床（看一项检验准不准），以及机器学习（这篇要写的）。","In machine learning, we use ROC Curves to analyze the predictive power of a classifier: they provide a visual way to observe how changes in our model’s classification thresholds affect our model’s performance. Similar to their original use in the 1940s, the curves allow us to select for classification thresholds that allow our model to identify as many true positives as possible while minimizing false positives.":"机器学习里，我用 ROC 曲线看分类器的预测力：阈值一改，表现怎么变，图上能看出来。跟 1940 年代的用法很像，它可以帮我挑阈值：尽量抓住真阳性，少报假阳性。","In particular, the ROC curve is composed by plotting a model's":"具体来说，ROC 曲线是把模型的","True-Positive Rate (TPR)":"真阳性率（TPR）","versus its":"对上","False-Positive Rate (FPR)":"假阳性率（FPR）","across all possible classification thresholds, where:":"，在所有可能的分类阈值上画出来，其中：","True Positive Rate (TPR)":"真阳性率（TPR）",": The probability that a positive sample is correctly predicted in the positive class. E.g., the percentage of radar signals predicted to be airplanes that actually are airplanes.":"：正样本被正确标成正类的概率。比如，雷达信号被当成飞机、而它真的是飞机的比例。","False Positive Rate (FPR)":"假阳性率（FPR）",": The probability that a negative sample is incorrectly predicted in the positive class. E.g., the percentage of radar signals predicted to be airplanes that actually are":"：负样本被错标成正类的概率。比如，雷达信号被当成飞机、其实并不是","To make the concept as clear as possible, we'll construct an ROC curve for ourselves. Let's pretend that we have our own model to classify radar signals as either airplanes (":"为了看清楚，我自己画一条 ROC。假装我有一个模型，要把雷达信号分成飞机（",") or noise (e.g. clouds":"）或者噪声（比如云","). Items greater than our classification threshold will be predicted to be airplanes, and items less than will be predicted to be noise:":"）。超过分类阈值的当成飞机，低于的当成噪声：","AUC: Area Under the Curve":"AUC：曲线下的面积","AUC (sometimes written AUROC) is just the area underneath the entire ROC curve. Think integration from calculus. AUC provides us with a nice, single measure of performance for our classifiers, independent of the exact classification threshold chosen. This allows us to compare models to each other without even looking at their ROC curves (though visualizing the performance of your models is never a bad idea!).":"AUC（有时写成 AUROC）就是整条 ROC 曲线下面那块面积。可以想成微积分里的积分。它给分类器一个不依赖具体阈值的总分，模型之间甚至不用盯着 ROC 曲线也能比一比（当然，把表现画出来从来不是坏事）。","AUC ranges in value from 0 to 1, with higher numbers indicating better performance. A perfect classifier will have an AUC of 1, while a perfectly random classifier an AUC of 0.5. A model that always predicts that a negative sample is more likely to have a positive label than a positive sample will have AUC of 0, indicating severe failure on the modeling side. Scores in the range [0.5, 1] imply good performance, while anything under 0.5 indicates very poor performance.":"AUC 从 0 到 1，越大越好。完美分类器是 1，完全瞎猜是 0.5。要是模型总觉得负样本比正样本更像正类，AUC 就是 0，建模这边已经翻车了。[0.5, 1] 还算能看，低于 0.5 就很糟。","At 0.73, our model's AUC isn't too shabby:":"0.73，我这个模型的 AUC 还过得去：","Area Under The (ROC) Curve":"（ROC）曲线下面积","OUR AUC: 0.73":"我的 AUC：0.73","It may be surprising given how ad-hoc the metric appears, but AUC has a deeper-than-expected interpretation. Actually, multiple interpretations exist, but we prefer the probabilistic interpretation:":"这个指标看起来有点凑合，读法其实比想象中深。解释有好几种，我更喜欢概率上的那种：","The AUC is the probability that the model will rank a randomly chosen positive example more highly than a randomly chosen negative example.":"AUC 是：随机抽一个正例、一个负例，模型把正例排得更高的概率。","In other words, if you were to randomly select an observation belonging to the positive class and an observation belonging to the negative class, the AUC tells us the probability that the model will assign a higher score to the positive class. This interpretation helps qualify the AUC: A model that always predicts that a negative sample is more likely to have a positive label than a positive sample will have AUC of 0. If the predicted probabilities are random, it will be 0.5. Finally if the model always predicts a positive sample is more likely to have a positive label than a negative sample, then it will have an AUC of 1. This strategy also provides a very easy method to estimate the AUC: simply tally up the proportion of correctly ranked positive-negative pairs! And what's even cooler, it has been shown that this method of estimating the AUC is equivalent to a popular nonparametric statistical test: the Wilcoxon-Mann-Whitney test (see Mason and Graham in the resources to learn more).":"换句话说，随便拿一个正类观察和一个负类观察，AUC 告诉我：模型给正类打更高分的概率。这能帮我给 AUC 定个位置：模型总觉得负样本比正样本更像正类，AUC 就是 0；预测概率完全随机，就是 0.5；总能把正样本排在负样本前面，就是 1。这也给了一种很好估 AUC 的办法：把排对的正–负对比一下比例就行！更巧的是，这种估法等价于一个挺有名的非参数检验：Wilcoxon-Mann-Whitney 检验（想多看可以翻后面的参考）。","In modern machine learning, ROC and AUC are commonly used tools for evaluating the performance of a classifier. However, as with everything, they’re not perfect! In cases when you desire well-calibrated probability outputs (i.e. when you care about the true likelihood of an event, and not just model performance), ROC/AUC will give us no such information. Additionally, the AUC metric treats all classification errors equally, ignoring the potential consequences of ignoring one type over another. For example, in cancer detection, we’ll probably want to minimize false negatives (even at the risk of increasing false positives). That said, even in these cases plotting the ROC may help in understanding the potential strengths and weaknesses of your model, and where special care should be given.":"现在做机器学习，ROC 与 AUC 常被拿来评估分类器。可它们也不是万能的！要是我在乎校准过的概率（也就是事件真正有多可能发生，而不只是模型排得准不准），ROC / AUC 给不了这个信息。另外，AUC 把每类分类错误都当成一样重，不管漏掉哪一种代价更大。比如做癌症检测，我多半想尽量少漏诊（假阴性），哪怕假阳性会多一点。即便如此，把 ROC 画出来，仍能帮我看见模型哪边硬、哪边虚，哪里得格外小心。","Thanks for reading! We hope that the article is insightful no matter where you are along your Machine Learning journey, and that you came away with a better understanding of ROC and AUC in the context of machine learning.":"今天先写到这里。不管你学到哪一步，希望这篇对你有点用，也把机器学习里的 ROC 与 AUC 看明白了一点。","for his valuable contributions to the article.":"","What Makes A Good ROC Curve?":"怎样才算一条好看的 ROC 曲线？","The ROC Curve is valuable not only because it gives us an overview of our model's performance (and lets us select an optimal classification threshold for that model), but because it also gives us an easy visual to compare the performance of different classifiers to one another.":"ROC 曲线值钱，不只是因为它能让我一眼看到模型表现（并帮我挑一个合适的分类阈值），还因为它很好拿来对着比：不同分类器，谁更像样。","A perfect classifier is one that hugs along the outer-left and top of the chart. This is expected, as ‘perfect’ here implies the classifier will always have a TPR=1, regardless of the FPR. On the other hand, a diagonal line implies that TPR=FPR for every classification threshold - in other words, the classifier is just making random guesses. The model is garbage!":"完美分类器会贴着图的左外侧和顶边走。说得通：「完美」意味着不论 FPR 是多少，TPR 永远是 1。对角线上则是每个阈值都有 TPR=FPR——也就是在瞎猜。这模型可以扔了。","Perfect Classifier":"完美分类器","Random Classifier":"随机分类器","Our Classifier":"我的分类器","Comparing ROC Curves":"比较 ROC 曲线","When it comes to comparing models, the rule of thumb is that curves that fall above the ROC Curve of a random classifier (the diagonal line) are good or decent. The higher up they are (i.e. the closer they are to the curve of the elusive perfect classifier), the better. Anything below the diagonal line has worse performance than random guessing, so likely isn't worth any consideration.":"比模型时，经验是：落在随机分类器那条对角线上面的，还算能看。越高越好（也就是越靠近那个几乎摸不到的完美分类器）。对角线下面比瞎猜还差，多半不用再看。","Our model shows something that hangs somewhere between perfect and random. Indeed, this is the kind of result you’d expect to get in the real-world. Obtaining a perfect or exactly random result likely indicates a problem. An exactly random result may indicate that your problem is not well-framed, and may not have a solution without additional data. If the result shows a perfect classifier, make sure that there is no place where the training data is leaking information about the label which would not be available when the model is in deployment, as such a result likely points to overfitting or data-leakage.":"我这条线停在完美和随机之间。现实里多半就是这种结果。要是得到完美，或刚好随机，反而该怀疑出了问题。刚好随机，可能是问题没问对，没有更多数据就解不开。要是完美分类器，得检查训练数据有没有把部署时看不见的标签信息漏进来——这种结果常常指向过拟合，或数据泄漏。"};
